Munich 1856: Four maps that change your view of the city
An insane asylum, where young people are supposed to meet today.
A tomb chapel, which is now being dug for the S-Bahn tunnel.
Kleingärtner cultivates the former reason of farmers.
Munich's oldest official map brings exciting stories to light.
Annoyed when maps aren’t up-to-date.
Everyone knows this, who already had to get upset, because the car navi indicated a green meadow instead of a bypass road.
The historical maps of the digital Bayern-Atlase, an offer from the geoportal Bavaria of the state government, are anything but up-to-date - but that is precisely why it is very insightful.
Especially if you compare them with current online maps.
Then it becomes clear how cities and municipalities have changed since the 19th century in the distribution area of Munich-based Mercury.
The digital Bayern-Atlas is a patchword, which as such one does not immediately recognize.
More detailed maps, so-called primordial position sheets, were incorporated into the large overview map for cities and municipalities.
According to the Bavarian Surveying Administration, these emerged between 1810 and 1864 and originate from Bavaria’s first survey.
Her client: Napoleon.
The French army leader occupied Bavaria since the spring of 1800 during the Second Coalition War with Austria.
Napoleon wished for an overview of the occupied land and commissioned a topographical recording of Bavaria - for military purposes, of course.
Even if the French troops finally withdrew with the peace of Lunéville of 9 February 1801: it was today's neighbors who created the idea of a nationwide Bavaria map.
Elector Max IV. Joseph continued to advance the project, founded the "Topographische Bureau" - and thus the Bavarian Surveying Administration.
The Urpositionsblatt for Munich dates from 1856 and, like everyone else, can be found on the website of the Bavarian State Library.
The comparison earlier/today is most impressive using the example of the state capital.
The city has expanded in all directions and has grown together.
An example of this is Untersendling, which still appears relatively isolated in the map of 1856.
Sendling was incorporated 21 years after the creation of the map, i.e. 1877, as a Munich district.
Today Untersendling is located in the middle of the city.
The houses Untersendlings concentrate around 1856 along today's Plinganserstraße, which was only named after Georg Sebastian Plinganser in 1878, a leader of the uprising of Bavarian peasants against Austria 1705.
Where the West Park is today, you can see nothing but fields - at first glance: When zooming in, a more small-part map with dozens of lines and numbers appears: handwritten numbered parcels from the so-called "primordial recording", from which the property tax was derived.
Today, small parcels no longer occupy farmers there - but allotment gardeners of the "Land in Sonne" plant.
A large greed area looms north of Untersendling.
The Theresienwiese, the scene of the Oktoberfest since 1810, was actually still a meadow in the 19th century - and no concrete desert.
On the other side of the Isar, the historical map gives an insight into the history of psychiatric medicine.
In the Urpositionsblatt, in the area of today's Au-Haidhausen district, one reads not only the street name "am Gasteig" or of the hill "Auf der Lüften" - but also in large letters "Irren Anstalt".
Yes, here on Auerfeldstraße, people with delusions were once imprisoned.
In 1858/59, there was there the Munich Kreis-Irrenanstalt "Auf der Auer Lüften".
From 1872, Dr. Bernhard von Gudden headed the institution - the psychiatrist who is brought into connection with the death of King Ludwig II.
"He was progressive for his time and, for example, ended the chaining of the so-called lunatics.
In addition, he has forbidden the staff to use violence against the inmates," says Hermann Wilhelm, head of the Haidhausen Museum, who dealt with the institution for an exhibition.
There had initially been room for 280 patients, later expanded to 500 places.
At some time, even this capacity was no longer sufficient - the reason for the construction of the Kreisirrenanstalt in Haar 1905.
Since 2006, the Isar-Amper-Klinikum München-Ost, one of the largest psychiatrists in Germany, has been located there.
On the area of the former Auer Anstalt, teenagers now meet in the Munich Salesianum.
The order acquired the building complex in 1920 and converted it into a youth home with training companies.
To the west is the football field of the TSV Munich Ost, east residential buildings from the 1980s.
In 2015, the children's house "Casa Don Bosco" was built - in architectural reference to the original construction of the insane asylum, which had been destroyed in bombing raids in the Second World War.
Marienplatz was listed around 1856 as "Haupt Platz", the Kaufingerstraße was called "Kaufinger Gasse".
A decisive change brought the Second World War also in the heart of Munich.
Allied bombs tore many holes in the city centre in December 1944.
A large one remained to this day - at the Marienhof.
The English Institute, small commercial buildings and a tomb chapel from the Middle Ages once stood at the Marienhof, where these days the preliminary work takes place and on April 5, 2017 the groundbreaking ceremony for the second S-Bahn main line.
With the slider, the old Munich map can be compared with the aerial photo of today - and replenishe the hole at Marienhof.
Civil rights group releases travel warning for Missouri
The National Association for the Promotion of Colored Persons has issued a warning for people of colour who travel to Missouri due to discriminatory policies and the state’s racist attacks.
"NAACP Travel Advisory for the state of Missouri, which is scheduled to enter into force by August 28, 2017 requires African-American travelers, visitors and Missourians to pay special attention and exercise extreme caution when traveling through the state, as a number of questionable, race-based incidents occur lately across the state and are noted here," the group's statement said.
A recently passed Missouri law that makes it harder for people to win discrimination lawsuits, as well as the state's law law enforcement agencies aimed disproportionately at minorities prompted the group to issue the travel warning, the NAACP said.
"They have violations of civil rights that happen to people.
They're stopped because of their skin color, they're beaten up or skewered," the president of the Missouri NAACP, Rod Chapel, told the Kansas City Star.
"We're hearing complaints in such frequency that we haven't heard yet."
It is the first such warning the organization has issued for a state in the U.S.
The group cited incidents such as racist offences against black students at the University of Missouri and the deaths of Tory Sanders, 28, a black man from Tennessee.
Sanders died under questionable circumstances earlier this year, having run out of gasoline while traveling through the state and being taken into custody by Missouri police without being accused of a crime.
The council also points to a recent report by the Missouri Attorney General's office showing that black motorists in the state are 75 percent more likely than whites.
"The recommendation is that people are aware and informing their families, friends and co-workers about what could happen in Missouri," Chapel said.
"People need to be willing whether to bring bail money or let relatives know they're traveling through the state."
Missouri recorded 100 hate crimes in 2015, according to the latest figures from the FBI's hate crime reporting program, which rank the state to 16th place in terms of the number of such violations.
The travel warning is also a response to a new Missouri law that would make it harder to sue a company for housing or employment discrimination.
Earlier, the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) had issued travel warnings for Texas and Arizona after states passed immigration laws requiring local law enforcement agencies to arrest people for immigration violations, which the ACLU believes will contribute to further racial profiling.
Travel warnings are usually issued by the State Department for other countries, but lately stakeholders have deployed the measure in response to certain laws and trends within the U.S
Pasture fence project is elementary
The Fischerbacher Weidezaun project is a successful project and will continue next year.
The CDU members of the Bundestag Peter Weiß and Kordula Kovac got a picture on site.
Representatives of the Fischerbacher CDU local association together with mayor Thomas Schneider and the two deputies met at the Rechtgrabenhof of the Schwendemann family.
Markus Schwendemann initially provided insights into his company, in which he cultivates 55 hectares of meadows and 40 hectares of forest, supplies 40 dairy cows and just as many juveniles and houses holiday guests in five apartments.
The "Schwarzwaldmilch" is for him the alternativeless marketing route for the milk of his cows, according to Schwendemann.
On the table of the holiday guests comes exclusively regional.
Since he is also BLHV chairman of Fischerbach, he described the pasture fence project as "elementarily important."
I can't operate profitably on such steep areas.
The grassland area provides a lot of work, a lot has to be processed with the scythton.
"We are struggling here with the conditions and with topography," Schwendemann stressed.
In 2003, he took over the parental company and cultivated it without chemical plant protection and fertilizer, even if he did not run an organic farm.
For Mayor Thomas Schneider, it was clear: "The municipality has recognized the importance of the pasture fence project and is happy about the leader funding".
At a cost of 3000 to 4000 euros per hectare, the farmers would have to be supported.
It is hoped that the reforestation of the areas could be pulled out for quite a while.
"The greatest treasure we have is the people who work in agriculture and are thinking about how they take their operations further," the mayor stressed.
Farmers are often under criticism because of funding
A total of 25 parcels from eleven participants were fenced in 2013 via pasture fence funding.
The opening area is thus 40 hectares, which may not be reforested until at least 2023.
Of the total costs of 71 500 euros, half was funded via Leader, a quarter contributed by the municipality of Fischerbach.
Thus, the farmers remained 12 610 euros, which they had to invest in 16.2 kilometers of pasture fence.
"The great advantage is the longevity of the fences," Schwendemann explained.
Kordula Kovac emphasized: "You are doing a really good job, without farmers and rural women it is not possible".
Peter Weiss also said: "The best food is produced here."
The Baden-Württemberg Landscape Conservation Directive (LPR) enables the state funding of pasture fences.
The aim is to keep the Black Forest open.
Prerequisite is the presence of a conveying backdrop designated by LPR.
In addition, the area to be fenced must not be traversable (tendency above 35 percent) and must be threatened by reforestation.
For the Fischerbacher Weidezaun project 2018, the application is currently underway by eight participants with 24 parcels who want to build almost 16 kilometers of fences.
The opening area corresponds to 35 hectares, the calculated costs are more than 112 000 euros.
Isn't anything sacred anymore?
Now Trump's White House takes the Statue of Liberty
The safest sign of a regime change is when they start attacking the statues.
The Americans appreciate these as well as anyone - hence the carefully staged overthrow of Saddam Hussein on Firdos Square in Baghdad in 2003.
Stephen Miller, one of the key ideologues of the Trump regime, certainly knew what he was doing when he took a symbolic axe in a heated dispute with CNN's Jim Acosta over the president's proposals to drastically restrict legal immigration to the Statue of Liberty.
To stay fair, Miller didn't attack the statue himself.
A horde of boat owners and Liberty impersonators would have lynched him if he had done it.
But he attacked their importance, and in particular the meaning attributed to it, when Emma Lazarus' famous poem was added to its base in 1903, 17 years after the completion of the monument.
When Miller Acosta insulted: "I don't want to dwell on a complete thing about history here, but the Statue of Liberty is ... a symbol of American freedom that enlightens the world.
The poem to which you refer, which was later added and is not part of the original Statue of Liberty.”
Miller is objectively right, but as he put it himself, it's not really about history.
It's about the contemporary resonance on the stirring words of Lazarus, the only ones in which a state invites not only some old immigrants, but the poorest of the poor: "Your tires, your arms,/your afflicted masses who yearn to breathe freely,/the miserable apostasy of your rich shores."
There has never been a time when the "wretched waste" on our screens has been better seen.
Miller wasn't engaged in literary criticism - he made it clear that these people aren't welcome in Trump's U.S.
This conflict because of the importance of a statue is part of a broader political and cultural contention: it is indeed a conflict over America's importance.
Lazarus - and her friends who campaigned to place her words at the grassroots after her death - knew very well that they were carrying out a highly political act.
Sculpture was intended to show the connections between French and American republicanism by portraying the female embodiment of freedom in a worn-and-worn classical pictorial representation.
Lazarus changed this meaning: In her poem, the female figure is no longer abstract - she has received a voice.
And she gives herself a completely different name: mother of exiles.
The poem's connection with the image is a brilliant feminist coup and a devastating attack on American nativism.
And just as Lazarus changed the meaning of the statue, the people from the right wing wanted to change it back for a long time.
The great screaming neck Rush Limbauugh, a kind of St. John the Anabaptist for Trump's coming, argued in 2010 that the Statue of Liberty doesn't invite anyone, but is rather an early neoconservative that brings the American flame into the naughty world: "Lady Liberty is moving forward.
She is said to carry the torch of freedom from the United States to the rest of the world.
“The torch shouldn’t illuminate the path to the United States.”
Quite uncommon Limbaugh has come across something here.
The significance of the statue is entirely a matter of the point of view of perception.
Lazarus's great imaginative deed consisted in seeing them as seen by the exhausted, miserable but hopeful people after long and often horrific journeys on the decks of ships.
It's literally about seeing the world from the perspective of people seeking refuge and a chance to improve their lives.
Her statue speaks to them the words of hope and welcome they must hear.
And, of course, that's not the perspective that Miller and Trump ever want to acknowledge.
Their torch is not meant to illuminate the way, but ignite the hatred that is their only source of power.
World Cup 2017: Neil Black praises Scottish members of Britain's team
Eilidh Doyle recalls a time when only two Scots belonged to Britain's team
UK athletics athletics director Neil Black says the 16-strong Scottish contingent in the world championship squad "will make a massive contribution to the team."
A record number of Scottish athletes have been selected for London 2017, which kicks off on Friday.
Black believes that "something special is developing in Scotland" and the UK's athletics needs to learn something out of it.
"We're welcoming it, and we're trying to understand, and we're going to carry it on until we figure it out," Black said.
"I'm talking to people at the Scottish Institute of Sport and they're thinking it has something to do with what they've done.
Scottish athletes think it has something to do with what they've done.
British athletics: It has something to do with what they've done.
And also with the guy on the street corner.
Whatever the combination of circumstances, it's absolutely brilliant.'
Several of the Scottish athletes are medal contenders, including Laura Muir and Andrew Butchart - who will compete against Sir Mo Farah on the 5,000 m track, while Eilidh Doyle has been chosen by the squad as team captain.
"Eilidh is quite modest.
Her captain's speech has been incredible," Black says.
"A number of athletes, whether they were Scottish or wherever they live or train, walked out of the room and felt very proud and were very motivated.
The passion and the real feeling Eilidh naturally brought in was great.”
The Commonwealth silver medalist, who will compete at her fifth world championship at the 400m hurdles, says it's "incredible" that 16 Scots are in the British squad.
Scotland's best total number of athletes so far at the World Cup was seven achieved in 1983 and 2015.
Medium-distance runner Muir leads along the way, setting five British and two European records last year.
She will take part in the 1,500 m and the 5,000 m runs.
Chris O'Hare, who runs 1,500 m, is another Scot you're supposed to watch after a great season that included victory at the British Team Trials and Anniversary Games.
Doyle remembers her first world championships, when the only other Scot was in Team Lee McConnell.
The team captain says she is "enormously proud" of the fact that many of her compatriots will not only belong to the team, but in some cases will also be involved in the medals and at the finals.
"What's special about being a team captain is because she's been elected by the other members of the team, so it's been a great honour anyway, but knowing that my teammates voted for me and elected me makes it very special for me," she added.
"Obviously I'm very proud of all the Scots who belong to the team.
It just shows how far we've come.
And they're here as established athletes, athletes who want to get on podiums.
As for my speech as captain?
Yes, I think she went well.
I've just pointed out that we're very happy in this situation as we have a home World Cup.
So it's really important to rely on this home support and benefit that we're back in the Olympic Stadium.
I'm getting a second chance to go out there and compete and it'll just be such a great atmosphere out there.'
Block critics via Facebook: Don't apply for a public office if you can't handle the heating up of voters
The father of our country knew something of bad press.
Americans loved George Washington, but it didn't take long for the newspapers to beat him because of everything from domestic politics to his political principles.
He scolded the criticism, for sure.
But he didn't silence his critics.
In 1783, Washington said: "Freedom of speech can be taken away - and, dumb and dumb, we are led to slaughter like sheep."
That takes me to Maryland to Gov. Larry Hogan, who has to work on being more like Washington.
Hogan's employees have blocked and deleted the posts of at least 450 people who expressed their opinions on his official Facebook page.
And the American Civil Liberties Union sued him earlier this week.
The governor's staff dismissed the lawsuit as frivolous, and the online commentary was full of a backlast with "Well, and?" .
"It's just Facebook," many people said.
But it's important.
And it's especially important if it's a guy like Hogan.
This is a republican governor in an overwhelmingly Democratic state that is amazingly popular.
He has the second-highest approval from the nation's 50 governors.
Hogan's not a reactionary hothead.
He has shown a firm hand in the leadership of his state and strict adherence to the
He was also very keen to use Facebook as a primary means to connect with his constituents and playfully debut his hairless head after chemotherapy on his side.
Therefore, the blocking of individuals who come to the side of the governor - who is a public forum that is dubbed official and by employees paid with public tax dollars - is unnecessary and ultimately dangerous.
In an interview with The Washington Post, Hogan's spokeswoman Amelia Chasse defended the governor's actions, arguing that blocking the comments was nothing more than moderation.
But it's too easy to use the image of trolls or spammers or hateful people swinging the whip online.
The post spoke to some of the real people who were blocked by Hogan.
And these are only real people who talk to their elected leaders: a teacher, an entrepreneur and a pastor, not a troll.
They all said their comments were respectful, thoughtful and not profane.
The pastor cited the Bible in his post and appealed to Hogan's Catholic faith.
Lakshmi Sarma Ramani of Bowie, Md., was not hateful, but she asked for hate crimes.
"I politely commented that I was disappointed that there was no reaction to hate crimes and other recent news," she wrote in the news sensation's comments section, "The Post."
“I don’t appreciate this idea either, even if a number of people comment on the same issue, they are disregarded by some rather as a so-called collective deployment and not recognized as a large group of affected citizens.”
What the governor's staff called a "concentrated spam attack," others would probably call "advocacy."
The Facebook era makes it easy to tailor a message by simply blocking a critic or deleting a negative comment.
It's much cleaner than in earlier times if you'd done the same thing that employees were dispatched to collect and burn newspapers with critical editorials or arresting and silencing protesters.
But that’s what’s happening digitally.
Hogan is not the first public official to be criticized for berating Facebook and other social media.
President Trump is sued by Twitter users who have been blocked by his Twitter feed.
One of the first milestones on this issue fell last week in Virginia.
Loudoun County's board of supervisor chairman injured the First Amendment, according to U.S. District Judge James C. Cacheris in Alexandria, when she banned a voter from her Facebook page.
And in Kentucky, Governor Matt Bevin (R) also received a visit from the ACLU for its use of Facebook and Twitter.
That shouldn't be so hard.
At the time of Washington, the period of affordable postage had a similar effect as the Internet.
The number of newspapers multiplied between 1776 and 1800, and anonymous letter writers embarked on his leadership.
And even then, Washington had anonymous trolls.
The people who used pseudonyms such as "Juricola," "Valerius," "Belisarius" and "Portius" wrote all the letters to newspapers that put down Washington's decisions.
Petitions criticizing his point of view on Amity's contract, commerce and shipping with Britain overwhelmed his office, according to the historical documents collected by the George Washington Project's online papers.
But he didn't silence her.
Freedom of speech, disagreement and discourse are part of the foundation of our nation.
And true leadership means accepting that.
Ronan Rafferty hopes for domestic comfort at Renaissance club
Former European No. 1 Ronan Rafferty will be hoping for domestic comfort over the next three days as the Renaissance Club in East Lothian hosts the 25th edition of the Scottish Senior Open.
The Northern Irishman, who led next door next door at Archerfield Link until the final round before losing to Paul Eales, is affiliated with the new venue and acts as tournament ambassador on behalf of club founder and CEO Jerry Sarvadi.
"I've seened the Renaissance club being built, and we're seeing him achieving his fame with this event," Rafferty said.
"The players will see this course at its best, he's in great condition.
Jerry did a great job when he fixed this place.
He's an zealous advocate of this game and is proud of his performance here.
This is a great showcase for this course.”
The Englishman Eales is looking forward to defending a championship for the first time, even though he has won in his 32-year career at both the European Tour and the European Challenge Tour.
"That feels really special," the Southport-based player said.
"I couldn't defend the Extremadura Open as it was taken out of the program in the year after I won it.
Getting back to this part of the world is magical.
It's a wonderful place to play golf.
The memories from last year are coming back, and it was something special for me and my wife Sharon to win here last year.
It’s been a really special time and one we’re going to appreciate.”
Gary Orr, who came to the European Senior Tour earlier this year after his 50th birthday earlier this year, returns to action on Heimatboden.
This event will be his first Scottish appearance since the Aberdeen Asset Management Scottish Open in 2013.
"It always means a little bit more when you're playing at home," said Orr, the man from Helensburgh.
"You want to do well, but you're also feeling that extra pressure.
I've really enjoyed playing again and I've been playing solid golf.
“I’m happy with how it’s gone.”
With the trio on the field, former Masters winner Ian Woosnam and senior major champion Paul Broadhurst, Roger Chapman, Mark James and Mark McNulty.
Orr and former Ryder Cup captain Sam Torrance are among the six Scots on the field, the others being Andrew Oldcorn, Stephen McAllister, Bill Longmuir and Ross Drummond.
Admission is free, parking costs £5 per car with an occupant and £10 per car with two or more occupants.
All proceeds will go to Prostate Cancer UK.
Fewer lung cancer deaths caused by passive smoking
In the past two decades, fewer and fewer people have died of lung cancer because of second-handed smoking.
Scientists at the Universitätsklinikum Hamburg-Eppendorf (UKE) come to this conclusion in a study.
"According to our estimates, 167 pulmonary cancer deaths per year are due to passive smoking," said study leader Heiko Becher.
As a comparison, he used his own study on this topic in 1994.
At that time, there were 400 lung cancer deaths due to passive smoking per year in Germany.
The new study is published in the International Journal of Public Health.
House connections turn out to be very complex
The construction work in Villinger Straße is in full swing and "is somewhat in time," according to the head of the construction yard Fred Heinze.
In the area of house connections, the situation is more complicated than suspected, since there is a lot hiding underground, which is not noted in the plans.
The traffic, which repeatedly hinders the construction work, poses a major problem for the construction squad of the company Stumpp.
"The ignorance of motorists against red traffic lights and shut-off grids is already enormous," Heinze reports.
The road is closed and only the scheduled buses are entitled to travel.
Angelo Caruso, Marvin Kostorz and Tim Kosorz from Stumpp have been working on the bridge for weeks.
"The hot days have been quite exhausting.
But fortunately we were supplied with cool mineral water by our friend Fritz Matt," says Marvin Kostorz.
Poverty and Death in Indonesian Goldland
If Bardina Degei cooks dinner, she doesn't use a stove.
She rarely uses a pot.
In her wooden house in Enarotali, the capital of the Paniai reign in the troubled Indonesian province of Papua, the housewife usually puts a sweet potato - locally referred to as "nota" - right into the fireplace.
After half an hour, the charred tuber is removed and devoured with zealous, unwashed hands.
Degei sits on the clay floor - she has no furniture - where she also carries out her daily work, such as washing dresses with murky water from the nearby swamp.
A bucket in a noiseless room serves as a latrine.
As the youngest of her husband's four wives, no fields have been assigned to her.
Polygamy is common here.
Of course, working late can be dangerous: most villagers are unemployed, and many are drinking hard, and there are soldiers here.
"Nobody dares to walk around the village after 5 p.m.," she says.
It's a rare glimpse of daily life in the highlands of Papua, a former Dutch colony recorded in Indonesia in 19969 after a disputed referendum, when only 1,026 elders were supposed to vote by a public hand-survey in front of the occupying forces.
An existing movement that stirred for independence against Dutch rule quickly turned against the government of Jakarta, which maintains control of the region and excludes foreign journalists or legal observers.
In 2003, the province was officially divided into Papua and West Papua, with the independent part of Papua-Neuguinea encompassing the eastern part of the island.
Enarotali is as remote as it is dull. The trip includes a 90-minute flight from the provincial capital Jayapura to Nabire and then a morning 5-hour drive by rental car.
There's no public transport.
The town with about 19,000 inhabitants consists of wooden houses surrounded by bamboo fences, and corrugated metal roofs, which have been converted by rust into various yellow-brown tones.
Very few Indonesians have made their journey here, let alone journalists and virtually no foreigners.
Before Christian missionaries arrived, Mee Pago Papuans worshipped a god named Uga Tamee.
There have been other changes as well.
"We weren't used to wearing these clothes," Degei says, pointing to her lively, hand-woven turban, her dark shirt and bright skirt.
“We used to have only leaves on our bodies.”
Papua is Indonesia's poorest province, where 28% of people live below the poverty line and have some of the worst infant mortality and literacy rates in Asia.
But it's Indonesia's golland, too.
The world's largest and most profitable gold mine, Grasberg, owned by Freeport McMoran from Phoenix, is located just 60 miles from Paniai, a highland province the size of New Jersey with 153,000 inhabitants.
In 2015 alone, Freeport mined US$3.1 billion in gold and copper.
In addition, Papua has wood resources worth an estimated US$78 billion.
These riches, however, are a source of misery for the Papuas, which ensure that Indonesia's powerful military maintains a suffocating presence.
A 2005 New York Times investigation reported Freeport paid nearly US$20 million to local military personnel and units between 1998 and 2004, including up to US$150,000 to a single officer.
Papuanian demands for greater autonomy threaten these golden gooses and are mercilessely treated.
According to activists, more than 500,000 Papuas have been killed and thousands have been raped, tortured and imprisoned by the Indonesian military since 1969.
Mass killings in Papua's tribal highlands in the 1970's became genocide, according to the Asia Human Rights Commission.
In the region alone, Indonesian police arrested more than 3,900 peaceful protesters last year.
"We'll Lose Everything," according to a 2016 report by the Archdiocese of Brisbane containing reports of atrocities from the previous year, such as unlawful executions and torture - rape and electrical strikes are particularly popular, according to another report - and the brutal crackdown on peaceful demonstrations.
"It's difficult to count the number of casualties as incidents happen every week," says Andreas Harsono, Indonesia researcher for Human Rights Watch.
The screws have intensified as Papua's resources provide an influx of settlers from other parts of Indonesia.
The population of 3.5 million inhabitants of the province is 83% Christian, but the demographic development is changing as Muslim economic migrants come from the populous islands of Java, Borneo, Sumatra and Sulawesi.
Javanese Warung cantines sell fried chicken and gado gado, a mixed vegetable with peanut sauce served.
Local people struggle to keep up with here.
"Migrants have started selling chickens and vegetables at the traditional market cheaper than the local Papuas," explains Abeth You, a 24-year-old Paniai native who moved to the provincial capital Jayapura for work.
"It has cost the native Papua - the mama-mom [the women] of Papua - their market."
Indonesia's President Joko Widodo, popularly known as Jokowi, promised to address the inequalities and violations in Papua during his 2014 election campaign.
The former carpenter secured 27 of Papua's total of 29 districts - including Paniai - on his way to the presidential palace in Jakarta.
But little has changed in Papua, and today the locals feel betrayed.
"Our hearts are broken because we voted for Jokowi in 2014 with the expectation that he will fulfill our hopes that justice will be restored," You says.
In fact, Paniai suffered a low point just two months after Jokowi's inauguration in October.
On December 7, 2014, a group of 11 children were outside singing Christmas carols in front of a bonfire in Enarotali, when two Indonesian soldiers broke through the darkness on a motorcycle.
Terrified children told them to turn on their headlights.
One of the soldiers took offense to their tone and later came back with four soldiers, according to local pastor Yavedt Tebai.
The soldiers who had been drunk chasing and beating the group with their rifle buttocks said victims and witnesses.
Then one of the soldiers fired into the children's group.
One child, 16-year-old Yulianus Yeimo, was beaten so badly that he fell into a coma.
A few hours later, the building of the nearby government commission was set on fire and things escalated the next day.
Approximately 1,000 young Papua men, women and children gathered on a football pitch outside the local police station and the military squad centre to demand justice.
They wore ceremonial hunting bows and performed the Waita dance - running in a circle and imitating a vogel singing - of Papua's Mee Pago tribe.
Some protesters began hurling stones at police and military posts.
When the mood was heated up, the soldiers were sent an order via an internal radio: "If the crowds resist more than three times, they shoot them dead," according to an official TIME document that has not been released to local media.
Yeremias Kayame, 56, the leader of Enarotali's Kego Koto district, saw the looming danger and called for calm and urged the crowd to return home.
No one was in the mood of listening.
"When I turned around, I was suddenly shot into my left wrist," he told TIME on the porch of his colorfully painted wooden house.
Kayame still doesn't know who fired, but says the bullet came from the ranks of soldiers gathered here.
"It was overcrowded, a lot of shots were fired," he adds.
Local man Alfius Youw has been hit three times, according to his cousin who witnessed the shootings.
"I ran to him and examined his body to make sure it was him," Yohanes, who, like many Indonesians, bears only one name, told TIME in a gloomy mood.
"I saw that he was dead ... I kissed him."
Papua police chief inspector Yotje Mende told reporters that his officers only wanted to "secure" their station because it was undertaking.
"We have to defend ourselves when people threaten to kill us," Papua police spokesman Commissioner Pudjo Sulistiyo said in 2015.
"It's about life and death."
According to Human Rights Watch, five young protesters were killed and many more injured.
News about the killings didn't come to Jakarta 'filtered' until the following day.
For three weeks, Jokowi gave an impassioned speech in Jayapura expressing sympathy for the families of the victims and vowing to address the historical grievances in Papua.
"I want this case to be solved immediately, so it won't happen again in the future," he said.
Security Minister Wiranto said in October 2016 that he established an extrajudicial mechanism for resolving historic human rights violations.
But the excuses began almost immediately.
"Most injuries occurred a long time ago.
Some were in the 90's and early 2000's.
The point is that we're committed to addressing these violations, but there are processes that need to go through," he said.
Then Wiranto made a withdrawal when he spoke to TIME in Jakarta on June 5 and said he had no plans to set up a complaint mechanism in Papua.
Instead, "everything is regulated by law," he said.
Wiranto, whom the U.N. has indicted of "crimes against humanity," claiming more than 1,000 fatalities in Osttimor's bloody 1999 independence vote, said 11 cases of human rights violations in Papua had already been settled, including the Paniai incident.
Families of Paniai victims accepted such claims with grim incredulity.
"I've been interviewed four times in the last three years, but there has been no progress whatsoever," Yohanes says.
“I’m tired.”
He says he's still living in fear years later.
"I'm scared," he says.
"I'm afraid of being arrested by the military, I'm afraid of being shot."
His brother Yacobus reiterated the view that the people of Paniai are terrified to discuss the incident.
He says he was beaten by the military after he helped buried four of the victims.
"Having I buried the bodies, the military searched for me," he says.
The shootings didn't stop there.
On Tuesday, Indonesian police shot at villagers in Paniai's neighborhood Deiyai.
During a confrontation between villagers and the manager of a construction company that refused to take an unconscious man to the hospital, one person died and 17 others were injured, including children.
The man, 24-year-old Ravianus Douw, who drowned while fishing in a nearby river, died on his way to the hospital.
Exciting villagers protested outside the company's site office.
Police said the villagers threw stones at officers who responded by firing warning shots.
But locals say the mobile brigade (Indonesian paramilitary police) began shooting at the crowd, killing one.
"We've been so panicked, we're terrified that there's going to be revenge," says 29-year-old Dominggu Badii, who lives near the hospital and witnessed how the injured were quickly admitted here, according to TIME.
"I've been hiding in my house for two days."
The Deiyai Parliament has called for the officers involved to be held accountable and to withdraw police mobile brigade from the area.
Paniai has always been a turmoil for the Indonesian government.
The lack of meaningful development strengthens the dissatisfactionment of Tribal members Mee, Moni, Dani and Damal, who live in Papua's green highlands.
Many joined the "Free Papua Movement" (OPM), the rebel army claiming to defend Papua's rights by launching sporadic attacks and kidnappings of Indonesian soldiers.
Some of the top OPM leaders come from Paniai, including Tadius Yogi and Daniel Yudas Kogoya.
In response, thousands of people have been arrested and arbitrarily taken into custody in Paniai in recent years, under the pretext of "securing national sovereignty."
Some will never reappear again.
Among the people of Papua, Paniai is known as "a tragic, forgotten place."
Poverty strengthens dissatisfaction.
The bit of rice sold in Enarotali is too expensive for locals.
Bread is also out of reach.
The people here grow everything they eat: especially notes and fruit and leafy vegetables.
Agriculture is the task of the women, who can each work four or five fields of sweet potatoes.
They keep the bulk of the harvest for the family, while the rest is sold in the local market.
Ten pieces of Nota cost only 10,000 Indonesian rupees (75 cents).
Over time, economic inequalities have grown between native Papuas and the new migrants who have arrived in greater numbers since the opening of new flight routes to Nabire airport.
The few jobs that exist in the region usually go to the better educated and wealthier migrants.
Papuas rarely have the capital or skills necessary to run their own businesses competitively.
"The young people are not interested in staying in the village ... because there are no jobs or money here," says John Gobai, the chairman of Paniai's tribal council.
Isolation keeps the world's eyes away from Papua.
In addition, restrictions on reporting remain tense to international media.
Earlier this year, French journalists Franck Escudie and Basille Longchamp were deported because of a lack of coordination with related institutions from Papua, even though they were given rare permission to film.
According to Phelim Kine, Human Rights Watch's deputy Asia director, Jokowi's election campaign to lift restrictions on reporting to promote transparency and development have not been realized.
"There are new dangers for foreign journalists who are trying to try to report from Indonesia's restless easternmost provinces of Papua and West Papua: visa denial and a blacklist," he said in a statement.
Lack of press control means that international pressure on the Indonesian government is largely limited to Papua's immediate neighbours.
In March, six Pacific states - Tonga, Nauru, Palau, Tuvalu, the Marshall Islands and the Solomon Islands - urged the U.N. Human Rights Council to investigate the "different and widespread violations" in Papua, including the Paniai shooting.
These same countries have historically supported the OPM.
Donone's Foreign Ministry spokesman Arrmanatha Nasir dismissed the group's accusations and told journalists in Jakarta: "A democratic system still applies in Indonesia, and there are free media, so it's difficult to cover up the evidence of human rights cases."
The people on the ground want more foreign governments to take note of this.
When an official delegation from the Netherlands, leaded by human rights embassy Kees Van Baar, visited Jayapura on May 4, locals broke their silence and harassed him "We want freedom," according to a source who also attended the meeting but would like to remain anonymous.
Indonesia has another presidential election in 2019, but the Papuas say they're unlikely to vote for Jokowi again.
"Jokowi is a person who has good intentions, but he's surrounded by the people involved in the Paniai shots," says Gobai, the chairman of the tribal council.
He wants Jokowi to know that the Paniai people, apart from living under the constant threat of a predatory military, are in poor parenting and health services.
Gobai says the Paniai people, like other Papuas, regard their vote for Jokowi as a "guilt" he has to repay.
"They don't need money, they just want justice," he says.
Despite the threats and intimidations, families of the Paniai gunshot victims carried out a final symbolic act of defiance: a victim's body was buried on a site exactly opposite the police and the military station.
They know that they may never receive justice, but at least they don't let those responsible forget their crimes.
"A member of our family was killed," Yacobus says with his head lowered.
"What else can we do?"
Fire captures Dubai 'torch' skyscraper for the second time
A fire found its book in one of the world's tallest residential towers in Dubai on Friday, forcing hundreds of residents to flee the 337-metre-high, 79-storey building.
It is the second blaze that sweeps through the high-rise in just two years, which happens to be called the Torch Tower.
The building was evacuated, no injuries were reported, and there was no direct word about the cause of the flame.
"We've been sleeping and we've stood up at the fire alarm and screaming people.
We ran down the stairs, and it took about 10 minutes to get down from the 50th floor," recounted a resident who gave his name as George, Reuters.
Dubai's civil defence authorities said firefighting units extinguished the fire around 4.00 a.m. local time (00.00 a.m. GMT) and cooled the building.
The government said it was working on providing accommodation for those affected.
The incident can revive questions about the safety of materials used in outdoor areas of high-rise buildings around the world.
An investigation by the management of the Torch building after its fire in 2015 revealed that most of the damage came from the cladding, the exterior cladding for decoration or insulation.
In Britain, the police said they believed that the system of plates for insulation and cladding at London's Grenfell tower in June may have contributed to the rapid spread of a blaze in which about 80 people died.
No Davis Cup reform: Continue best-of-five
The president of the International Tennis Federation (ITF) has failed with his major Davis Cup coup at the General Assembly.
At the meeting in Ho Chi Minh City, the revolutionary proposal for this sport to play Davis Cup individual only on two sets of wins was wafer-thin not supported by the required two-thirds majority.
The five-sentence classics, which are often very long, but also highly valued for this reason, are thus preserved in the traditional competition.
63.54 percent of the member countries voted in favour of the reform, the goal of ITF President David Haggerty was therefore only narrowly missed.
"We respect this decision, but are disappointed that our member nations have not accepted the entire reform package for the Davis Cup and Fed Cup.
We need a change to secure the long-term future of these iconic and historic competitions," Haggerty said.
We will continue to work on improving the competitions.
After many objections, it had not even come up for a vote that the finale should be held in a neutral place.
New from 2018 are only comparatively minor details: For example, the finalists in the Davis Cup and Fed Cup will have the host right in the first round the following year.
In order to reduce the cost for the organizers, the requirements in terms of training grounds, etc. will be eased and the players will have to complete less official media appointments before the Davis Cup.
More than 1 in 5 U.S. travelers put prohibited items on the plane, a survey finds
A survey of air passengers found that more than 20 per cent deliberately or subconsciously smuggled prohibited items past U.S. Transportation Security Administration checkpoints, including at least 6 per cent who entered the plane while accidentally woring prohibited knives or other items with blades.
Less than 1 percent claimed that they later discovered that they had traveled erroneously with firearms, ammunition or explosives.
The survey of more than 1,000 people - conducted by a jet charter service, Strato's Jet Charters Inc. - also noted that younger travelers are more likely to disregard the rules.
Of the respondents who admitted that they knowingly tried to fly with something banned by the TSA, 19.7 percent were millennials, compared with less than 15 percent who were members of Generation X.
The most common items that were intentionally passed by TSA agents were food and liquids.
More than 3 percent admitted knowingly carrying items with blades through the security checkpoint, while 2.2 percent of female respondents and 3.7 percent of male respondents also confirmed that they had deliberately put banned drugs on the plane.
The company said it surveyed travelers across the country to figure out how many had accidentally brought smuggled goods through airport security and how many had done it deliberately.
It's no surprise that the people most likely to carry contraband - knowingly or unwittingly - through safety have been those who fly most often.
However, the company points out that the TSA guidelines are somewhat complicated and confusing.
People are often unsure which liquids they are allowed to take with them or how much of them.
So it's no surprise that the largest amount of items seized by the TSA are prohibited liquids.
For a while, people thought the TSA might see books as contraband.
Liquids are allowed on domestical flights, but not on international flights.
Elon Musk doesn't care: Tens of thousands of Tesla customers cancel order
Electric thousand sasade Elon Musk likes to indicate with the huge number of pre-orders for his new Tesla "Model 3".
So far, he has embezzled that a part of the customers has already withdrawn their non-binding reservation again.
In addition to the always triumphant figures on increases in sales and even more gigantic sales targets, Tesla CEO Elon Musk betrayed for the first time a different, less glamorous figure in the announcement of the latest quarterly balance sheet: that of the cancellations on the new mass market model 3.
Musk had frequently advertised with the more than 500,000 pre-orders for the new car in the past few months, the delivery of which is just beginning.
Now Musk concedes: Of the total 518,000 pre-orders, 63,000 have already been withdrawn again.
Critics had repeatedly pointed out that these are only non-binding reservations.
In case of a cancer, Tesla will pay back the 1000 euros (in the USA 1000 US dollars and 1000 pounds in the UK) "reservation deposit" in full.
There are currently 455,000 such non-binding reservations, Musk admitted in a conference call with analysts.
However, the Tesla CEO does not believe that the cancellation of around twelve percent of all orders so far could become a problem.
His argument: the challenge with the ambitious growth targets lies not in demand, but on the production side.
Last year, Tesla delivered 84,000 vehicles.
In 2018 - all models combined - it should be 500,000, in 2020 one million.
Demand for this is obviously there.
According to Musk, there are 1800 reservations per day.
If you order a "Model 3" today, you can expect delivery in the second half of 2018 at the earliest - if Tesla manages to drive its brand-new production lines, which have never been tested in practice, without major interruptions with maximum utilisation.
Many experts doubt that this is possible.
But if there are major delays, the non-commitment of the reservations could become a problem for Tesla.
Because then the company cannot prevent dissatisfied customers from running away in droves.
EPA chief Pruitt is withdrawing to reduce emissions because of delaying Obama-Era rules
A day after being sued by 15 states, Scott Pruitt, the head of the Environmental Protection Agency, withdrewed his earlier decision to delay the implementation of Obama-era rules to reduce emissions of smog-causing air pollutants.
Pruitt presented the change as his agency proved more responsive to the needs of the state environmental authorities than previous administrations.
He did not mention the lawsuit, which had been brought against his previous body in a federal appeals court.
It's about a deadline that expired on 1 October for states to comply with 2015 standards for ground-level ozone.
Pruitt proclaimed in June that he would delay compliance by a year to give his authority more time to study the plan and avoid "mingling into local decisions or impeding economic growth."
Pruitt, who was an Oklahoma prosecutor prior to his appointment by President Donald Trump, has long been a relevant advocate of tighter environmental regulations.
Since his arrival in Washington, Pruitt has repeatedly moved to block or delay regulations that the chemical industry and the fossil fuel industry oppose.
Wednesday's sudden reversal is the latest legal setback for the Pruitt's regulatory rollback agenda.
Last month, a federal appeals court in Washington ruled that the EPA's administrator overstepped his authority when he tried to delay the implementation of a rule by the Obama administration requiring oil and gas companies to monitor and reduce methane leaks.
In an opinion published on Wednesday evening, Pruitt suggested that his U-turn on ozone standards quite simply intensify the EPA's readiness to cooperate with states through the complex process of timely fulfillment of the new standards.
"Among previous administrations, the EPA often failed to meet the deadlines for implementation and then waited to be indicted by activist groups and others, and, in a settlement, set the date for execution," said Pruitt, who sued the EPA more than a dozen in his previous job.
"We don't believe in regulation through legal proceedings, and we're taking deadlines seriously.
We also take seriously the law and the authority we have.”
Still, the EPO's opinion stated that Pruitt will at some point use his "delay power and all other legally available powers" to ensure that the regulations are "based on sound policies and the best available information."
Republicans in Congress are pushing for a broader recast of ozone rules.
A bill approved last month is intended to delay the implementation of 2015 rules by at least eight years.
The measure has not yet been put to a vote in the Senate.
New York Attorney General Eric Schneiderman, who leaded the coalition of states suing the EPA this week, said the group intends to maintain legal pressure.
"The reversal of the EPA - according to our lawsuits - is an important gain for the health and safety of these 6.7 million New Yorkers and the over 115 million Americans directly affected by the smog streaming into their communities," Schneiderman said.
In this case, New York joined California, Connecticut, Delaware, Illinois, Iowa, Maine, Massachusetts, Minnesota, New Mexico, Oregon, Pennsylvanien, Rhode Island, Vermont and Washington and the District of Columbia.
Soil ozone occurs when pollutants from cars, power plants, oil refineries, chemical plants and other sources in the atmosphere react to sunlight.
Emerging smog can cause serious breathing problems in sensitive groups of people, contributing to thousands of premature deaths each year.
Representatives of public health and environmentalists cheered over Pruitt's surprising change of course.
"It's worrying how much pressure it takes to get this sensible move from those responsible for protecting the air we breathe," said Lori Ann Burd of the Center for Biological Diversity.
"We must continue to fight against the ideological crusade of the Trump administration to engage with polluters and their special interests."
Tumlingen celebrates 750 years of St. Hilarius church
750 years of St. Hilarius Church: On the occasion of the anniversary of the mountain church, the old cemetery area in Tumlingen will be invited to a serenade on Sunday, July 16, at 4:30 p.m.
Whether its idyllic location and beautiful views of the village, the historic St. Hilarius complex is a popular place to linger.
Visitors to the Sixth Benefit Serenade can be enchanted by this powerful place.
The rescue and preservation of the oldest building in the village has made the friends and patrons of Tumlingen their concern.
Walter Martini emphasizes: "It's a unique village gem".
In 1267, the St. Hilarius Chapel was mentioned for the first time in the Württemberg Book of documents: Count Hermann von Sulz sold "the mountain with the parish church in Tumlingen located on it and the Vogtei" to Magister Eberhard von Horb and to his brother Dietrich for 20 marks of silver.
According to the wording of the document, in addition to the former parish church, there was also a stately court on Tumlinger Berg, to which the church or the patronage right belonged and not the other way around.
People lived there, too.
As can be seen from the description of Freudenstadt from 1858, in addition to the lower village and the upper village, which can still yet be seen today in the townscape of Tumlingen, the "mountain" was the third, the stately settlement core of the village.
Old maps from the period around the year 1600 underline these historical conditions.
Patronage law arose from early medievallian equivalence law.
A gentleman who built a church, equipped and occupied it with a pastor, remained the owner of this church and was also able to inherit this own church.
Associated with this was the right to appoint a pastor or to propose a new pastor to the bishop.
There were three altars in the church and two bells on the tower.
The taller of the two shattered at Christmas 1673.
The newly acquired bell first sounded in 1692, but had to be handed over later in the Second World War in 1942.
In the 30-year war (1616 to 1648), the window panes were smashed and the bell ropes were stolen.
The roof was leaking.
In 1796, French troops camped near the church.
They broke out the pulpit and church chairs and used them as firewood.
Remnants of the church threatened to collapse in 1809.
Until 1869, the Tumlinger Hilarius cemetery also served as the final resting place for the dead from Hörschweiler.
In 1823 the cemetery was expanded and the Hilarius Church was demolished.
Only the church tower was left standing.
On New Year's Eve in 1834, a storm split the church tower and half collapsed.
In order to prevent an accident, the tower was demolished.
Only the tower dwarf, which still exists today, was preserved as a witness to 750-year-old church history.
Today Horst Kallenberger and Rolf-Dieter Wasem from the "Schaffigen Pensioners" maintain the Hilarius facility as successors to Dieter Dockhorn.
As organizers, the friends and sponsors of Tumlingen invite on Sunday, July 16, to the sixth serenade to the old cemetery to the St. Hilariuskapelle.
It is designed by the combo of the Kepler-Gymnasium Freudenstadt and with speeches by Kurt Kübler and Reinhold Matteis.
Local leader Hartmut Romann speaks the final word.
The programme begins at 4:30 p.m.
Admission is from 15.30.
At the end of the serenade there will be a drink in front of the cemetery.
"Enjoy a summer evening under the shade of old trees and with the wide view over meadows to the forest," the organizers put out the fantastic view.
The serenade only takes place in dry weather.
The organizers hope for many supporters for the preservation of the historic St. Hilarius facility, which is important for Tumlingen and Hörschweiler.
Tumlingen's friends and sponsors are particularly committed to home care, as Serenade organizer Walter Martini confirms.
Association wants to have approval of wind branders examined
"Boisheim resists" sees no legal requirement for the construction and has therefore contacted the district council.
The "Boisheim resists" club has called on Landrat Andreas Coenen (CDU) to withdraw its approval for the construction of planned wind turbines in Boisheimer Nette.
It was unlawful.
The association argues that no wind turbines could have been approved at Boisheimer Nette at the time of approval on 30 December 2016, since at that time a wind power concentration zone only existed on the city border with Schwalmtal.
Within this concentration zone, construction and operation of wind turbines are basically possible, but at the same time the concentration zone causes an exclusion of the use of wind energy for the rest of the urban area.
Such zones are intended to prevent "asparagus" of the landscape.
The city of Viersen had decided a few days earlier by changing the land use plan a second wind power concentration zone in the Boisheimer Nette - but this had not been approved by the district government of Düsseldorf, because of a formal error.
"Due to the lack of a planning law requirement, the approval decision granted is still unlawful and therefore also to be withdrawn," said Christoph Erkens, chairman of "Boisheim resists."
Up-to-date state of affairs: Before the summer break, the city council once again decided on the wind concentration zone in Boisheimer Nette; an approval from the district government is still pending.
Erkens: "At this point, a permit for the construction of wind turbines is excluded under planning law."
The circle confirms the receipt of the request.
"The corresponding letters have been received since last week at Kreis Viersen," a spokesperson explained.
The circle takes them as an opportunity to re-examine the matter.
However, they are unlikely to stop the procedure.
With regard to ongoing proceedings at the administrative court, the district did not want to comment on the substantive content on the reasoning of the association "Boisheim resists".
NEW Re, a subsidiary of NEW, is planning the construction of four 200-meter-high wind turbines on an area on the Boisheimer Nette.
Already last year, the company, in which the city of Viersen is indirectly involved, had concluded leases with the owner of the approximately 65-hectare site.
With the construction of the four wind turbines, the electricity demand of around 11 400 households is to be covered - this corresponds to around one-fifth of the electricity requirements of all Viersen households.
NEW Re plans to invest around 20 million euros.
Last summer, Viersen’s city council gave the go-ahead for the purchase of three wind turbines at the Linnich wind farm near Aachen as well as a stake in a wind turbine in Übach-Palenberg in Heinsberg district.
Car and tractor collide: Woman dies in horrific accident
