One woman was fatally injured.
Two other people were slightly injured.
The highway remained closed for hours.
According to polices, a 17-year-old young woman from Bornich drove with her car with her 53-year-old mother as an accompanying person in the direction of Bogel.
City and merchants plan 'home-shopping' weekend
Bad Langensalzaer and people from the region are to rediscover the shopping facilities in the city center in September.
The trade association and the city administration want to use the weekend on 8 and 9 September to attract the residents for shopping in the city center.
As the chairman of the trade association, Christian König, announced, the "home shopping" weekend is intended to sensitize customers to what offers their own city offers away from the usual supermarket goods.
Accordingly, a market is planned with regional providers, similar to the weekly market.
In addition, domestic companies should present themselves with their training places.
In addition, the shops of the city center should invite you to "midnight shopping".
In doing so, the action is not only aimed at residents of the city and the surrounding region.
In preparation are commercials on the radio, an Internet video and printed bags pointing to the action weekend, according to Christian König.
City administration and trade association cooperate with the Chamber of Commerce and Industry.
Thus, the trade association and the city go into a further stage of the "Attractive Downtown" project.
This includes new offers such as the first "active day" for the Brunnenfest including Sunday open for sale, discussions with all traders and hosts to encourage them to actively participate.
Attractive offers for Saturday and waiving parking fees on this day are also part of the concept.
In addition, customer potential is to be redeveloped.
These include spa guests and tourists.
But also many locals would have lost sight of the city center while shopping.
In the end, everyone would have to help enliven the city center - up to landlords, the establishments of the whole city and clubs.
It was necessary to overcome with a network the lethargy, some of which had spread, King had said in the spring.
Birgit Streich, head of the IHK-Center in Mühlhausen, announced that five Thuringian cities are participating for the first time in the third "Heimat-Shoppen" day nationwide.
Next to Bad Langensalza these are Weimar, Sömmerda, Heiligenstadt and Bleicherode.
"The day should promote the appreciation of local commerce and emphasize its importance for the common good of our cities more strongly," Streich said.
The action under the leadership of the IHK and with many partners also makes it clear that every citizen can influence his direct environment himself with his purchasing behavior.
Two dead and several injured after explosion at U.S. school
During construction work, there was a gas explosion in the school building.
New other people were injured.
In a gas explosion in the U.S. city of Minneapolis, two people have been killed and nine others injured, some of them seriously.
According to the fire department, on Wednesday (local time) an injured man was still in critical condition.
One man died when part of a school building collapsed in the explosion.
An initially missing person was later also recovered dead.
The dead were employees of the school.
As a cause of misfortune, the authorities suspected work on a gas pipeline.
During the holiday season, only a few people were staying in the Minnehaha Academy building.
According to leaked transcripts of phone calls, Trump pressured Mexico over the payment of the border wall
Transcripts of phone calls between U.S. President Donald Trump and leaders of Mexico and Australia have been published by the Washington Post.
On January 27, in a phone call with Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto, Trump urged to stop saying publicly that he wouldn't pay for a planned border wall.
The U.S. president berated him for publicly denouncing the wall. He reportedly told him, "You can't tell the press."
Trump launched his presidential campaign with a promise to erect a wall on the southern U.S. border and vowed to let Mexico pay for the project.
Transcripts of his first call with Australian Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbull have also been released and revealed a tense exchange.
Mr. Trump told Mr. Turnbull that a trade for the U.S. refugees emigrating to Australia was "ridiculous," "lousy" and "stupid," and that the insistency on trade mediated by the Obama administration would make him look like a "dip" after proposing a ban on resettlement of refugees into the U.S.
Mr. Trump ended the phone call after he told Mr Turnbull "this is the most awkward call for the whole day."
Moore's Ford Massacre: Activists revive racist lynch act as call for justice
A 1948 Ford Mercury drove through a group of spectators in rural Monroe, Georgia, and rumbled towards the Ford bridge from Moore.
Before the car, which had four black passengers and a white driver, could reach the bridge, a group of white men with rifles stepped out to block his path.
The mob's leader - a middle-aged man in a pinstripe suit - took a long train at his cigar and gazed scrupulously through the windshield.
Inside, the inmates hovered in fear.
"We want this n --- r Roger!" the man roared.
"Go get him out of the car!"
Screams tore the silence when a young black man was dragged from the passenger seat.
Luckily, this horrific scene, played on July 22, is just a re-enactment.
But when a sling is throwned around the neck of "Roger", almost everyone in the audience ejected very real sighs.
Since 2005, civil rights activists have come back to Moore's Ford Bridge to imitate the night in which two black couples - Roger and Dorothy Malcom as well as George and Mae Murray Dorsey - were lynched by Ku Klux Klan in 1946.
No one has ever been charged with murder.
"It's really unbelievable that not a single person has been arrested in all these years - even though we're seeing them in our communities even though we know who they are," said Tyrone Brooks, who assists in organizing re-enactment.
“It’s a scord on a story, but it’s a burden on our souls.”
Brooks is a 71-year-old former congressman from the state of Georgia and a lifetime civil rights activist.
For him, re-enaction serves as a dramatic call to action and represents an annual reminder of the Monroe community that a wrong has never been corrected.
"We want law enforcement, we want cessation, we want healing, we want reconciliation, but first we have to receive justice," he said.
"We can't come to healing and reconciliation until we receive justice."
A coroner puts a sheet over the corpse of one of the victims of the Moore-Ford lynch on July 27, 1946.
According to FBI files obtained from the NBC News, more than 50 men from Monroe and the surrounding counties were suspected in the lynching trial.
But none has ever been charged.
Although the suspects have remained at large for decades, civil rights activists say the dual motivation for the lynch actions across the city has always been well-known: politics and sex.
In April 1946, a decision by the Supreme Court made it possible for black citizens in Georgia for the first time to cast their votes in the pre-election campaign in the summer.
Roughly at the same time as the election, according to the FBI, black farm tenant Roger Malcom stabbed a white landowner, Barnett Hester, during a fight - allegedly because of a woman.
Brooks said the rumor had been in the city that Hester had slept with Malcom's wife Dorothy and that the baby she was carrying was not from her husband.
On July 25, 19946, Loy Harrison, a prominent white landowner, paid 600 dollars to free Malcom from prison, according to the FBI.
He was escorted to prison by Dorothy and her cousins Mae Murray and George Dorsey.
But when they then moved away from the prison and approached Moore's Ford Bridge, a lynch mob surrounded the car.
"As it turns out, it was Harrison who prepared and ordered the murders," concludes an FBI report.
“It’s also obvious that there was a plot between state and local law enforcement that wasn’t just involved in the murders, eliminating evidence, and covering up the identities of witnesses.”
Brooks puts it even more succinctly.
"It was an electoral massacre," he said.
"You've all been killed to send a message to black people in this community: 'If you're going to register and if you vote, then that will happen to you here."
For nearly a decade Cassandra Greene has led the annual re-enactment of the Moore Ford Bridge Lynch killing.
Their portrayal is vivid, poignant and is grounded in a kind of deep racism that many would like to believe no longer exists in America.
It's hard to watch, and even harder to look away, but Greene said that's the point.
"We don't want to talk about lynching because then we're confronted with its ugliness in all of us," she said.
"But I can't build a relationship with you if you don't want to listen to me or don't want to hear what I've been through."
Rehearsals for this year's production began in June, and on the first day, nearly 30 actors - a dozen whites and the rest of the black - crowded into a room and listened when Greene launched a well-rehearsed overview of the play.
Wade Marbaugh is Greene's co-director here.
He's played the role of chief clan member for years, but he said the role would never get easier.
"I'm feeling dirty because I'm not," Marbaugh said.
"But I think it's very important to keep this story alive because we don't want to return to those times."
On the other side of the room, actors Darrius Bradshaw and Nick Rascona, the Roger Malcom and Barnett Hester, rehearsed their lines.
The play begins with Malcom shouting at Hester and harassing him, angry that he slept with his wife Dorothy.
"I said n --- he, disappear from my property!"
Rascona reciprocates fire.
There's a pause.
And then everyone (at least all black actors) breaks out into laughter.
Greene laughs the loudest.
"What?!" asked Rascona.
He's alarmed and confused.
Was I too hard?
"No," Greene said, still giggling.
"I just don't think that white people at that time said 'n --- he."
"Yes," quipped one of the other black performers.
"You just have to meet the hard 'R."'
And that's exactly where the energy in the room shifts, a sense of camaraderie replaces unease.
Everyone is busy getting this right, not only because of the game, but also because of the memory of the four people killed.
The scene begins again.
This time, when Rascona says the N-word, he says it sharply and emphatically.
Absolutely no one is laughing.
This repetition ends violently and quickly.
There are screams, gunshots and then silence.
A woman dressed in a grief's dress stands over the corpses of the actors and performs Billie Holiday's grieving eulogy "Strange Fruit."
Her voice breaks at every second word.
About a hundred meters behind, a group of about four dozen spectators wiped sweat and tears from their eyes.
The audience is divided almost evenly between black and white.
One couple has arrived from Ithaca, New York, to watch the performance, many just coming down the street.
They've all experienced Georgia's oppressive moisture for nearly eight hours as Brooks takes them on a pilgrimage to the Malcoms and Dorseys.
Up to this point, the murders have remained an anecdote, but now, standing on the same land where the lynch murders took place feels spooky.
When the song's last notes end, Greene invites the crowd to get closer and inspect the bodies.
Children squat on their heads, register the fake blood and silent bodies and whisper to each other.
"They're not really dead," mutters a little boy, as if he wanted to remember it.
After a few minutes, Greene thanks the crowd - and suddenly it's over.
The actors throw the lynching rope away and wipe their tears.
Bradshah hug Marbaugh, the man in a suit who played the clan member.
Nick Rascona hugs two of his teammates and then breaks down crying in their arms, the emotional burden of the performance eventually breaks through with him.
"It's okay," one of the women whispers.
"We know it's not in your heart.
You did this for a reason.”
Later, the audience sits together with the actors for dinner.
At a table, a white couple from Atlanta chat excited about the re-enactment with a black couple they've just met.
Denise Duplinski struggled to find words about what feeling the performance left.
"It's hard to hear these horrific words and deeds ... coming from people who look like yourself and who do because they look like you," she said.
"What's really doing this to me makes me believe the lynching still goes on," she added, "today that's just called police shooting."
In the same room Tyrone Brook's yard stops at his table, he eats and reminiscent of civil rights.
He said he'd already started planning the performance next year.
"Re-enactment is inspiring in some ways, because it describes the horror of what we've endured, but it's also a moment of learning," he said.
"It's so, if we don't learn anything from it, then it can happen again.
Those who do not study their own history, those who do not know their own history, are condemned to repeat it.”
"Götterdämmerung" reconciled the Wagnerians in Bayreuth
Also in the fourth part of Richard Wagner's monumental work, director Frank Castorf relies on a colorful-frivolous staging against a gigantic, six-storey backdrop.
The revolving stage (designed by Aleksandar Denic) shows either a shabby street corner with wall rest and kebab stall in Berlin, a "Plaste und Elaste" logo from GDR times or the New York Stock Exchange.
In front of that, an open Mercedes vintage car, in which the Rhine daughters pose as rocker girls, or alternatively an Isetta.
Stacked oil barrels are reminiscent of Castorf's leitmotif - money, power and greed - that runs through his "Ring" staging.
The three Rhine daughters - sung by Stephanie Houtzeel, Wiebke Lehmkuhl and Alexandra Steiner - harmonize wonderfully as in "Rheingold".
Kess seduces them Siegfried (Stefan Vinke), dragging a corpse into the trunk in a Hollywood manner and giving the krawallo clique.
Stephen Milling sings the gloriously nasty Hagen that surrounds the aura of a heavyweight boxer.
Brutal the scene as he slew Siegfried.
Later, Gunther (Markus Eiche) must also believe in it.
The audience thanks the ensemble with strong applause.
Foster, Milling, Vinke and conductor Janowski are stormingly celebrated, bowing modestly and their hands to the Merkel rhombus.
There is great applause for the festival orchestra, which shows itself on stage for the first time - in view of the great heat again in casual casual clothing.
The appearance of Frank Castorf and his directing team had been eagerly awaited.
If he had had to endure a whistling concert in 2013, there were great cheers five years later, in which the isolated "boo" shouts perished.
Festspiel boss Katharina Wagner may have been happy about it.
"You can see that the audience has grown together with the "Ring" in some ways," she had said shortly before the dpa's performance.
The audience had not only made a form of peace with Castorf, but it had also grown an understanding of his point of view.
I'm drawing a positive conclusion.
