Pearson cuts another 3,000 jobs and cuts the dividend to resuscitate business
Britain's Pearson education group said on Friday it would cut another 3,000 jobs and cut its interim dividend by 72 per cent, as another restructuring programme would be launched to overcome the pressure the company is facing.
The job cuships, which Director-General John Fallon announced predominantly for the end of 2018 and the beginning of 2019, comes in addition to its previous restructuring, in which 4,000 jobs have already been lost.
Pearson announced in a third cost-cutting programme in May a plan to cut costs by another 300 million pounds (US$394 million) to revitalize a deal hit by the rapid move towards digital learning.
The company, which divested its stake in Penguin Random House in partner Bertelsmann last month, said the outlook for the financial year was unchanged after it posted an increase of 1 percent to 2.05 billion pounds in the first half.
Kabul convoy attack: Nato soldier killed in suicide attack
A suicide bomber has attacked a military convoy in the Afghan capital of Kabul, killed a NATO soldier and injured several others, officials say.
Five soldiers and an interpreter were injured, a NATO statement said.
Unconfirmed local reports also killed three civilians in the incident in Qarabagh district.
The bombing is the last act of violence struck by Afghanistan this month - on Tuesday, more than 30 people were killed at a mosque in Herat.
On Monday, a suicidal bomber killed two people near the Iraqi embassy in Kabul.
Nato's statement said the explosion happened on Thursday evening when a joint patrol with the Afghan army was attacked by an "impromptu explosive device carried by individuals."
The statement said that all the injured are in a stable condition and are being treated in the U.S. military hospital at Bagram airfield.
Correspondents say that the growing strength of the Taliban and the group known as so-called Islamic State (IS) in Qarabagh is a major concern for NATO troops in nearby Bagram.
According to the United Nations, there were at least 1,662 civilian fatalities in Afghanistan in the first half of 2017, of which about 20% were in the capital.
In late July, a suicide car bomb killed at least 30 people in a mainly Shiite district of Kabul.
The Taliban said they carried out this attack.
On May 31, a huge bombardment in the center of the city killed more than 150 people with the country's deadliest militant attack since U.S.-leaded troops ousted Taliban from power in 2001.
Violence underscores the precarious security situation in Afghanistan, as U.S. President Donald Trump weighs to increase the number of U.S. troops who support military and police in the country.
Correspondents say that the suicide attacks in Kabul in recent weeks have also caused a crisis of public confidence in the government, its policies and, in particular, the reputation of President Ashraf Ghani.
EPA reverses decision to delay smog rule after lawsuits
The U.S. Environmental Welfare Agency has overturned a decision to delay regulation of Obama's administration, requiring states to curb smog-causing emissions. This came a day after 15 states sued the agency because of the move.
The EPA late Wednesday announced the decision to advance the so-called "Ozon Regulations 2015," and said it demonstrates the agency's commitment to cooperate with states.
"We believe in dialogue with and that we are responding to our state partners," EPA chief Scott Pruitt said in a statement.
Pruitt had announced in June the EPA's intention that ozone regulations - in which pollutant emissions are to be measured in a part of the country to determine where incisions must be made to comply with stricter air quality standards - to be delayed one year by October 2018.
A group of 15 largely democratic states, along with the district of Colombia, filed suit Tuesday, saying the effort was illegal.
President Donald Trump's administration has tried to scale back a wide range of environmental regulations imposed by former President Barack Obama as part of a broader agenda designed to boost economic growth, according to Trump.
Efforts, however, have sparked Democrats' setbacks and conservation groups worried about the impact of greater emissions on public health.
EPA's website says inhaling air containing ozone "can reduce pulmonary function and amplify respiratory symptoms" such as asthma.
