New results of Swedish asylum age assessment tests published
The most recent results of tests for asylum seekers in Sweden that have undergone a new medical assessment of their age show that more than 80 percent of those tested are older than 18. But not everyone believes in the accuracy of these results.
The Swedish Forensic Medicine (Rättsmedicinalverket) agency has begun carrying out the tests last year.
They are supposed to make the assessment of age more accurate during the asylum procedure, after the Swedish migration authority (Migrationsverket) has been criticised because of errors in assessing the correct age of some refugees who were minors.
To date, Migrationsverket has sent out 6,880 cases to be tested and the Forensic Medicine Agency has now released the results of a total of 2,481 tests from mid-March to July 31, 2017.
Eighty percent (2002) were judged to be 18 or older, while in another 25 cases the Forensic Medicine Agency judged that the person was "possibly 18 years or older."
In 432 cases, the conclusion was that the person is "possibly under 18 years old."
The tests are carried out only in cases where the person's age is questioned.
According to Johan Göransson of the Forensic Medicine Agency, the results have been consistent over the past few months.
"We don't see a big difference in the statistics of the past few months, the numbers are quite constant," he told The Local magazine.
The high proportion of cases where the person has been judged to be over the age of 18 does not necessarily mean that the asylum seeker lied about his age.
In May, the agency released the findings of 581 tests, saying there were 442 cases "proposing that the person examined was 18 years or older."
When newspaper Svenska Dagbladet examined the 581 published results, she found that in 243 of these cases, the person had openly said she turned 18 this year.
The method of medical age assessment, in which X-rays of wisdom teeth and MRI images of knee joints are taken, which are then analyzed by dentists and radiologists, has also been criticized in some areas.
A skeptist is endocrinologist Claude Marcus of the Karolinska Institute, whose work includes assessing the biological maturity of humans to see if they went through puberty sooner or later than usual.
"I had reason to think about it, and I don't think the type of measurement should be used.
Judging biological age in this way is very uncertain.
If we can't say for sure, we shouldn't pretend we could," he told Metro newspaper.
Göransson of the Forensic Medicine Agency, however, defended the accuracy of the tests: "In general, you can say that the risk of incorrectly assessing a child as an adult is low.
The biggest risk is when the person is just under 18, in this case that's about 10 percent."
Fully on schedule
Work on the three bridges of Landstraße 108 between Lauterbach and Schramberg is progressing well.
On Wednesday, a seven-ton concrete part was installed on the bridge at the eastern entrance of Lauterbach.
"We are very good in time, it's going great," says Reinhold Leicht, construction manager at the Department of Roads and Transport at the Freiburg government headquarters.
In April, work began simultaneously on all three bridges in need of renovation between Schramberg and Lauterbach.
The end of construction is scheduled for October and Easily it is certain that the schedule will be adhered to.
The first bridge, coming from Schramberg, leads over the Trombach.
It is a vaulted arch bridge made of concrete, which is clad with red colored sandstone and has a total length of 4.80 meters.
It was built in 1900.
Like the other two bridges, it is getting old by now.
In order to ensure stability and road safety, the three structures will now be thoroughly refurbished.
The other two bridges lead over the Lauterbach.
They're made of red colored sandstone.
Both were built in 1939 and are twelve or 10.60 meters long.
The bridge at the entrance to Lauterbach has now received a 1.30-meter-wide and seven-ton so-called bridge cap on the north side on Wednesday morning.
This is an edge attachment, which is also used as an operating route.
A 2.20-meter-wide bridge cap is to be deployed at the southern part of the bridge on Friday.
Then the pedestrian path should run on it.
What is special about this is that these are finished parts, a patent of the Schramberger company Dizwo.
The other two bridges are already equipped with the patented bridge caps.
Particular sensitivity has required the laying of pipes through the building in Lauterbach.
Because there is also a fiber optic cable, to which many companies are connected.
The local residents were informed about the project in advance of the work by handouts.
In addition, fish and crabs first had to be fished off.
For the bridges between the construction yard building and the collective pond in the direction of Schramberg, the traffic is one-lane regulated by a traffic light.
For the construction work on the bridge at Lauterbach entrance, the road in the area of the bridge at the height of the hotels "Tannenhof" and "Sieben Linden" is fully closed.
The cost of the three bridge renovations together amounts to 700 000
The whole thing is financed by the country.
AfD in Gerolstein: Colorful protest at German potato salad
It doesn't happen so often that parties pull together, but when it comes to the Alternative for Germany (AfD), it comes together, which is otherwise difficult to reconcile.
The parties (CDU, SPD, Grüne, FDP, FWG, Linke) represented in the Kreistag belong to a broad alliance, which on this day peacefully shows flag, because the AfD invites in the Gerolsteiner Stadthalle Rondell according to the "large election campaign kick-off".
And brings well-known names to the start - such as Alice Weidel, who together with Alexander Gauland forms the top duo for the federal election on September 24, and the deputy federal chairwoman Beatrix von Storch.
But those who are already on site hours before the start of the event don't want to listen to AfD learners, but want to demonstrate.
The motto "The Eifel remains colorful".
Originally, the city picnic was planned under the motto "All at one table" on the Brunnenplatz, but is now taking place in the parking lot below the Rondell - and thus directly in front of those who come to the AfD event in the Stadthalle.
It’s a colorful picture that presents itself there.
And which Christa Karoli, chairwoman of the Forum Eine Welt and one of the authoritative organizers, is really pleased: "Great how many people have come together here".
There's music and plenty of food, including a "primordial-German potato salad that the AfD could easily eat," says the woman who brought him along.
Police has ordered many officers to Gerolstein, showing presence especially at the station, which is only a few meters away from the venues.
How did the AfD actually come about Gerolstein?
Mario Hompes of the AfD Vulkaneifel reports that he has tried for such an event and received the contract.
The 45-year-old from Gönnersdorf had initially asked in Daun, but received a cancellation there, because there were not enough parking spaces because of the already built-up Laurentiuskirmes.
So now the Rondell, Hompes is happy that "we can have such a nice event here".
Referring to the event on the Rondell forecourt, he says: "That's the good right of the people, the main thing, it stays peaceful".
That's it: Around 8 p.m., a band provides entertainment on the Rondell forecourt, in the Stadthalle various speakers, including the Rhineland-Palatinate AfD chairman Uwe Junge.
In the hall, there are about 200 listeners who are waiting for the top candidate for the federal election, Alice Weidel, and the deputy federal chairwoman Beatrix von Storch to give their speeches.
The police presence is unchanged, but so far the officers have had a quiet evening.
The AfD event lasts just over an hour, the guests on the forecourt have a longer breath, but even there shortly after 9 p.m
The police can turn away - no special incidents.
Trombone meets trumpet
A rehearing with the Wolfach trombonist Frederic Belli will take place in the Haslacher Klosterkonzerte series tomorrow, Sunday, at 8 p.m. in the monastery church.
Together with the Bulgarian trumpeter Miroslav Petkov, he gives a concert under the motto "Black Sea meets Black Forest", or "Balkanfolklore" meets a classical German education with roots in wind music.
Both soloists are the winners and winners of numerous international competitions and hold solo positions in top European orchestras.
Petkov is a solo trumpeter with the renowned Concertgebouw Orchestra Amsterdam and Frederic Belli is a soloposaunist of the SWR Symphony Orchestra.
Both combine the desire not only to cross the boundaries on their instruments, but also merge the boundaries of the different genres.
Johann Sebastian Bach resounds with the two as in a new guise, Daniel Schnyder's "Weltmusik" combines with Bulgarian folklore, and in the piece "Exchange Suite" by the jazescho laureate Nils Wogram, classical music, jazz and free playing mix into a fascinating work of art.
This centerpiece of the program was premiered in Freiburg only this spring with huge success.
The renowned composer Nils Wogram has written it especially for the two musicians and even more: The Haslacher Klosterkirche with its special acoustics is deliberately included in the concert.
Kristen Stewart talks about her bisexuality
"Some people love grilled chease and will eat until the end of their lives.
But I want to try everything," Kristen Stewart openly said of her bisexuality.
"Once I bite in grilled cheese, I'm like, 'Oh that's cool, what's next?'
Arguably the Hollywood star's best-known relationship was that to her "Twilight" colleague Robert Pattinson, who ultimately broke up bitterly.
In spite of all this, the brunette actress assures "Harper's Bazaar" magazine: "I was seriously in love with everyone I've dated.
Or do you think I played it?”
After her break-up, Kristen admitted that she was bisexual: "I've always lived the dualism and seriously believed in it.
So it's never confused or hindered me.
I've just never liked not to be taken seriously.'
According to "Bang Showbiz," she suffered in particular when she preferred to dress like a bub: "Not all girls are typical girls.
That really hurt me a lot.
When I was eleven, the kids told me I looked like a boy.
I really felt assaulted, terrified and embarrassed.
Now I look back and think to myself, 'Girl, be proud of you!'
She doesn't like to explain her love life to the public in particular: "People expect you to behave in a certain way and that annoys me.
And maybe some call me ungrateful, but I don't like having to justify myself in front of the public.'
The governor of West Virginia announces that he'll change parties
. West Virania's Democracy Governor, Jim Justice, announced Thursday night at a rally with President Trump that he would change the party.
Whether you like it or not, the Democrats have moved away from me," Governor Justice said.
"Today I will tell you with a lot of prayers and a lot of reflection ... today I will tell you as residents of West Virginia, I can't help you anymore if I'm a Democratic governor."
The rally emerged in cheers.
"Tomorrow I'll change my registration to Republicans," Justice added.
"As a coach, I'd say it's time to play another game."
Mr. Trump previously promised a major announcement at the West Virany Rallye.
Mr. Trump won West Virginia by 42 points, and Justice didn't endorse Democratic nominee Hillary Clinton.
Justice, who according to Forbes is the only billionaire in the state, was elected in 2016.
Forbes notes that he owns coal mines in five states, and inherited a coal business from his father.
It's worth about 1.59 billion dollars.
Meanwhile, the Republican National Committee issued a statement on Thursday naking Thursday naxe saying: "The party change by Governor Justice is another blow to a Democratic Party that would prevent us from working to make our country great again."
"With only 15 governorates and the scrutiny of the fewest legislative bodies in history, Governor Justice's announcement is only the last rejection of a party that is without leadership from top to the bottom and is unable to find a positive, unifying message," RNC chairwoman Ronna McDaniel said in a statement to the CBS News.
Coe praises 'honest' Russian apology for doping scandal
Athletics' worldwide chief executive, Sebastian Coe, on Thursday praised what he described as an overt apology from Russia on a doping scandal, but he reiterated that it was not the moment for the country to be welcomed back into the sport.
Russia's athletics leader Dmitry Shylakhtin said during the IAAF congress on the eve of the World Athletics Championships that his country's banishment from the sport was correct and that he was determined to fight doping.
He said he was sorry for "all athletes who were snatched away from gold and silver medals at competitions."
Despite his comments, the IAAF Congress voted in favour of maintaining the ban imposed in November 2015 after a World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) report found evidence of state-sponsored doping in Russia.
"I thought it was a very open response today, a very honest presentation," IAAF President Coe told reporters.
"The whole council and the entire congress were pleased that the Russian Federation has recognized with itself that it has experienced some fairly hot times and will do everything possible to ensure that the federation is being redesigned."
Coe said he was also "pleased" that Russia was accepting the criteria for its reintroduction.
"I think it was a very constructive day, and I think progress has been made, but Congress supports the task force's recommendations that this is not the moment to reinstate Russia," Coe said.
"We need to do everything in the next few months to normalize this situation.
The guiding principle has always been that we wanted to separate the clean athletes from the tainted system," he added
Nineteen Russian athletes will compete as neutral players at the World Athletics Championships, which start in London on Friday, according to the stern IAAF criteria.
The head of the IAAF task force, Rune Andersen, said on Monday that Russia still had to meet some of the criteria for reinstatement.
Drug tests are still inadequate and banned trainers are still free to work, he told reporters in London after a talk at the IAAF Council.
Funeral act for soldiers who crashed in Mali
At a funeral service in Fritzlar in northern Hesse, around 1000 people remembered the two soldiers who died in a helicopter crash in Mali.
The ceremony took place on Thursday in the cathedral city under high security precautions.
Defense Minister Ursula von der Leyen (CDU) said nothing about the unexplained crash cause.
However, she described the two killed pilots as the "adversaries of her trade".
"We are bowing to this supreme sacrifice they made on behalf of the Federal Republic of Germany," she explained.
The 33-year-old major and 47-year-old chief of staff from northern Hesse had died a week ago in West African Mali on Wednesday.
Their coffins were transferred to Germany and on Thursday escorted by a Bundeswehr wake to Fritzlar Cathedral in Sankt Peter.
At noon only the relatives in the church said goodbye.
Afterwards, the funeral ceremony began in the circle of the family, closest friends, representatives of the Bundeswehr and politics.
Among them were Hesse's Prime Minister Volker Bouffier (CDU) and the inspector general of the Bundeswehr Volker Wieker.
Mourners laid down roses, the relatives stood arm in arm on the coffins.
In front of the church Fritzlarer and numerous soldiers followed the ceremony on a canvas.
Defense Minister von der Leyen described the perished soldiers as "highly decorated" and experienced.
The chief of staff helped set up the flight operations of the Tiger attack helicopter in Fritzlar and was one of the first six tiger pilots of his regiment.
Volker Bauersachs, commander of the regiment to which the soldiers belong, also declared: "If this crew could not avert the crash, then no one would have."
Doubts about the U.N. mission in Mali didn't see Minister von der Leyen: The deployment was dangerous.
But he is also of high importance "for a country from which we are separated only by two states," she said.
After the funeral, the coffins were taken out of the cathedral.
When and where they will be buried, the Bundeswehr did not provide information.
Prince Henrik doesn't want a tomb beside his wife
The husband of Danish Queen Margrethe (77), Prince Henrik (83), does not want to be buried next to his wife after his death.
This was announced by the royal family on Thursday.
Traditionally, the Danish royal couple is buried in Roskilde Cathedral Church.
The sculptor Bjørn Nørgaard already made a sarcophagus for the two.
The queen agreed with her husband's decision, the statement said.
It is still unclear where Prince Henrik will be buried.
However, he had no intention of being buried in his French homeland.
Prince Henrik had never made a secret of the fact that he was not happy with his title.
After ascending the throne of his wife Margrethe in 1972, he would have liked to become king.
In 2016 he retired and also passed the title "Prinzgemahl".
Since Henrik in Denmark did not get the title and the function he wanted, he does not see himself as equal to his wife - even after the death, explained Lene Balleby, the chief spokeswoman of the Danish royal family.
Where the 83-year-old's final resting place will ultimately be is still unclear.
However, he did not intend to be buried in his French homeland, but in Denmark.
He likes the country he's been working for over the past 50 years.
Henri de Laborde de Monpezat, son of a French nobility, met Margrethe in London in 1965.
He worked on the Thames in the French Embassy.
While his later wife’s sympathy statements of the people, Prince Henrik had a difficult stand among the Danes in his 50-year regency.
In an autobiography, Henrik commented on the first few years in Denmark: "A few months after my arrival, everything I did was criticized.
My Danish was fluctuating, I preferred wine instead of beer, silk stockings instead of knitted socks, Citroën instead of volvo, tennis instead of football.
Even for the Gauloises I smoked instead of Virginia tobacco and who had a reputation in this country for being the brand of society-critical intellectuals, I couldn't hope for indulgence.
I've been different.
I seemed satisfied with that position and not ashamed.
That was two mistakes!”
Prime Minister Weil wants a quick new election
CDU and FDP before possible majority in Lower Saxony
Lower Saxony's Prime Minister Stephan Weil (SPD) is after the loss of the red-green government majority for a rapid new election of the state parliament, but he does not want to resign.
Weil spoke out in favour of the dissolution of parliament on Friday.
Previously, the surprise withdrawal of a Green MP from her state parliamentary group in Lower Saxony had plunged the government of Prime Minister Stephan Weil (SPD) into a serious crisis.
Five months before the official date of the state election, the red-green coalition in Hanover thus lost its narrow one-vote majority in parliament to the opposition of the CDU and FDP.
Elke Twesten, a member of the Greens, justified her move with the non-nomination for the 2018 election in her constituency in Rotenburg (Wümme).
"I see my political future in the CDU," she declared in Hanover on Friday.
CDU parliamentary group leader Björn Thümler wants to advise his group to accept it.
As a result, CDU and FDP together have 69 seats in the Lower Saxony state parliament, the SPD and the Green would have 68 seats.
So far, the ratio has been
The new state parliament is regularly elected on 14 January next year.
Thümler said the red-green state government now had to decide whether it could continue to govern without a majority in this situation.
Stephan Weil, however, thinks it is indispensable that the state parliament decides on its self-dissolution as soon as possible.
The CDU group is expected to decide on its further action on Tuesday.
The state constitution also provides for the possibility that the state parliament deprives the prime minister of confidence and chooses a successor.
"Our Constitution offers several options.
These possibilities must be examined legally clean," Thümler said.
"You will quietly discuss all questions if they are legally sensibly tested."
Thümler called Twesten's move "but somewhat curious."
The politician herself emphasized: "I'm not a traitor.
“I feel very good.”
She described herself as a supporter of black-green.
The Greens urged breakaway MPs to return their state parliament mandate.
"Of course, we assume that she will return her state parliament mandate, which she has received via the green state list, with immediate effect," the state chairmen Meta Janssen-Kucz and Stefan Körner said in a statement.
Anja Piel said: "We are extremely sorry for Elke Twesten's decision".
She consciously decided not to have any debate in the group.
"Even against the background that there were no substantive differences, we cannot understand this step."
SPD chancellor candidate Martin Schulz condemns the conversion of Lower Saxony's Green MEP Elke Twesten to the CDU as a betrayal.
"The behavior of the former Green MPs in Lower Saxony is not only betrayal of the voters, but also betrayal of red-green," the head of the Social Democrats wrote on Facebook on Friday.
He supports the decision of Prime Minister Stephan Weil (SPD) to let the state parliament re-elected quickly.
The departure of Twesten costs red-green in Lower Saxony the majority.
"In her hurt vanity and in her frustration, the politician put her personal interests before the welfare of the country," criticized SPD Secretary-General Hubertus Heil.
"It is a scandal that the CDU in Lower Saxony participates in this dirty intrigue game and tries to make political capital out of it."
Lower Saxony's CDU parliamentary group leader Björn Thümler wants to advise his deputies to add Elke Twesten to the CDU.
The parliamentary group leader of the SPD in the Bundestag, Thomas Oppermann, accused Twesten of distorting the result of the state election in 2013 with personal career thinking.
The CDU was the strongest force before the SPD at that time, but the SPD and Greens had a wafer-thin majority of one vote in the state parliament.
Minister of Agriculture in an interview - How do we protect ourselves from poison eggs, Mr Schmidt?
Germany is discussing contaminated eggs.
How did the scandal happen?
How can consumers protect themselves.
Minister of Agriculture Christian Schmidt (CSU) is in an interview with the RedaktionsNetzwerk Deutschland (RND).
Mr. Schmidt, How can the consumer protect himself from poisoned eggs?
I can only encourage all consumers to look closely at the eggs in your refrigerator and check the stamp imprint.
On the consumer protection portal www.Lebensmittelwarning.de, the federal states set up the latest information about the affected egg batches.
Affected eggs should be destroyed or returned.
Even if, according to everything we currently know, a health hazard is practically excluded.
Is Aldi now adopting the best possible crisis reaction - all the eggs out of the offer - because the central role of enlightener is not really perceived by the Federal Minister of Agriculture?
Food surveillance is the responsibility of the federal states.
Nevertheless, my house has turned up immediately and has been in close contact with the authorities of the countries since Germany became aware.
All information and the risk assessment are available to consumers.
Trade, like manufacturers, must ensure that the products sold are roadworthy and safe for health.
In the case of eggs, the maximum residue level of 0.005mg/kg has been exceeded, they are not marketable.
Unencumbered eggs, however, can be sold and eaten.
It's not the companies' fault this time.
Are production companies and consumers in the same boat?
According to the current state of knowledge, the production companies are not directly responsible.
Rather, they have to expect economic damage due to alleged illegal practices of service companies.
As always, there are too few inspectors in an emergency.
Is there only a lack of personnel or conclusive jurisdiction?
It was precisely the official checks that uncovered the process.
The truth, however, is that food fraud and criminal energy can never be 100 percent excluded.
Precisely for this reason, a consistent investigation of such events and the handover to the public prosecutor’s offices are of crucial importance in the cases in which crimes may exist.
I have already made it clear on several occasions that I am expecting complete clarification from the responsible authorities.
Is regular alarmism in food scandals really necessary?
Consumers have the right to receive only safe and marketable food.
When an unauthorised insecticide is used in the food industry, consumers are rightly outraged.
Due to the panicked means, manufacturers, distributors and consumers have fallen into a situation that is absolutely not necessary.
In principle, it must be clear that the illegal use of the insecticide Fipronil in keeping food-supplying animals is unacceptable and must be terminated immediately.
This must be worked on consistently and at high pressure at all levels.
I expect all the competent authorities to clarify the matter quickly and completely.
How football's financial rules affect Neymar's move to Paris
The rules aimed at preventing unhindered spending by European football clubs won't bother Neymar's world-record traffer from Spain's Barcelona to France's Paris-Saint Germain.
Any consequences are yet to come for Paris Saint-Germain from UEFA, because the governing organisation overseeing the European game has the power to ban squads from access to the prestigious Champions League tournament.
Financial fair-play rules launched by UEFA in 2011 mean PSG will eventually have to prove that Neymar's transfer has been financed without major losses.
Barcelona has paid 222 million euros ($262 million) to buy the Brazilian out of his contract, and Neymar will receive a salary that will amount to several ten million dollars a year.
Here are some of the financial aspects of Neymar's relocation to France.
PSG can certainly loosen the money for Neymar to sign and take him to a glaxy of other stars, given the team's funding from Qatar's energy-riching ruling family.
Clubs are allowed to spend a lot of money on the acquisition of players, but have to counterance with legitimate revenue streams that allow them the break-even point in their football-related business.
FFP was conceived by Michel Platini after his election as UEFA president in 2007.
The former French player believed that clubs that had incurred enormous debts in their quest for success were effectively cheating and risked ruining themselves if banks or owners withdrew their support.
In 2011, FFP shifted from concentrating on clubs' debts to monitoring their losses.
The change meant that the business mopule Roman Abramvich originally used in Chelsea in 2003 - bought a club with potential and then invested vigorously to improve the squad and build a global brand - should become a tougher affair.
During the current three-year FFP assessment period, which runs until 2018, clubs can accept losses amounting to 30 million euros.
And although Platini has fallen out of favour by the presidency in 2015, his UEFA successor Aleksander Ceferin remains committed to deterring excessive spending.
"Certainly FFP is not dead and we will certainly reinforce the rules for the future," Andrea Traverso, who is in charge of FFP at UEFA, told The Associated Press last week.
Although PSG has paid Neymar's buy-out clause in a lump sum, it can distribute the amount for invoicing over the duration of its contract.
Neymar has signed a five-year contract, so that the transfer amount for the next five years in the amount of 44.4 million euros can be reported in the annual financial statements.
The first wave of FFP sanctions in 2014 has hit PSG, along with Abu Dhabi, which is owned by Manchester City, with the heaviest sanctions.
The PSG received a fine of 60 million euros (then 82 million dollars), which was later reduced to 20 million euros - and it has been ordered that the team members for the Champions League of the 2014/15 season remains limited to 21 players instead of the otherwise normal 25 players.
UEFA is looking for clubs that might try to outwit their financial oversight by inflating their revenues through companies affiliated with the owners of a club.
While a Qatari company could sponsor PSG if the value of sponsorship were to exceed the standard market rates, UEFA would not accept this as a legitimate income.
That's exactly what happened in 2014, when UEFA decided that the value of the PSA's sponsorship was bloated bleated by the Qatar Tourism Authority.
It has been reported that a portion of the PSG payments to Neymar could come via payments from the 2022 World Cup's organising committee in Qatar.
But PSG and Qatar in 2022 have denied that Neymar will become World Cup ambassador and such a manoeuvre for UEFA regulators could be easy to find out.
The previous sanctions against PSG mean that UEFA will be even more attentive to observe any attempt to circumvent the rules.
UEFA had no preparatory talks with PSG about Neymar's rental, the people familiar with the situation said on Thursday.
They spoke with The Associated Press on contending anonymity as they were unauthorised to discuss the PSG situation.
The latest published reports show that PSG achieved a profit of more than 10 million euros in 2015/16 with a turnover of 542.4 million euros.
Accounts for the final fiscal year have yet to be released.
PSG expect Neymar's arrival to be a boon for sponsorship deals, merchandising and jersey sales.
The association expects sales to increase by at least 30 percent after signing.
The Spanish league was clearly irritated to seeing one of the best football players go, and she tried to prevent Neymar's departure by claiming PSG would injury the FFP.
"It's a clear case of "financial doping" with the club and the state," the president of the La League, Javier Tebas, told the Spanish daily AS regarding Qatar's eventual ownership of PSG.
Arsenal manager Arsene Wenger, whose club is in possession of an American sports tycoon, shares La Liga's reservations about a state funding a club.
"It's the consequence of ownership and that's completely changed football's entire landscape over the past 15 years," Wenger said Thursday.
"Once a country owns an association, anything is possible.
It will be very difficult to respect financial fair play, because you can take different paths or have different interests when a country has such a big actor to represent a country.”
Swiss is looking for a new Schoggi supplier
In recent years, Chocolat Frey has produced on-board chocolate for Swiss.
Now the sweet cooperation is facing a possible end: The airline is rewriting the order.
Swiss spokeswoman Meike Fuhlrott confirmed at the request of the news agency a corresponding report of the newspaper "Nordwestschweiz" on Friday.
According to up-to-date planning, the airline will conclude a new contract in the first half of 2018.
The products would be inspected every two to three years and subjected to quality control.
"We are very satisfied with the current supplier Chocolat Frey," the spokeswoman said.
Around 17 million small schoggi boards packed in red-and-eyed 14 grams are distributed to the passengers each year prior to landing.
Swiss did not want to provide information on the costs.
According to "Northwest Switzerland", industry insiders estimate the order volume at 1.5 to 2 million Swiss francs - the Migros subsidiary generates an annual turnover of around one billion Swiss francs.
But the advertising effect is always more important: For a chocolate manufacturer, it is a prestigious order.
How many applicants there are in the new tender, Swiss does not want to reveal.
However, only Swiss manufacturers are eligible.
According to the newspaper report, Chocolat Frey intends to seek a renewal of the contract beyond the spring of 2018.
For the sda, the largest Swiss chocolate manufacturer could not be reached for comment.
Swiss has been distributing chocolate boards aboard its flights since 2006.
Before Swiss entered into a contract with Migro's subsidiary, Barry Callebaut, in cooperation with Hug, had delivered the chocolate tablets.
Swiss introduced the possibility to place the logo two and a half years ago with the start of the collaboration with Chocolat Frey.
The airline cooperates with various Swiss chocolate producers.
On long-haul flights, first-class customers are spoiled with delicacies from the Sprüngli confectionery, in business class, passenger Schoggi von Lindt and in the economy class Schoggi by Chocolat Frey.
Swiss Schoggi from Chocolat Frey is also distributed on the short route, both in business and economy class.
Athletics - Discus Olympic champion Robert Harting reaches World Cup finals
The three-time world champion is competing for a medal in Saturday's final.
His brother Christoph, Olympic champion of 2016, had failed in the national World Cup elimination and could only look at the discus competition from the stands.
Also with the first attempt, Polish defending champion Piotr Malachowski, who threw 65.13 meters wide, qualified for the final fight.
Nostalgia goes into the nich in "Wet Hot American Summer" and "Comrade Detecto"
This repetition of the story is simultaneously nostalgic because of the glorious days of the characters of the 80's, for the 90's grunge era (when the thought leaders David Wain and Michael Showalter shot the sketch comedy "The State" for MTV), and for themselves - that mean, for 2001, before her stars, like Elizabeth Banks, Amy Poehler and Paul Ru
As a cultural artifact, it is equally a parody and an embodiment of our Facebook-enabled era of permanent memories.
But as a franchise, it feels like time is running out.
"First Day of Camp" worked surprisingly well, not just because of its absurd humour (it contained an original story for a talking can of vegetables spoken by H. Jon Benjamin).
By stifling to the core of the gag - middle-aged actors play horny children who grow up - they still managed the impossibility of winning back the last part of the joke.
"Ten Years Later," with its adults now playing adults, is less emotionally rooted and its parody goes more according to the watering can principle, including a reef on the stalker films of the 90's such as "The Hand That Rocks the Cradle."
At one point, a character browses through an Apple powerbook, some inline skates and "a number of other things from 1991."
A lot of the central stories are the same.
Gracious Coop (Mr. Showalter), now novelist, still hasn't forgotten Katie (Marguerite Moreau).
Victor (Ken Marino) still hasn't lost his virginity.
And there's another mad-malicious plot involving Ronald Reagan (also Mr. Showalter), who is now backed by George H. W. Bush (a well-successful impression of Michael Ian Black).
As always, excries are the best part of
It's especially fun to see the eternally young Mr. Rudd showing Matt Dillon's lower lip beard of "Singles," whose former big-man-on-camp Andy now feels like an old cracker.
It's the moments of random madness that could fuel this franchise like a kind of repetitive "Seven Up!" series until he achieves what I'm taking as his logical conclusion: "Wet Hot American Summer: Twenty Years Later," whose final scene the gange will show how she will attend the film premiere of "Wet Hot American Summer."
"Comrade Detective" on Amazon, meanwhile, is reminiscent of another type of nostalgia: the revival of the Cold War of "The Americans," the film "Atomic Blonde," and even "GLOW" with its East-vs-West wrestling iconography.
The comedy created by Brian Gatewood and Alessandro Tanaka with Rhys Thomas as the director claims to be a found TV series: a Romanian drama of the eighties with the two policemen as friends, two officers from Bucharest fighting for truth, atheism and the path of Marxism-Leninism.
The leading roles - a hard-boiled Gregor and an idealistic Iosef - are played by Florin Piersic Jr. and Corneliu Ulici, whose dialogues are dubled by Channing Tatum (also an executive producer) and Joseph Gordon-Levitt are synchronized in English.
It's a brilliant idea.
But it's not much more than an idea.
There's some beautiful gags to watch (the police spend the time watching chess on TV, as Americans might do with football).
And the list of famous speakers - including Jenny Slate, Nick Offerman, Mahershala Ali and Chloë Sevigny - makes for a decent game 'Find out the voice.'
But "Comrade Detective" engages so much for the veracity of a desperate propaganda drama - a murderer wearing a Reagan mask is included - that it often plays like a single one.
With six long episodes it drags on, and the comedy isn't fast or frequent enough.
"Comrade Detective," who has been redicated to a lesser length, could deliver his agitprop satire better, as Gregorge and Iosef repeatedly visit the American embassy, whose lobby is always occupied by two-thick men devouring a bunch of hamburgers.
The message for the imagined communist audience is that Western culture can make everything a fodder for a feast.
The fictional Romanian creators weren’t wrong: we’re even doing it with our own recent history and with their own.
Blow to drug mafia in the country
The police has struck a blow against the Italian mafia in Baden-Württemberg.
On Wednesday morning, numerous objects were searched in the Schwarzwald-Baar-Kreis, in Rottweil, Konstanz, Esslingen and Stuttgart, as the public prosecutor's office Konstanz announced on Friday.
In the process, 15 defendants aged between 25 years and 77 were arrested and arrest warrants have already been issued against them.
In addition, the officers ensured larger quantities of marijuana and cocaine, firearms and several hundred thousand euros.
Two people were arrested in contemporaneous raids in Italy.
There were German arrest warrants against the two suspected Italians.
The two are therefore presumably extradited to Germany.
Italian and Bavarian investigators were involved in the action.
In total, more than 300 police officers were deployed.
Members of the mafia are accused of organized drug crimes, serious acts of violence, attempted murder and robbery and extortion.
Since the summer of 2016, the public prosecutor’s office has been investigating Konstanz against the organized drug trade in southern Germany.
There was a lively exchange between the public prosecutor’s offices in Constance and Palermo and the investigators of Kripo Rottweil had almost daily contact with their colleagues in Sicily.
Therefore, the large-scale operation was accompanied on Wednesday morning by four officials from Palermo.
The investigations were mainly targeted by Italian nationals with a close connection to the Italian mafia.
Also, an incident on May 27 in Hüfingen, in which several shots were fired early in the morning into a restaurant, is likely to go to the account of the gang.
Wise laments major gaps in foreigner register
"Data of citizens who should not appear there": According to a report, the register of foreigners in Germany has significant shortcomings.
This leads to sometimes serious wrong decisions in asylum proceedings.
Frank-Jürgen Weise, the commissioner for refugee management of the federal government, has drawn attention to large gaps in the central register of foreigners, according to a media report.
Deficiencies in the data registered there could "lead to serious wrong decisions during asylum proceedings and deportations," Weise warned in an analysment submitted to the "Süddeutsche Zeitung."
According to ten million people with foreign nationality are recorded in the register, including about 5.7 million from non-EU countries.
The data are therefore managed by about 600 immigration authorities in Germany.
Because the data had not been adequately maintained, the data quality in parts had not been good so far.
In individual cases, data from 1921 had been found, "from people who are no longer alive, or from citizens who have become German citizens and should actually no longer appear in the register."
"Faulty data inputs can significantly slow down return processes," the report said.
The statistics on people who are obliged to experience are also covered by EU citizens, who do not have to leave at all.
However, such incorrect assignments "led to a distorted debate about dealing with those obliged to leave," according to the analysis.
Meanwhile, the German government has apparently begun sending refugee families with children under the age of 16 back back to Italy when they arrived from there.
This is according to an internal paper of the Federal Office for Migration and Refugees (BAMF), reports the "Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung".
According to the paper, the BAMF has been carrying out such procedures "with regard to families with children - with the exception of infants and toddlers - since 1 June 2017".
Since the end of 2014, this was practically no longer possible, according to the newspaper with reference to the difficult situation on the ground.
According to the Dublin agreement, the EU state is responsible for refugees, which they reach first.
If a migrant travels on to another EU country, that state can send it back to the country of entry.
As a justification, the office now writes: "Meanwhile, the reception capacity for families with minor children in Italy has increased significantly".
Italy has provided guarantees for the reception and accommodation of families.
Even the European Court of Human Rights (ECtHR) considers these commitments to be sufficient.
Other EU states would also send refugees there under the Dublin agreement.
Ulla Jelpke, spokesperson of the left-wing political group in the Bundestag, criticized this decision: "Families with children threaten due to the shortcomings in the Italian asylum system human rights violation and an endangerement of the child's welfare".
Second-best result achieved
A total of 220 liters of blood from people living in Oberreichenbach, the blood donation service already made available for other people.
At least from the twelve citizens who were honored on Friday.
Above all Jürgen Greule, who now shared 50 liters of blood with his now 100 donations.
Traditionally, at the meeting of the local council, the congregation pays tribute to the multiple readiness to donate blood.
"It is brilliant how our bodies are laid out for it and we pay respect to the donors, who provide their blood for strangers, even more unselfishly," emphasized Mayor Karlheinz Kistner.
"A single blood donation helps several people," explained Holger Maisenbacher, head of readiness for readiness of the German Red Cross (DRK), because the preserves are decomposed into their components.
So even three people could benefit from a blood donation.
At the same time, Maisenbacher reported on the actions, which will be carried out in Oberreichenbach in the spring.
"This year we achieved the second best result with 114 donors," the DRC district readiness manager said.
Obviously, the move to the Theaterhaus a few years ago led to higher numbers of participants.
Thomas Bockstadt, Susanne Reichle, Sandra Schöck, Thomas Reichle and Meta Umbeer were among the multiple donors before that and were honored for 50 blood donations each.
Herbert Mair and Dorothee Riexinger record half as many, while Tamara Pfrommer, Petra Schleeh, Stefanie Stoll and Uwe Vetter gave ten times 500 milliliters each from their life juice.
The Bank of England will keep interest rates at 0.25%
The Bank of England is expected to announce later that interest rates will remain unchanged after weak economic growth and weakening inflation dampened speculation of an increase.
The Bank of England has warned that economic growth will remain "sluggish" as it maintained interest rates on family incomes in the midst of intensifying pressures.
The Bank's Monetary Committee (MPC) policymakers voted 6 to 2 to keep interest rates to 0.25%, with fewer members seeking an increase this month as weak economic growth has weakened support for an increase.
In its quarterly inflation report, the bank reduced its 2017 growth forecasts to 1.7% and for 2018 to 1.6% and warned that the pressure on household incomes would continue, with inflation expected to rise to just under 3% in the autumn.
But it signalled that interest rate hikes would become necessary in the next few years to curb inflation fuelled by Brexit and she said credit costs might need to rise more than expected in financial markets.
Members also voted to withdraw part of the mammoth ecyclogy package issued a year ago.
It will call for the financing plan to provide cheaper funding for banks from next February, although it has now been said it is expected to provide 15 billion pounds more under this system - with 115 billion pound sterling.
Minutes after deciding on the interest rate, the bank said: "In MPC's central forecast, gross domestic product (GDP) remains weak in the short term, as the pressure on household real incomes continues to put a strain on consumption."
Commenting on interest rates, it reiterated that "a certain tightening of monetary policy" would be needed to curb inflation and a "slightly greater" extent than markets expect.
Markets are currently forecasting the first increase in the third quarter of next year and another in 2020.
However, the bank stressed that increases would take place "gradually" and "limited."
The bank's downwardly revised growth forecasts for this year and next year represent 1.9% and 1.7%, respectively.
It has maintained its growth forecast to 1.8% in 2018.
The pound fell against the dollar and the euro after these news.
The pound fell 0.5% to US$1.31 and fell 0.4% to 1.11 euros.
The decision comes to no change after the recent disappointing growth figures dampened rising expectations for an interest rate hike and GDP rose from 0.2% in the previous three months to 0.3%.
Growth is expected to be 0.3% in the third quarter, although it is expected to recover slightly after the bank towards the end of the year.
The latest inflation report offered little enthusiasm for households affected by rising inflations and small pay rises, as tightening gets even worse first before it then improves.
She added that monetary policy cannot "prevent" the income slump over the next few years, but she added that wages at the end of their three-year forecast will recover "significantly."
The economy is also boosted by rising demand for British goods, which is due to the weak pound, which will offset a portion of lower consumer spending.
The decision comes one year after lowering interest rates to 0.25% last August following the shocking EU vote causing the pound to fall.
The bank estimates it will probably take four years for this to fully reach prices and the economy.
Prior to the bank's final verdict on interest rates, the call for an interest rate hike had risen as inflation boosted pressure on hard-heaved households, with three policymakers calling for an increase to 0.5% in July.
Inflation has fallen to 2.6% last month from 2.9% in June, although the bank said this was expected and will tighten again in the coming months.
The bank said the overrun in relation to its target of 2% was "entirely" attributable to the weak pound.
Andrew Montlake, director at Coreco Mortgage Brokers, said: "Given the recent decline in swap rates, the mortgage market is likely to become more competitive again, as lenders loudly look for deals and want to end the year positively.
The market looks particularly good for those where the existing deals expire and take out the new mortgages, and many with royalty-free options and low installments are becoming increasingly popular."
NHS (The National Health Service) to prescribe controversial HIV drug 10,000 people
Patients will be given access to "transformative" HVIB prevention treatment from September after a fight before the high court against funding the drug in the NHS.
NHS England announced that pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) taken before intercourse, which can reduce the risk of infection in high-risk patients by around 86 per cent, will now be delivered by the health service over a first three-year study for an estimated 10,000 people.
He agreed to fund the trial after the appeals court upheld a Supreme Court ruling last year that the NHS had the power to pay for the PrEP despite claiming that local authorities should fund it.
The move, announced on Thursday, has been welcomed by NHS England boss Simon Stevens as an "important new intervention," while the National Aids Trust (NAT) described this as a "decisive moment."
Sexual health clinics in London, Brighton, Manchester, Liverpool and Sheffield will be among the first to allocate registration of people, and more clinics will follow in October, before it will be introduced across England in April 2018.
Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) is a consequence of HIV supplements taken before sex to reduce the risk of HIV infection.
Studies have shown that PrEP significantly reduces HIV transmission in those at risk of infection.
Taking PrEP before sexual intercourse means that there is just enough medication in a person’s body to prevent HIV infection.
Such medications can be taken every day or just two hours before sexual intercourse.
Those eligible for treatment include gay or bisexual men, transsexuals, and individuals with HIV-positive partners who have not been treated successfully.
In clinics, appropriate participants are identified who agree with the study, including men, women, transgenders, and individuals who have a partner whose HIV status is not known to be controlled by antiretroviral treatment.
People who live in England and are registered with a GP may also sign up for a possible participation in their local participating sex clinic.
The programme will be £10 million and NHS England signed a contract to supply the drugs this week following international competitive procurement.
The study is intended to assess PrEP’s full potential by collecting clinical evidence on a large-scale approach, incorporating and implementation.
Stevens said, "This major new intervention should complement and overake wide-ranging and increasingly successful efforts to prevent HIV.
It's another milestone in the more than three-decade-long progress in addressing one of humanity's greatest health challenges."
Deborah Gold, chief executive at NAT (National Aids Trust), said: "This is a pivotal moment in the fight.
PrEP, if it is specifically geared towards people in need and at risk, provides an opportunity to change the English HIV epidemic.
From September, people with a high risk of HIV will have access to this NHS-funded attempt in England to develop a new tool that is genuinely individually controlled and not subject to negotiation with a partner, leading to the improvement of many, many lives.
We sincerely welcome this announcement."
Ian Green, chief executive of the Terrence Higgins Trust, said: "The priority must now be to ensure that the study is carried out quickly across the country and that no one who has a risk of HIV will be left behind.
Now that PrEP testing medicine has been procured, we are on track to protect more than 10,000 HIV-infected people."
Sharon Hodgson, shadow health minister, said: "The start of the PrEP process is welcome and overdue after months of delays and hesitant implementation by the government.
The evidence shows how transformative this drug can be as part of our approach to HIV prevention and can end the transmission of this life-changing infection.
This attempt brings us one step closer to understanding the benefits of PrEP.
Now it is important that this study is carried out across the country as soon as possible to protect individuals who are HIV-infected, and that it will contribute to getting one step closer to ending the spread of HIV in society.
Living big life: Mezzanine floors and clever designer tricks have transformed this little Earls Court apartment into a generous home
Joanne Leigh, a former banker with a passion for real estate, downsized from a large house in Knightsbird to an Earls Court apartment in 2014.
It's on the first floor of an elegant Queen Anne terrace - and it's proof of the new design that Joanne hasn't packed and sold her bags.
"Before that, I lived with my ex in Knightsbridge in a much larger space," Joanne says.
"I've been looking for something comparable to my price.
I was shown this apartment in Earl's court, and it had the same high ceilings.
I fell in love with her and made an offer that day.”
Since the first visit, the 1,000-square-foot apartment has been completely overhauled.
Originally a one-bedroom property with a nested layout - you had to walk through the kitchen to get into the bedroom - Joanne wanted to add storage space and a mezzanine floor to make optimal use of the generous ceiling height.
"I wanted high ceilings, big windows, lots of light.
That was my requirement," she says.
For this purpose, the architecture and design studio Duck & Shed of Erfan Azadi from Notting Hill instructed her to take advantage of the 12.5-foot-high ceilings and create a home that was not only a development but also a place to live for Joanne.
The ceiling was not large enough to place two rooms on top of each other, so the architectural challenge was to create a mezzanine where you could stand.
Erfan had to think creatively in order to solve the requirement.
"The steel plant is quite complicated," he explains.
"We had to arrange the rooms in such a way that they were stacked on top of each other without us having to bend over.
It wasn’t just about building a simple platform, it was about putting on the steel in such a way that it had the right height for the top or bottom function.”
This arrangement enabled Erfan to place two rooms in full height on top of each other - with plenty of additional drama.
For the mezzanine floor, a classic copper light window from Retrouvius looks down the glamorous living area.
Mirrored gold furniture, silver accents and Vintage Serge Mouille lighting set the style for the program.
The use of mirrors plays a role in adding theatre, space and light.
At both ends, a short corridor is mirrored to make it look longer.
In the kitchen, the reflection feels like a window into another room.
At the entret, mirrored units offer space for all candy and jars from Joanne
By perceiving a more glamorous space, the beauty of the living room can close the kitchen when not in use.
Collapsible kitchens are nothing new.
What's special about this is that it's a garage door.
"A bit of engineering went in there," explains Erfan of the steel-reinforced, veneered MDF door.
"It's counterweight, so when you push up the door, it glides very easily."
Thanks to the clever use of space and storage space, Joanne was able to win an additional 16 m2, an additional bedroom and an improved layout.
"At the end of the day, you'll sell the floor space - or how your property is valued," Erfan says.
"In a rudimentary form, a real estate broker will enter and perform a calculation based on soil size."
Here they have managed to enlarge the floor area and at the same time use high-quality materials that give the room its luxurious advantage.
"The materials are the things that touch you and that therefore help create an emotional attachment in a home," Erfan says.
"You don't fall in love with the electrical outlets in the walls.
These are important, but they don’t play with your heart.”
It shows that an apartment can turn into something quite amazing due to good design and increased square footage of something standard.
In fact, like Joanne, you could choose not to leave those.
Brothers struggle because of a US$500 million real estate empire
Two brothers are four decades after their Italian family founded the dynasty, in an altercation over one of Victoria's largest real estate empires worth over 500 million dollars.
Former director of 'Carlton football club' and developer Colin De Lutis lives in a mansion in Toorak, just three doors away from his younger brother Paul. The latter has taken him to the Supreme Court to clarify how her property and business assets are to be divided.
Paul wanted to withdraw from the family business since 2014, but a keen dispute over the separation takes place in the Supreme Court.
Together with her mother Anna, the brothers own 38 properties worth more than half a billion dollars, accumulated since the family bought a business on Smith Street, Collingwood, in 1973.
That's where the Westco Jeans family founded and grew into a chain of 160 retail stores.
It was sold for US$85 million in 1999.
This money has been invested in real estate, and De Lutis' portfolio now includes offices in the city cenrum, the development areas of Brunswick and Sorrento, the apartments in Gold Coast and Mt Buller, as well as shopping malls in the suburbs.
According to an agreement with her late father, Colin - described by Paul as "the main operator and the dominating personality" in the De Lutis family - owns about two-thirds of corporate assets and Paul about a third.
A court file states that this means that Colin owns property worth 316 million dollars and Paul worth 176 million dollars.
The distribution of real estate and cash has proved to be a problem point for Paul.
"The divisions proposed by Colin left him with what I thought was the best objects," he wrote in a first Affidavit in 2015.
Last Friday, after several failed mediations, the first hearing of the Supreme Court took place.
The brothers are now also battling for control of the existing business - of which Colin, as Paul claims, has ruled him out.
Colin argues that this is not true but accuses Paul of incompetently managing some possessions.
In an early affidavit submitted by Colin, he says he hopes to settle the dispute.
"My Italian heir prescribes that the family is the most important priority," he wrote.
Costs are rising in the case, with hundreds of pages with affidavits, emails and reports from companies, including Deloitte, Pitcher Partners and Charter Keck Cramer, and with leading lawyers, including Allan Myers, QC, and law firms held by both sides.
Miss De Lutis and her late husband Luigi were Italian immigrants who ran milk bars and delicacies shops from the late 1950's and bought a shop in Brunswick in the early seventies.
They used security to buy the Collingwood property where Colin opened the family's first denim shop.
Colin says he began Westco there when he "was high school in my final years, when Paul was still a young child (about 10 years old)."
Anna De Lutis says she and Luigi, who died in 2012, hoped to amicabily pass on their property to their sons.
"Luigi often told me that he never wanted the brothers to end up in court," she wrote.
Two other brothers, Mark and Luke, are no longer part of De Luti's group of companies, with Colin claiming that Mark was paid 10 million dollars and Luke 26 million dollars when leaving the company.
Both Lukas and Mark, Paul says, "have been embittered by the terms of their financial separation."
Colin has held back a lot even though he was a former AFL board member and, according to a 2015 biography from Eddie McGuire, the TV star's best friend.
Fairfax Media contacted Colin and Paul De Lutis and their lawyers.
Only Colin responded via text message to say "I really can't say much" because the case is in the courts.
"It's very, very sad that my brother Paul has decided to sue me and pull out our family name through the courts," he wrote.
In mid-2016, Colin wrote in a letter to the court that he had tried to meet his brother's demands.
"Our father wouldn't have wanted Paul and me to end up in our current quarrel," he said, adding that he "still hopes for a solution."
On Friday, that hope seemed to be over.
"Our affair will be resolved by the courts in the near future," Colin wrote.
Trump's new chief of staff plans to curtail the president's media abstinence.
Others have tried and failed.
Maybe John F. Kelly can actually do it.
If so, he'll be the first.
Politico reports that the new White House chief of staff plans to limit the flow of information on President Trump - including the news media reports - hoping to keep the boss on a more even course.
Here's a piece by reporter Josh Dawsey:
"When John F. Kelly, White House's new chief of staff, met with senior executives on his first day of work, he posed a major problem in President Donald Trump's White House, which he wanted to fix: bad information falling into the president's hands.
Kelly told employees that information about him should flow - whether on paper or in briefings - because the president would make better decisions if he received good information."
Kelly's diagnosis makes sense, but others have tried to tame Trump by monitoring his media diet.
President Trump's relationship with television goes back decades - and now that he's in the White House, his televisal advertising is still strong.
"If nominee Trump was annoyed because of unfair reporting, it was prolific to show him that he received fair reporting from jobs that were to be persuaded," former campaign adviser Sam Nunberg told Politico in February.
Politico's Tara Palmeri wrote at the time that "the key of keeping Trump's Twitter habit under control, according to six former campaign officials, is to make sure his personal media consumption includes steady praise."
All right.
But the idea that Trump's Twitter habit has ever been "under control" is ridiculous.
Perhaps these campaign officials know something we don't don't know to others - that Trump's tweets would have contained even more igniter without their interventions.
We'll probably never find out about the tweets Trump hasn't sent.
If his collaborators have managed to keep trouble away from him a few times, then their efforts have been worthwhile.
But no one has been able to consistently prevent Trump from causing controversy.
Part of the problem is that the people in a White House made up of competing parties continue to try to advance their own agenda by presenting Trump material that potentially promotes their views from the world.
Politico - all about this history - reported in May about the adviser's penchant for providing doubted information to the President for strategic reasons.
This was an example described by reporter Shane Goldmacher:
"Current and former Trump officials say that Trump, like a volcano, can react to negative press excerpts, especially those with damaging leaks, and that he's stiffing to find out where these came from.
That's exactly what happened at the end of February, when someone maliciously gave the president a printed copy of an article by GotNews.com, the website of internet provocateur Charles C. Johnson, who accused deputy chief of staff Katie Walsh of being "the source behind a pile of leaks" at the White House.
Even though Johnson was permanently banned from Twitter because of harassment, or that he has offered no concrete evidence or that he has made false accusations in the past and revoked them.
Trump read the article and began questioning staff about Walsh."
Goldmacher added that then chief of staff Reince Priebus and White House secretary secretary Rob Porter "tried to implement a system to manage and document the paperwork Trump is receiving."
How did that work out?
Kelly tries to do the same thing three months later.
Maybe he's going to prove himself a more effective manager than Priebus, but Trump is still his impulsive self, and his advisers are still striving for influence.
These unchanging factors make Kelly's mission very difficult.
Most wanted criminals get mail from Europol
Since the address is unknown, the help of the population is requested and hoped for.
Police across Europe is sending (virtual) postcards to dangerous criminals on the run this summer.
Since the addresses of the wanted serious criminals are naturally not known, hints from the population are hoped for and requested.
Europol reported on Friday of the action, which follows the "EU Most Wanted" advent calendar from winter.
Experience had shown that many fugitives are hiding from holiday destinations, it was said on the part of Europol.
21 postcards have been compiled for sought-after criminals from 21 EU countries.
Since 2016 there is the website www.eumostwanted, more than 2.5 million times it has been viewed.
36 of the online-sought-and-seekers have been arrested so far, at least eleven of them due to clues after publication on the website.
Travelers negotiate a ditch and return to 'millionaire series'
Some of the wealthiest homeowners of the West Midlands are once again with some determined travelers on war foot who have negotiated a trench drawn by the community to reclaim a field in front of their mansions.
Travellers have parked their 20 caravans on a field outside a prosperous Strait of Solihull called the "millionaires series."
They have brought in horses, babies and an army of vans after being kicked out only in May, but without being deterred by a ditch excavated by the council to prevent their incursions.
The tactful group found a gap in the defenses and pushed their vehicles through a section left unobstructed to find a way.
Angry residents have afflicted Solihull's advice, who said it was "useless to deal with the issue."
An anonymous resident said: "They moved there on Monday night after being sent on by Bentley Heath, where they threw stones at passers-by.
The Council must be forced into action by shame because it was useless when dealing with the problem.
The travelers were there in May, were moved on and are now back again.
More arrived last night, probably even more today.”
She added: "The Council of Birmingham knows how to deal with it by holding long instruction to protect their parks.
“I’m living on Streetsbrook Road and I’m not happy to continue paying my community tax if the council can’t even stop that.”
A local resident who didn't want to be named said: "It's ridiculous, we've only just seen the last lap of these travellers just to be plundered by another round.
The numbers have increased this time and you have even seen a horse in the parking area."
Solihull's Council spokeswoman said: "We are aware of an unauthorised camp in the countryside beyond Streetsbrook Road and are working with Solihull's police. We are taking the necessary legal action so that they move on as soon as possible.
In order to support our collection of evidence, it is very important that as many residents as possible report anti-social or criminal behavior to the police by calling 101.
This contributes to creating a picture of the situation in which the court must consider when to present our case."
Birkenhof becomes Waldhotel Luise
The former Hotel Birkenhof becomes the Waldhotel Luise.
The house at the gates of Freudenstadts is currently being renovated and is due to reopen on 1 March next year.
That's good news for the city.
Because with the closure of the Hotel Birkenhof by the then Ambiente Betriebsgesellschaft mbH as of 13 April 2014, around 30 000 overnight stays per year were lost at that time and also income from the tourist tax.
In the fall of 2014, in view of dramatically increasing numbers of refugees who came to the district, the hotel building was offered to the district office as a collective accommodation, which caused controversial discussions.
The city prevented the accommodation of asylum seekers by drawing up a development plan in order to secure the area as a hotel location.
The decision was not uncontroversial in the local council at the time.
But Freudenstadt’s mayor Julian Osswald in particular defended the hotel location and already spoke of serious interested parties in the house at that time.
He's supposed to be right.
The group Luisenhotels with managing director Birgit Scherer, based in Bad Bergzabern, has acquired the former Hotel Birkenhof at a voluntary auction in Berlin and wants to make it back into a three-star-plus-category house under Waldhotel Luise.
The group has four houses with the Freudenstadt object.
Two of them in Bad Bergzabern and one in Bad Herrenalb.
They are called Hotel Luise, Luisenpark, Parkhotel Luise and now Waldhotel Luise.
At the former Hotel Birkenhof there have been building debris containers in front of the entrance for a long time, and entering the house it becomes clear what extensive renovation work will be carried out.
The floors were ripped out, blankets partially dismantled, old bathtubs removed from the rooms.
The whole hotel building is a single construction site
There's still a lot of work to be done by March.
"It's actually a nice hotel," Thomas Scherer says in an interview with our newspaper.
Everything should be renewed in Black Forest style.
The rooms are equipped with large walk-in showers, the entrance area will be redesigned, new windows will be installed.
Everything should later become "nice close to citizens", as Thomas Scherer expresses himself.
"We want to make a piece of jewelry out of it."
Swimming pool, wellness area and a meeting room are part of features.
You don’t want to position yourself on the high level of hotels in Lauterbad, but already at an upscale downtown level, Scherer emphasizes.
The Waldhotel Luise with its approximately 60 rooms wants to position itself wide, Scherer explains further.
Both individual holidaymakers, groups or businessmen should feel comfortable in the hotel.
He has already held talks with OB Julian Osswald and tourism director Michael Krause, according to Scherer.
The ideas of the city are congruent with those of the hotel group.
"We're on the same line."
When asked about the amount of investments, Thomas Scherer does not want to commit himself exactly.
Just under one million euros, he reckons.
The outdoor facilities are also brought to the fore, which is already clearly visible.
In a courtyard there is a large terrace, which will be accessible from the restaurant, which is public.
Thomas Scherer is optimistic: "Freudenstadt is a good market".
Russia affair: U.S
In the Russia affair surrounding U.S. President Donald Trump, Special Counsel Robert Mueller is said to have used a so-called grand jury, according to a report.
The panel has commenced work in recent weeks, the Wall Street Journal wrote, citing people familiar with the matter.
The newspaper assessed the move as a sign that Mueller had expanded and intensified his investigation.
CNN reported that there were already initial subpoenas by the panel in connection with a meeting between Trump's eldest son and a Russian lawyer.
U.S. intelligence agencies have long accused the Kremlin of specifically interfering in the 2016 presidential campaign.
Mueller investigates whether there were collusions between Trump's campaign team and Moscow.
The investigation should focus on several men who acted as advisers to the Republican during the election campaign.
Mueller also reportedly scrutinized more and more Trump's private and business environment.
The Washington Post wrote several weeks ago that the special prosecutor had personally put Trump in the center and examined the allegation of whether the president possibly obstructed the judiciary in the course of the affair.
The main task of grand juries is to examine existing evidence in possible crime cases and to decide whether to bring charges.
Grand juries are therefore also called indictment chambers.
They are usually switched on if they are major and controversial cases.
The procedure always takes place behind closed doors.
The panel can also be involved by the public prosecutor's office in order to subpoena people for interrogation under oath.
According to CNN, subpoenas have already been issued that revolve around the encounter between Donald Trump Jr. and a Russian lawyer.
People involved in the meeting had been asked to make a statement, the report said.
In addition, they should provide documents.
In addition to Trump Jr., Trump's adviser and son-in-law Jared Kushner as well as his then campaign chief Paul Manafort were also involved in the conversation.
Legal experts said on U.S. television on Thursday night that the establishment shows that Mueller is not intimidated in his work and that if necessary, he wants to act quickly.
However, it does not necessarily have to mean that the grand jury will be involved at the end in order to decide on raising an indictment.
Nevertheless, the establishment of the panel is the beginning of a new phase in the investigations and a new cause for concern for President Trump.
Amid the new revelations, the president used a speech in West Virginia on Thursday night to portray the Russia affair as a campaign by his political opponents.
"Russia history is a total invention," Trump said.
“There’s a possibility for Democrats to feel good because they don’t have anything else they can talk about.”
His special adviser Ty Cobb stated in a statement that he knew nothing about the appointment of a grand jury.
However, this also usually works in secret, he emphasized.
The White House advocates any measures that hastened the work of the special prosecutor in a "fair" way.
Former FBI chief James Comey has said three times that the president will not be investigated.
There was no reason to assume that this had changed, the statement said.
Mueller, however, had only been deployed after Trump fired Comey.
Because of this ejection, accusations against Trump that he had wanted to obstruct the judiciary.
Comey testified before a Senate committee in June that Trump had asked him for a cessation of investigations against then-national security adviser Michael Flynn.
The president is under a lot of pressure because of the investigation.
According to U.S. mediaries, the White House collected material against the special prosecutor and his associates.
That sparked speculation about whether Trump wanted Mueller fired.
Unlike an independent congressional special prosecutor, such as the Watergate affair surrounding later resigned President Richard Nixon, Mueller has a position in which he could theoretically be fired at the insistence of the president by the Department of Justice.
Several senators from both parties want to prevent such a move.
Two groups of Republican and Democratic senators presented bills on Thursday, according to which the special prosecutor could no longer be discharged so easily.
Antarctica: Gigantic iceberg floats in Antarctica
In West Antarctica, a gigantic iceberg has detached itself from the ice shelf from almost seven times the size of Berlin.
This was announced by the Bremerhaven Alfred Wegener Institute for Polar and Marine Research on Wednesday.
The colossus, weighing more than one trillion tonnes and 5800 square kilometres, is now moving north and will probably take two or three years until it has melted.
With a length of 175 kilometers and a width of up to 50 kilometers, it is one of the largest ice giants that researchers have registered in the past three decades.
A danger to people does not emanate from him.
According to researchers, the extent to which climate change has played a role in demolition of the iceberg is unclear.
Overall, however, there has been a series of unusual collapses of ice shelves in the region for around 30 years, writes the U.S. Research Center for Snow and Ice (NSIDC) in a factual background.
According to many researchers, this could be related to global warming.
The colossus, which has now been aborted, has so far been part of the Larsen-C ice cream.
Shelf ice swims on the sea and is fed by large glaciers.
It thus grows further and further into the sea and breaks off regularly.
Larsen-C Shelfish is located south of South America almost at the top of the Antarctic Peninsula.
North of this ice shelf, the balance of waxing and aborting was disturbed a few years ago.
A complete shelf ice has disintegrated there, writes AWI researcher Daniela Jansen.
There is a lot to suggest that the Larsen-C ice shelf is also in danger, because the front edge has never withdrawn as far as currently.
In addition, the ice slab is under tension and can disintegrate if a piece breaks at a key point.
The melting of ice shelf and ice berg does not increase sea levels because they float on the water and ice displaces as much water as it results after melting itself.
However, the loss of the ice shelf causes the glaciers to flow directly into the sea and drain away much faster than before.
This could possibly raise the sea level, but only to a small extent, writes the British research project for observation of Antarctica (Midas).
According to AWI data, the Larsen-C ice shelf is the fourth-largest ice shelf in Antarctica with almost 50 000 square kilometres.
At the transition from the mainland it is up to 700 meters thick, at the front edge to the free ocean about 200 meters.
That's where it breaks off regularly.
"Although it is a natural event and we are not aware of any direct connection to climate change, the ice sheet is now coming into a more vulnerable position," said Martin O'Leary of Britain's Swansea University.
"This is the furthest withdrawal since records began," he also confirmed.
Among scientists, it is controversial whether the Larsen-C ice shelf will now grow again or decline in the long term and possibly collapse, his colleague Adrian Luckman said, adding: "Our models say it will be less stable."
But this development will take decades.
Even if these researchers do not attribute the demolition of this gigantic iceberg to climate change, in the past decades a large number of ice shelves of the Antarctic Peninsula have disintegrated.
This long tip of Antarctica stretches towards South America.
According to data from the British Antarctic Survey research programme, the ice surface of the Antarctic Peninsula has declined by 28 000 square kilometres since the 1950s.
Temperature has contributed to the decline.
Over the past 50 years, the Antarctic Peninsula has warmed by around three degrees, making it one of the areas in which the temperature rises fastest.
CDU wants domestic tariff on staff
Because it is becoming increasingly difficult to fill vacancies, the CDU is calling to withdraw from the employers' association in order to become more attractive.
Only shortly before the summer break, the district council waved through 41 new positions in the administration.
More than 1100 employees and civil servants work for the district administration in Mettmann.
Now another 17 apprentices have their first working day behind them.
The apprenticeships in the circle are obviously highly sought-after.
Around 620 applications were received for the places to be allocated this year.
Nevertheless, the circle still lacks personnel in order to cope with the increased requirements and tasks.
There are still 50 unfilled positions at the Mettmann district.
Among other things, doctors and engineers as well as forces of the general administration are wanted.
The CDU now wants to take the initiative together with the FDP and the UWG in the district parliament and counter the shortage of skilled workers in the administration.
According to the wishes of the parties, the administration should "develop a concept for personnel recruitment and personnel development".
The aim is not only to meet short-term needs for skilled workers, but to counteract applicant shortages in a future-oriented manner, it says.
The overall concept should orient itself internally and externally, identify concrete measures for lateral entrants and include a training offensive, the CDU, FDP and UWG demand.
This sounds like a good approach.
Because the circle is dealing with personnel problems that you didn’t know before.
Other cities recruit good civil servants and employees and offer more attractive conditions.
We are talking about a veritable "competition of public employers".
In addition, there are a high number of employees who leave the service due to illness or age.
It’s time to act, so that even more jobs don’t go vacant.
But Klaus-Dieter Völker, parliamentary group leader of the CDU, goes beyond the joint motion with his party.
He recently caused a fist-thick surprise in the district parliament, when he publicly questioned the personnel costing and amazed the other parties.
Peoples would like all possibilities to be reviewed, which allow an exit from the municipal employers' association.
"If we do not come to a satisfactory solution here in the short term, the district administration with the unions - perhaps also in cooperation with the district-affiliated cities - should aim for a domestic tariff," Völker said.
In doing so, the CDU is trying to pay the employees and civil servants better than it actually envisages the tariff regulations for the public service.
Salaries are currently precisely prescribed, increases or more money is only available if you switch to a higher stage based on professional years.
But the tasks of the circle have grown more and more in recent years.
Just a few weeks ago, new rooms had to be set up in which employees take care of the provisions of the new Prostitute Protection Act.
Not least due to the increasing number of asylum seekers, District Administrator Thomas Hendele and district director Martin Richter have a problem: they have too few staff.
Italy tightens tone on NGO migrant rescue boats
Italy has warned NGOs using lifeboats for migrants in the Mediterranean that they will not be allowed to continue this if they do not abide by new rules for their operations.
"If NGOs don't sign a new code of conduct, it's difficult to see how they can keep working," Interior Minister Marco Minniti said in an interview with Turin's La Stampa newspaper.
Minniti's warning came a day after Italian authorities seized a boat from the German aid organization 'Jugend Rettet' because their squad had effectively cooperated with traffickers in a way that facilitated illegal immigration.
The aid agency, which has only been in operation for a year, declined to comment on detailed allegations by Italian prosecutor Ambrogio Cartosio.
"For us, saving human life is and remains our top priority, so we are very sorry that we can't operate in the search and rescue zone at the moment," the organization said in a statement on social media.
"We can only assess all the accusations that are being made after we have gathered all the information and can assess the situation."
Italian authorities had been overseeing 'Youth Rettet', Iuventa since October.
It is suspected that his crew will take inflatable migrants on board who will be delivered to them directly by traffickers, and that the smugglers can depart so that the ships can be used again.
At least such a meeting allegedly took place just 1.3 miles off the Libyan coast, according to the prosecutor's file, the contents of which were published by Italian media.
The crew is suspected of disregarding the authority of the Italian coast guard, which monitors rescue operations in the zone for humanitarian reasons rather than any other motives.
Under the code of conduct, boats such as Iuventa must mainly have an Italian police officer on board to monitor their activities.
Only three of the nine NGOs involved in search and rescue missions have so far agreed to adhere to the code: Save the Children, MOAS from Malta and Pro-Activa Open Arms from Spain.
The latter said on Thursday that the new rules would be seen as unnecessary, but acceptable, as they would not entail "essential changes or obstructions" of their bailouts.
Among those who refuse to sign is the Nobel laureating organization Doctors Without Borders (MSF).
Minniti, meanwhile, said that the number of migrants who arrived in Italy after the bailout operations had declined sharply in recent weeks, suggesting that efforts to strengthen the Libyan coast guard and cooperation with local mayors had borne fruit.
"In the last few days we've begun to see light at the end of the tunnel," the minister said.
He announced plans for further talks with Libyan mayors this month on economic development initiatives and with Chad, Niger and Mali on measures to reduce the number of migrants these countries leave in the hopes of being able to reach Europe.
Ascensions and Trials
Nine Weilermer bikers, the "Berlin outpost" and two guest riders met in Wachenheim in the North Palatinate to enjoy trials and sights for four days.
There were three friendly guides available.
The accommodation in Wachenheimer Valley proved to be a good starting point for the tours.
The castle and wine festure of Wachenheim was a welcome extra.
The first tour led in hot summer temperatures along the vineyards to the Michaelskapelle above Deidesheim.
This was followed by viewpoints, partly on towers, from which you could look far over the Palatinate forest and the upstream plains.
In between, there have always been longer climbs, for which long trials rewarded.
On the second day we went to Bad Dürkheim to the saline and the Volksfest Wurstmarkt.
Once again, viewpoint lined up with viewpoint, connected by "floppy" trials and moderate climbs.
On the last two days there were again many meters of altitude.
Caves, rocks, towers, castles, ruins and refreshment huts brought welcome breaks.
In Bad Dürkheim, the bikers breathed healing brines into the open-air inhalatorium.
Neustadt was also approached.
After 173 kilometers and almost 4500 meters of altitude, the group had got to know the Palatinate Forest at its best - sportingly and culturally.
Console instead of turf - Bundesliga gears up for e-sport
In the future, will the sports stars sit in front of computer consoles instead of fighting over the stadium turf in wind and weather?
E-sport has long since become a serious industry, a lucrative business - big stars, millions of fans and huge tournaments with high fees included.
This has also been recognized by the first Bundesliga clubs, which are increasingly involved in this area.
VfL Wolfsburg started in 2015, followed by FC Schalke 04 and most recently VfB Stuttgart, who founded an e-sports department in mid-July and signed two professionals for the FIFA 17 computer game.
"We hope to inspire completely new young target groups for VfB," says board member Jochen Röttgermann.
He is sure "that several clubs are still ascending on this train".
Next weekend, the world football federation FIFA is organising the first "Interactive Club World Cup" in London, in which the gamers of various clubs compete against each other.
In addition to Schalke and Wolfsburg, Manchester City, Paris Saint-Germain or PSV Eindhoven have qualified.
Robin Dutt also sees great potential in e-sports.
The former coach of Bayer Leverkusen and Werder Bremen, meanwhile, advises an agency for virtual football players.
"Real football in particular has to deal with the 14 to 30-year-olds that the young people will still go to the stadium in the future," says the 52-year-old.
And: The clubs tried to conquer all sorts of markets, especially the Asian ones.
With prominent e-athletes under contract, one could be present in these markets much more often online than only once a year to send the Bundesliga team to Asia.
Exactly what is it about?
In e-sports, computer games such as "League Of Legends", "Dota 2", "Counterstrike" or the football simulation FIFA are contested at the competition level.
As in normal sport, leagues have long established themselves in which the professionals compete against each other.
And at the Asian Games 2022, e-sport is already part of the official program.
Is it even a real sport?
For many, the notion certainly takes some getting used to.
Ingo Froböse, too, has dealt with the question for a long time.
For the professor at the German Sport University in Cologne, e-sport is a sport because it is competitive and shows many biological and physical reactions, which can also be found in classical sports - for example, increased heart rates, stress responses, hormonal changes.
In addition, it requires cognitive abilities, a technical and tactical understanding and rapid responsiveness.
Professional gamers should train not only at the console, but also their bodies and pay attention to the diet.
"The fit player is a good player."
Froböse, however, also sees problems: "Doping is becoming more and more of an issue".
Checks are difficult to carry out in online games, and in the scene the structures of the classic sport were missing.
The scientist emphasizes the high integrative effect of e-sport positively.
The players are traveling internationally, this is like a large global family, there are no intercultural barriers.
A study by the management consultancy Deloitte and the Federal Association of Interactive Entertainment Software (BIU) predicts rapid growth for e-sports.
In 2016, 50 million euros were implemented in Germany, mainly through advertising, sponsorship and major tournaments.
In three years, sales are expected to be 130 million.
Against the almost 2.4 billion euros of the football league, this is little.
However, e-sports are "no longer far from the other established sports, such as handball, basketball or hockey," Deloitte advisers write.
Internationally, many countries are already further along.
Large markets are Asia and the U.S., where ex-basketbal player Shaquille O`Neal and baseball star Alex Rodriguez participate in U.S. Team NRG eSports.
Hollywood actor Ashton Kutcher invests in eSports betting provider Unikrn.
There is a lot going on in Europe, too.
In a Norwegian school, e-sports have been introduced as a teaching subject, says Froböse.
In the Netherlands and France, according to Dutt, the football first division clubs are now obliged to set up an e-sports department.
"By European comparison, Germany is lagging behind," explains VfB CEO Röttgermann.
Falling into nostalgia only, and transfiguring the old times, is no longer enough today.
It is about preserving the tradition of the association, but at the same time aligning the company with the modern market.
"We live in a digitized world," Dutt said.
"You can't stick to the fact that only in football everything remains as it always was."
Poison eggs ade - The egg alternatives for cooking and baking
Almost all federal states are now affected by the scandal for poisonous eggs.
Aldi Süd and Aldi Nord have reacted and taken all eggs out of sale throughout Germany.
Only eggs are to be taken onto the shelves, which have been proven to contain no fipronil.
Michelle Carter convicted of the lyrics pushing Conrad Roy to commit suicide
A Massachusetts woman was sentenced to two-and-a-half years for encouraging her friend to commit suicide through texts and calls.
Michelle Carter, now 20, will spend 15 months of her conviction behind bars and five years have been suspended on probation.
Carter was convicted in June of negligent homicide by Conrad Roy, who took his life on July 13, 2014.
Roy's father said Thursday at the hearing: "My family is heartbroken.
My son was my best friend.”
Carter was allowed to remain vacant until the appeal.
She had been confronted with a maximum sentence of 20 years, but her legal representatives argued that both she and Mr Roy suffered from a mental illness.
Dressed in red trousers and a cream-colored blouse, Carter stood with tears in her eyes, with her pleated hands, and downward eyes when she was convicted.
"Her actions have killed Conrad Roy," the prosecutor told the court.
"She ended his life to make her own better.
She didn't take responsibility," she said.
"She didn't show remorse."
But Carter's lawyer said it was a "horrific circumstance that she completely regrets."
However, Judge Lawrence Moniz said he did not find that their "age or degree of maturity, or even their mental illness, has a significant impact on their actions."
A prosecutor read a statement from Roy's mother Lynn Roy, who said there is "not a day where I don't entrust the loss of my beloved son.
I want him to be proud of me and how to deal with everything. I try to be there for his sisters despite all the pain we will carry with us for eternity," it reads.
Roy's sister, Camdyn Roy, testified that she would be "persecuted" by the realization that she would never attend her brother's wedding or be an aunt for his children.
"No longer having that single person I've been with every day since I was born is a pain I'll always keep with me for the rest of my life," she said.
The case appears to set a legal precedent as there is no law in Massachusetts that makes it a crime to ask another to commit suicide.
Carter was 17 years old when Roy was found dead from carbon monoxide poisoning in a vehicle in 2014.
The case attracted national attention after Carter's text releases revealed she had encouraged him to end his life.
"Hang up, jump off a building, stab yourself, I don't know, there are a lot of ways," she said in several messages she'd sent in the two weeks before he died while on holiday with his family.
In the moments before his suicide, she wrote: "You have to do it, Conrad" and "All you have to do is turn on the generator and you're going to be free and happy."
Judge Moniz said Carter was seeking attention from Roy's death and ordered that she should benefit from the case by any press.
The case has shared legal opinion.
Among the few who defended Carter included Amanda Knox convicted in 2007 of murdering her British housemate in Italy before being acquitted two years ago.
Miss Knox, aged 30, posted in the Los Angeles Times a commentation: "Michelle Carter deserves compassion and help, not prison."
Joshua Kimmich: Learning from FC Bayern's new mini-lame
It is no coincidence that Joshua Kimmich was allowed to play as captain at the Audi Cup.
FC Bayern trust the Confed Cup winner a big season - extra motivation does not hurt there.
There was something different in his place in the cabin, Joshua Kimmich saw immediately, even if the extra garment was very, very small.
But since on the second day of the Audi Cup there was of course no Philipp Lahm and no Manuel Neuer could be seened, he put the binding assigned to his arm.
"The coach didn't announce it," the Confed Cup victor recounted afterwards, after his first assignment as Bayern captain.
Well, the start could have run better than at the 0:2 against the SSC Naples.
But Kimmich's sign could also interpret the sign like this.
It was not the eight-year-older Arturo Vidal had been chosen as a captain in the C-Elf consisting of youth players, debutants and just from the holiday, but him.
Zuckerl prefers to be distributed to those whom you trust a lot, who you want to keep happy.
So if you have been chosen to claim the legacy of Lahm, you can only make good use of a small extra motivation.
Kimmich confirmed: "That's what you want as a player: take responsibility."
On Wednesday, the 22-year-old flitted across the field as mini-lame.
On the back right, that's now his position, the white bandage on the arm did the rest.
And at all Kimmich seemed in this starting formation like the one who can take the rudder best in his hand.
He gave instructions, tried to steer the unrehearsed squad.
He had an eye for the whole, while many others were concerned with himself.
Vidal by overplaying his lack of fitness (hardly worked); Renato Sanches to build on the performances of the trip to Asia (only worked to a limited extent); and Sebastian Rudy and Niklas Süle not to stand out negatively in their first appearance in the Bayern jersey.
After all, that worked quite well.
Süle ran alongside Kimmich, Rudy in front of the defense, both acted confidently.
The well-known Napoli storm was often difficult, in addition, Süle also tried successfully a few times in the build-up to the game.
His colleague Rudy admitted that the result "was not the way we hoped."
But he, too, put in a decent performance on debut.
Relocation of games, tempo changes: "For this style I was also brought in".
How unfamiliar this Bayern world still is for the new additions was seen after the game.
Rudy, for example, didn't give one or two in the catacombs, but determined six or seven interviews.
Kimmich said goodbye after a few crunchy statements in the back of his new teammates.
There he heard an interesting sentence from Süle: "What's going on here, I've already noticed right now.
At Bayern you just have to win every game.”
After all, he doesn't have to teach the new ones anymore.
Alaba motivates ÖFB ladies
David Alaba believes in the success of our ÖFB ladies
Austria's best-known footballer reached out via Twitter shortly before the match and wished the ÖFB ladies all the best.
After all, the Bayern legionnaire is almost a club colleague of ÖFB captain Schnaderbeck and is thrilled with our ladies.
The tweet of the Austrian footballer of the year and Champions League winner should be a special motivational injection.
Maybe our ladies might well need the famous Bayern-Dusel.
Companies satisfied with Rhineland-Palatinate minister: Economy praises Wissing's pace
It often became irreconcilable, the relationship between the country and the regional economy, when Eveline Lemke (Greens) was still economy minister of Rhineland-Palatinate.
This is evident after more than a year in which the new Economy Minister Volker Wissing (FDP) is in office.
Chambers and companies from the region speak of a change in economic policy - and at the same time the old minister.
Frank Natus, chairman of the Trier-based companies (VTU), says: "Wissing picks up on the desires of the economy, which Lemke has all blocked".
Natus sees the biggest U-turn in infrastructure policy: The gap closure of Highway 1 between Kelberg and Adenau is now at the top of the country’s list of priorities.
This year, the ministry hopes for a planning approval procedure, by 2021 the construction law should stand.
Also playing a role in the country's plans is the western bypass of Trier, which is in the urgent need of the federal transport route plan.
The aim is to put the line into operation in December 2020, says ministry spokeswoman Cornelia Holtmann.
Natus sees this as a signpost to support regional but internationally competing companies.
The Chamber of commerce and industry (IHK) Trier strives, as suggested by Wissing, a Rhineland-Palatinate label for wine to increase sales in supermarkets and to stop the trend of German wine exports, which has slumped for ten years.
Wissing also has an open ear for people who want to start their own company, praises the IHK.
The Trier Chamber of Crafts sees the new master bonus as an additional incentive for young apprentices, says managing director Matthias Schwalbach.
A premium of 1000 euros is earmarked for the passed master examination and 2500 euros if graduates become self-employed within ten years of master training.
However, the homework list for the country remains long.
In the planned road construction, facts have to be created quickly, at the same time the country lacks the necessary personnel, says Jan Glockauer, managing director of the IHK Trier.
Out of 56 new engineers that Wissing intends to hire for mobility, only 29 have been filled so far.
The IHK calls for more contracts to be awarded to external offices.
CDU parliamentary group vice-president Christian Baldauf refers to calculations by the Court of Auditors that the country invests more than 20 million euros less in the preservation of national roads in 2017 and 2018 than proposed.
Frank Natus is also expecting a concept from the country on how it intends to remedy the shortage of skilled workers in the region.
Also sobering is the previous broadband expansion in the country.
In 2016, regional companies had rated the grid supply in an IHK survey only with a grade of 3.4.
They complained about stalling internet connections and demolitions.
Former Economy Minister Eveline Lemke, on the other hand, is currently out of politics and still sits on the supervisory board of the wind power company Pro Wind.
The Green Group left Lemke on April to become president of a private university in Karlsruhe - where she cleared the field again after four months due to discrepancies.
Audi is expected to change 850,000 diesel vehicles - automakers face backlash
A day prior to Audi's announcement, BMW said it would offer updating the software in more than 350,000 vehicles.
The BMW chairman said the company was of the opinion that "there are smarter options than driving restrictions," referring to European cities - including Munich, where BMW is based - to ban or restrict diesel vehicles.
Daimler announced on Tuesday that it would modify three million Mercedes vehicles in Europe to reduce its diesel emissions.
None of the companies described the ventures as a recall.
European automakers have strongly encouraged the use of diesel vehicles in Europe and the United States to support compliance with rules on carbon dioxide emissions.
But the nitrogen oxides emitting diesel engines are considered carcinogens and can cause asthma.
The cost for car manufacturers to install facilities to neutralize exhaust gases emitted by diesel vehicles is also increasing, which makes it difficult to keep the price of cars competitive.
German chancellor Angela Merkel is also charged with coddling the powerful car companies and ignoring signs of the problem.
The companies are trying to avoid Volkswagen's mistakes, that the use of so-called shut-off devices that could regulate emissions to comply with the regulations when a car has been tested but drive back in normal driving conditions.
In the United States, several Volkswagen employees have been indicted, while others are being investigated on both sides of the Atlantic.
Last month, the former head of thermodynamics in Audi's engine development was arrested in Germany.
Former manager Zaccheo Giovanni Pamio is Italian and is therefore not protected from extradition and could be put on trial in the United States.
Earlier this year, Audi was involved in German criminal investigations at Volkswagen after authorities accused Audi of installing a system to circumvent emissions regulations in Europe, and a request focused on the United States has been expanded.
The inclusion of Audi in the investigation could put a heavy strain on Volkswagen: The luxury car manufacturer contributes disproportionately to Volkswagen's profits.
This message the raft has on the Isar on Saturday
The city wants to protect the Isar.
So that the Isar visitors also participate, a raft drives through Munich on tomorrow Saturday, which has a special message.
If it draws to the Isar again on Saturday, you will see a large raft there between Flaucher and Reichenbachbrücke.
And that's not a common raft, with Isar tourists.
But the construction department wants to enlighten.
On the raft you can see a great information transparent.
It shows a mountain of rubbish.
The prelude to a publicity campaign to appeal to Isar visitors.
With the motto "True love is ..." the understanding for the Isar is supposed to sharpen as a wild river worthy of protection.
Already last summer, the raft drove over the Isar and this year there are further actions and measures to achieve considerate and environmentally conscious behavior.
Mordillo's tuber noses and laughter against suffering
Mordillo was at times one of the most-published cartoonists, the tuber noses of his characters are his trademark.
Even at 85, the Argentine still draws and is working on a film.
His message: Laugh, "because there's too much suffering in the world."
Guillermo Mordillo was born on August 4, 1932, the son of Spanish immigrants in Buenos Aires.
In his first years as an artist, among others, he fought his way through as a draughtsman for greeting cards - which frustrated him.
"The happiest day in my professional life was the day when lost into my job," he once said.
After stops in Lima, New York and Paris, he made his breakthrough at the end of the 1960s: with the publication of his cartoons in "Paris Match" and then also in "Stern" and other leading magazines around the world.
Even in China Mordillo found many fans.
Again and again the artist was inspired by cities.
Big, rooming Mordillo figures frolic in drawings with the Leaning Tower of Pisa, Big Ben in London and Red Square in Moscow.
In the early morning, according to his own information, he still sits at the drawing table today, working almost ten hours a day.
Through calendars, puzzles, books for big and small, T-shirts, plush toys and watches, his drolled figures continue to find a lot of distribution worldwide.
For six years he has also been working on an animated film and a TV series with his characters.
The main character in "Crazy Island" is a giraffe - Mordillo's most popular animal in his drawings - which has a centipede as an opponent.
For the first time, his characters speak in the 3D film, which is to come to the cinema screen in 2019.
"I don't know exactly what they're going to say, it's strange to me," Mordillo says.
The script is not written by him.
That's why he're more relying on the TV series, which, like his original drawings, is wordlessly produced.
Lavender from Provence threatened by climate change
He has become a symbol of Provence in the south of France, but there is a warning that he is at risk from climate change.
The lavender is declining because of drought-reminiscent conditions and insectors because of the disease-transmitting insectors, giving scientists and farmers who are trying to find solutions a major headache.
Eric Chaisse, an expert at the agricultural research institute CRIEPPAM in Provence, said: "Lavender in Provence is effectively endangered because the plant has been exposed to the effects of climate change in previous years, which brought much less rain in the spring and much more in the fall.
This has an impact on lavender's lifetime.
There's also a small cicada, a micro-bacterium that transmits dwarf cicadas, and in very dry weather, there's a lot of it.'
And there's a lot at stake.
Lavender oil not only attracts holidaymakers, it is also in high demand for perfume and cosmetics.
Eric Chaisse said: "Instead of having very long plant ears with a large number of flowers, we have smaller, underdeveloped and either missing or weakened.
So we've got fewer parts with essential oil.'
Solutions considered include better water distribution and the slowdown of evaporation from the soil, but also spraying the plants with a protective blend of clay and water and the development of varieties more resistant to drought.
The town of Geisa in Rhön in Thuringia celebrates its 1200-year anniversary this year.
The highlight is this Saturday and Sunday with a medieval festival.
Krämer, guilds, traders, vagants or a Landsknecht camp are to be experienced on these days in the city center.
The assassination of a journalist underscores the growing threat in Mexico
Employees at the Riodoce weekly newspaper usually meet on Wednesdays to review their plans for reporting on the recent chaos in Sinaloa state through organised crime, corrupt officials and incessant drug wars.
But on that day, in the shadow of their own tragedy, they came together to talk about safety.
They have been told that it is important to change their routines.
Two high-ranking journalists are discussing what feels safer: they're supposed to take their children to the office, who were the target of an attack with grenades in 2009, or leaving them at home.
Security experts have written three words on a plaque at the front of the room: enemies, neutrals, allies.
They ask reporters to suggest names for each column - no evidence is needed, perceptions and gut feelings are enough.
There are drug smugglers, politicians, businessmen, journalists suspected of being on the payroll of the government or the cartels, a catalogue of criminals making the chaos of Mexico dangerous.
There is no rest from the violence, and while the bodies are piling up across the country, more and more journalists are involved: at least 25 since President Enrique Pena Nieto took office in December 2012. 589 are under federal protection after attacks and threats and so far this year there have been at least seven deaths in seven states.
Among the latter is its editor and inspirator of Riodoce, co-founder Javier Valdez Cardenas.
"The biggest mistake is to live in Mexico and be a journalist," Valdez wrote in one of his numerous books on drug-related violence.
His absence is heartfelt, even though his presence is present everywhere - a large photo of Valdez showing his middle finger with the word "Justice" hangs on the facade of the Riodoce building. Two reporters in thirties, Aaron Ibarra and Miriam Ramirez, wear T-shirts showing his smiling face with glasses or his typical Panama hat.
The workshop takes place less than two months after his death, and the reporters are discussing their shared themes, trauma, their nightmares, insomnia, paranoia.
Although a special federal prosecutor's office was established in 2010 to deal with the cases of journalists, according to the committee for the protection of journalists, it has initiated only two criminal proceedings.
As with most thousands of murders linked to drug trafficking and organised crime every year, the killers of journalists are rarely brought to justice.
At Riodoce, they still insist on treating the violence of Sinaloa, even though they are heartbroken and even though the terrain is now even more treasonable.
Without information about the killers, without justice, the meeting to discuss security has little benefit, Ibarra says.
"It's very stupid to waste my time in this workshop," he says.
"As long as we don't know why, we're distrusting everyone."
On the morning of May 15, Valdez leaved the Riodoce newspaper's office in Culiacan's state capital.
He only managed to drive a few blocks far before his red Toyota Corolla was stopped by two men. He was forced out of his car and scored 12 times, presumably because of the name of the newspaper - which translates to "twelfth river."
The gunman drove away in his car and damaged it nearby.
Valdez was 50 years old.
He left a wife and two grown children.
Rumours tend to fly around freely in Culiacan.
But there's virtually nothing but silence on Valdez.
Since Guzman's arrest last year and extradition to the United States in January, Sinaloa had become one of the country's bloodiest battlers as rival factions fight here to fill the vacue.
It doesn't matter that in Sinaloa every day someone or several people are shot in the street or that the cemetery is full of ornate, two-storey mausoleas for drug kings larger than many homes for the living.
Forget for a minute that there is a state of "quiet" when only one cartel has control of the coastal state with its valuable ports and drug trade routes to the United States.
Nevertheless, and despite Valdez was aware of the dangers of his work, Ismail Bojorquez (60), a co-founder and director of Riodoce, is shattered with the guilt that he couldn't protect his friend.
He believes two misunderstandings contributed to the murder.
Initially, in February, there was the publication of an interview with Damaso Lopez, a leader of one of the rival antitrust factions in the war with Guzman's sons.
This publication may have annoyed the sons, suspected gang members bought up every copy of the issuance as soon as it was delivered to newspaper kiosks.
The second mistake was not forcing Valdez to leave the country for its own security after the seizure of another newspaper that had published the same story.
Valdez was a legend in Mexico and abroad, and his killing is seen as a milestone in Mexican violence against journalists.
He had survived for so long that his friends and colleagues assumed he would always be there.
He worked as an experienced reporter for Noroeste in 2003 when he merged with five colleagues to found Riodoce and sold U.S.$50 stakes.
In Sinaloa, "it was impossible to engage in journalism without encountering the drug problem," Bojorquez said.
Over time, the newspaper earned a reputation because of courageous and sincere reporting, and sales and advertising increased.
Reporters loved being able to publish high-acking research without fear of censorship, and readers were fascinated by a publication in which they could read stories that no one else dared to treat.
Eight years after Riodoce's founding, she received the prestigious "Maria Moors Cabot Prize" for her reporting in Latin America.
That same year, Valdez won the International Press Freedom Award of the Committee for the Protection of Journalists for his courage in the pursuit of Mexican drug history, wherever it led.
He voluntarily admitted that he was scared.
"I want to live on," he said at the time of the CPJ award.
Drug trafficking in Sinaloa "is a way of life," he said last October in an interview with Rompeviento-TV.
"You have to take on the task that happens to you as a journalist - either that or you're playing the dumber.
I don't want to be asked, "What did you do in the face of so many deaths ... why didn't you say what was going on?"
The Riodoce team misses Valdez, the joker who could curse like a port worker, the friend generous with hugs and advice, a teacher who knew how to survive.
They relied on his routine.
He'd always wear his hat.
He'd always go to El Guayabo, the bar across from the office, and always sit there at the same table.
Now they ask: was his love of routine his ruin?
His death has also forced them to question their own assumptions about how best to do their work and stay alive in the process.
"They don't like it when you deal with their wives, their children, their clean shops, their clandestine runways" to move drugs.
"These things weren't allowed," Bojorquez said.
The result, even in the best of times, is a high degree of self-censorship and self-preservation.
Trust his own instincts.
If it smells wrong, stay away from it.
The trouble is, the editor of Riodoce Andres Villarreal said, that "smell a sense that can be deceived ... and then the thing happened with Javier."
The old rules, as he and others say, no longer apply in Sinaloa - just as they do in Tamaulipas, Veracruz, Guerrero and other states where a poisonous mixture of lucrative smuggling routes, weak institutions and corrupt government officials is at home.
The landscape is constantly changing.
In the room where Riodoce's employees met for safety training, no mobile phones were suddenly allowed. Days earlier, it was revealed that spyware, which is sold exclusively to governments, was used to monitor journalists and activists in Mexico.
Outside, two police officers sought shelter from the heat of 104 degrees (40 °C) in the shade of a tree.
They were mandated by the state government to protect Riodoce's offices in a four-storey building in a middle-class district of Culiacan.
Half joked, some of the reporters wondered if those officers are among the 50 percent of police officers the governor himself described as untrustworthy.
It's been months since the reporters went into the mountain landscape, where drug gangs are de facto under control.
For this week's issuement, Riodoce has looked at three main stories.
There was the assassination of the brother of the former well-known boxer Julio Cesar Chavez in Sinaloa.
They also had an exposé on government spending that focused on the governor's hometown.
And there was a group that carried out a kidnapping at one of Culiacan's most expensive restaurants, a block removed from the prosecutor's office.
There was no official word about who was kidnapped or how it happened, so caution was advised when it was time to write what everyone in the city knows: that the restaurant is a favorite restaurant of drug traffickers and authorities.
A reporter learned from public records that the restaurant was registered under the name of a politician who belongs to the ruling Institutional Revolution Party (PRI), which dominated all political levels for almost the entire century.
Recently, several PRI governors have been accused of high-profile cases of corruption.
Villarreal asked the reporter to write about previous incidents at this venue, including one where a son of "El Chapo" Guzman escaped a military robbery.
Just a few months earlier, readers would have regarded Valdez's column as the best-informed source about the kidnapping.
"Earlier, we'd have already known what happened," Villarreal, 46, nicknamed "El Flaco" because of its slender body stature.
"Now all channels of communication with our sources are broken."
Valdez's office has been rededicated to the storage room for signs and stickers protesting against the murders of journalists because employees have become something they themselves never expected: activists on behalf of the press.
Reporter Miriam Ramirez snatched a few signs and went to a demonstration the next morning about another journalist to the prosecutor's office.
Salvador Adame vanished three days after Valdez was killed in the western state of Michoacan.
A burned body has been spotted and officials say, based on DNA tests, that it's his.
Across the country, journalists have become louder by writing down "SOS Press" on sidewalks and buildings in organized protests.
On this day, director Bojorquez is gone in Washington trying to gather international support for the judiciary in the Valdez case.
At a meeting with Sinaloa governor the day after Valdez's assassination, Ramirez accused the authorities of spying on journalists and having them killed for telling the truth.
She has since asked for her to be re-divided by the government because she is concerned that her anger has violated her objectivity.
Valdez had repeatedly said that journalists in Mexico were "surrounded" by organized crime, government officials involved and an indifferent society.
In his last book "Narco-Journalism," he wrote that reporters are killed not only by drug gangs, but also on the orders of politicians and security forces who were under one roof with organized crime.
The media guard group "Articulo 19" assigned more than half of the attacks on journalists to the police and officers last year.
"In Mexico, you're dying because they'd like you kept your mouth shut," Ramirez said.
Clearly, the murders have a chilling effect.
No one is forgetting the death of blogger Maria Elizabeth Macias in the northern border state of Tamaulipas six years ago.
Her body was found along with a note allegedly signed by the Zetas cartel: "Here's me because of my reports."
A computer keyboard and headphones lay next to their severed head.
Some branches have chosen to close, such as newspaper El Norte in the northern border state of Chihuahua following the assassination of correspondent Miroslava Breach in March.
Others carry on, as El Manana of Nuevo Laredo did after the assassination of her director in 2004.
In 2010, Diario de Ciudad Juarez publicly addressed the drug cartels with an editorial on the front page entitled "What do you want from us?"
Some journalists have escaped from their home countries, or even from the country.
It's a painful decision.
It's hard to find work in exile, and they're still observing the streets looking for dangers.
And sometimes they are chased, as apparently the photographer Ruben Espinosa, who was murdered in 2015 together with four women in an apartment in Mexico City three months after fleeing Veracruz.
For those who stay behind and continue the work, it's a day-to-dail dance with high-risk decisions.
Ibarra, who once wanted to be a poet, admits drug trafficking scares him.
"Mexico goes to hell, and that's why I became a reporter," he said.
At midnight last Friday, when the last issue had already ended, Riodoce's editors sat on the sidewalk in front of the office drinking beer as everyone suddenly began buzzing their phones.
A series of shootings involving gang rivals and security forces near the seaside resort of Mazatlan had left 19 confirmed dead.
The war further escalated, as promised by a number of cartel embassies in the region.
From the curb, they posted the news on Riodoce's website via mobile phone.
The front page had to be changed the next day.
Sirens howled nearby - another shootout in the area.
Bojorquez took a look at the policians who held guard to see if they were on standby.
If they were afraid at all, they didn't show it.
Under the enormous portrait of the fallen founder of the newspaper, whose middle finger was visible to the whole of Sinaloa, Riodoce's staff followed in his footsteps.
"How else can you even think of shutting down," Bojorquez said, "when on the same day Javier was murdered, the intern asked me to send her to the streets for coverage."
Dinosaurs used camouflage to dodge its enemies
At first glance, it looks like a statue, albeit a frightening one.
Now, researchers have found that the initial findings of the incredible rhino of a Canadian miners have been so well preserved that experts have described it as "Mona Lisa the dinosaurs."
It was so well preserved that they could even determine that his flaky skin was of reddish brown - and they said she had something of a "restless past."
The report in Current Biolage referred to him as "the best-preserved armored dinosaurs ever found, and one of the world's best dinosaur specimens."
The fossil is a newly discovered species of nodosaurs that lived in the midst of the Cretaceous period between 110 million and 112 million years ago.
His unbelievably well-preserved state has baffled researchers who describe it as "really remarkable"
According to reports in National Geographic, the armoured herbivore is the best surviving fossil of its kind.
It was found by Shawn Funk when he dug at the Suncor Millenium Mine near Fort McMurray in northern Alberta, Canada, on March 21, 2011.
He encountered something that seemed out of place by the surrounding rocket and decided to take a closer look.
The fossil he discovered was sent to the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Palaeontology.
They spent the next six years uncovering the wild animal within the 2,500 pound (1,100kg) earth clump.
After all this hard work, the finished result is now ready to be unveiled.
"If you're blinking just a little with your eyes, you could almost believe it's slept," said lead author Caleb Brown, a scientist at the Royal Tyrrell Museum where the creature can be seen.
"It's going to go down in the history of science as one of the most beautiful and best-preserved dinosaur specimens - the Mona Lisa of the dinosaurs."
Researchers have found that this herbivore, even though it's covered with armor and resembles a walking tank, was probably threatened by meat-eating dinosaurs.
The heavily armored dinosaur used a red and white camouflage to hide from predators, and used a shielding technique known as counter-shading, which is also used by many modern animals.
This would have allowed it better to blend into its surroundings and dodge its enemies, experts say, suggesting that it was threatened by larger dinosaurs.
The 110-million-year-old creature from the Nodosaur family made headlines for the first time in May and can now be seen at the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Palaeontology.
Researchers have now studied the wild animal and called it Borealopelta markmitchelli according to museum technician Mark Mitchell, one of a team that has spent more than 7,000 hours trying to uncover it carefully.
The astonishing preservation of the specimen allowed Dr. Mitchell and an international team of scientists to document the pattern and shape of scales and armaments about his body.
They discovered counter-shading, a common method of defense in the animal kingdom, which means that the creature's tip was darker than its underside.
Although counter-shading is common, the results are surprising, as the size of Borealopelta far exceeds that of the animals living today.
It suggests that the dinosaur has been chased by energizing dinosaurs of sufficient pressure that evolution favored obfuscating confrontation as a means of survival.
But most contemporary animals that use counter-shading - think of deer, zebras, or armists - are much smaller and more violent than prey, signaling that this nodosaur was exposed to a real struggle for surviving.
"Strong predator behavior on a massive, heavily armoured dinosaur shows how dangerous the dinosaur predators of the Cretaceous period must have been," Brown said.
The chemical analysis of organic compounds in their scales also shows that the dinosaur's skin was reddish brown.
Scientists continue to examine the animal for clues about their lives, including their conserved bowel contents, to find out what it ate as their last meal.
They believe the dinosaur, when he died, got into a river and drifted out into the sea, where it sanked on its back to the bottom of the sea.
At the time Alberta was as warm as South Florida today, and rivers and oceans are likely to spread much further inland than they are today.
"This nodosaurier is really remarkable because it's completely covered with conserved scaffoled skin, but also preserved in three dimensions and retains the animal's original shape," Brown said.
"The result is that the animal today looks almost the same as in the early Cretaceous period.
You don’t need to use a lot of imagination to reconstruct it. If you’re squinting a little with your eyes, you could almost believe he’s sleeping.”
Cretaceous Age was a time when giant theropods, carnivorous dinosaurs that stood on two legs crisscrossed the Earth.
Although the kine of them, the Tyrannosaurus Rex, lived millions of years after Borealopelta, the armoured dinosaur was hunted by some of his terrifying ancestors.
This includes the Acrocanthosaurus, a 38-feet (11.5 m), six-ton monster.
Scientists, whose latest findings appear in the journal Current Biology, believe Borealopelta sank into the ocean after death and was mummified in the mud.
The creature was found by Shawn Funk when he dugged at the Suncor Millenium mine near Fort McMurray in northern Alberta, Canada, on March 21, 2011.
According to the museum, it's the world's best-preserved armored dinosaurs, including skin and armaments, and is complete from snout to hip.
Creatures were, on average, about 18 feet (5-meters) long and weighed up to 3,000 pounds (1,300 kg).
It contained two 20-inch-long tips that protruded from its shoulders.
The researchers believe the armored herbivore was swept through western Canada until an overflooded river flooded it into the open sea.
But the dinosaur's underwater burial preserved its armor in exquisite detail.
The petrified remnants of this special specimen are so well preserved that the skin residues still cover bumpy armor plates along the dinosaur
As Michael Greshko has written for National Geographic, such a level of preservation is as 'rare as a win in the lottery.
The more I look at it, the more confusing it becomes.
Petrified remnants of the skin still cover the bumpy armoured plates that carry the animal's skull.
His right forefoot rests by his side, his five fingers spread upwards.
I can count the scales on his sole.'
In thunderstorm: Mass carambolage on A8: Many injured
Violent accident on Thursday evening on the A8: Almost 30 vehicles are involved in a mass carambolage.
There are numerous injured, mile-long traffic jams - and gaffers.
On the Stuttgart-Munich (A8) motorway, about 20 people were injured in a mass carambolage late on Thursday afternoon.
According to initial information from the police around 17.00 clock between the junctions Adelzhausen and Odelzhausen (district Aichach-Friedberg) during a thunderstorm almost 30 vehicles, including probably also trucks, had crashed into each other.
There was initially no information on the exact number of injured.
These are minor to moderate injuries, a police spokesman said.
A large number of rescue vehicles was at the scene of the accident.
The A8 was completely closed towards Munich.
In the direction of Munich, a ten-kilometre-long rethrailation
According to an AZ reporter, there were also massive obstructions on the oncoming carriageway - through numerous gaffers.
The fire brigade recorded the license plates of the drivers, who now have to reckon with sensitive penalties.
Shortly after 18:45, a second accident occurred on the A8, this time between Augsburg and Dasing.
There had been a collision between a car van and a car.
A rescue helicopter was on its way to the scene of the accident.
Deutsche Bahn: Group tests "comfort check-in"
It's a moment that almost everyone has experienced once before: you don't suspect anything on the train and sleep.
Or you are immersed in his work and you have a brilliant thought right now.
And then: puff.
If the conductor arrives and awakens you rudely or tears the passenger out of the middle of the workflow.
ticket control.
But the days of interruptions will soon be over.
Deutsche Bahn is currently testinging creator-free ticket control.
One simply confirms by mobile phone that you taken his place and thus validates the ticket.
A test operation is currently running on two routes.
"Arrive the supply, let's expand it.
It is expected to be available early next year on many more long-distance routes in Germany," a railway spokesman said.
"Comfort check-in" the railway calls the ticket cancellation without conductors.
In order to use them, however, some prerequisites must be met: You need a smartphone or a tablet.
And you have to have booked an online ticket - which most passengers already do.
You also have to buy it via the app of the railway, which DB Navigator has bought, which is anyway the most convenient type of ticket booking.
And finally you need a seat reservation.
Because the "comfort check-in" works like this: You get on the train, call your ticket under "My tickets".
In the case of the test customers, a button for electronic devaluation will appear.
The train attendants receive a message on their mobile devices that passenger X is on the train and has taken the booked seat Y.
If the train attendant arrives at number Y at the ticket control, he knows that there is a passenger sitting there with already devalued online ticket.
So you can continue to sleep, continue to work, stay silent.
The "check-in" has two advantages: one for the customer, one for the train - which in turn is supposed to benefit the passengers.
"If you want to travel relaxed without ticket control through our train attendance staff, simply check in at the DB Navigator yourself.
This enables passengers to make their travel time on the train more personal," says Michael Peterson, marketing board member of DB Fernverkehr.
"And our employees have more time than today to respond to individual customer needs."
Those often travel by train know that, in addition to delays and missed trains, ticket checks are among the biggest annoyances.
Because often yourself, but in any case one of the fellow passengers does not have all the documents you need to devalue online tickets.
Either the credit card or the identity card is missing or otherwise a document that you have to show.
The result is endless discussions between train attendant and passenger, which leads to a bad mood in the compartment or large area and delays in the control of your own ticket and takes the end to take care of other concerns.
Only a small number of test customers can still use the new service.
And this also only on the connections ICE 915 Dortmund-Stuttgart and ICE 912 Stuttgart-Essen.
Further train numbers on the same route aren't unlocked yet.
How an activist came to learn the answer to the question: "Whose Streets?"
On August 9, 2014, Brittany Ferrell was shaken to her core.
Just a day after she arrived in New York City, those born in St. Louis sat on the bend of an Airbnb apartment she rented and scrolled through Facebook on her phone.
She stumbled over a post from someone from high school.
"Police just killed an 18-year-old child, and it's still in the street," it said.
Confused, since no one else had posted the information on her feed, she closed Facebook and opened Twitter.
A user with the Handle @TheePharoah was constantly retweeted on their timeline.
The rapper from the area of St. Louis was in the process of tweeting the death of Michael Brown, an unarmed black 18-year-old who was killed by a white police officer in Ferguson, Mo.
He posted a picture of Brown's lifeless body stretched out in the street and would stay there for four hours.
"That's wild," Ferrell thought to himself.
But death was nothing new for them and their community.
She dropped off her phone to return later in the evening to tweets about people on the canfield drive.
There were photos of the police tape and people screaming, and of a man who claimed to be Brown's father, who carried a sign that read: "Ferguson's police just killed my unarmed son!"
She watched a live feed where a police officer stood in front of a group of protesters with a barking dog.
In another video, she said, another police officer unlocked his rifle and shouted, "I'm going to kill you... animals all."
"That's not common," Ferrell recalled of her testimony.
"That's not the kind of murder we've gotten used to and that we can call typical."
At that moment, I felt chills.
I was angry and crying.”
She booked a return flight and went home the next day.
After landing at 3 p.m., she retrieved her 6-year-old daughter from her father's home and decided that the two would drive to Ferguson the next morning.
What happened to Ferrell and Ferguson in the following three years is the theme of a new documentary, "Whose Streets?", in theaters starting at 11.
It is a story of survival and protest, love and loss, strength and resilience, which consists from the point of view of the people who live in the community and have filled the streets to demand answers.
As Ferrell went to Ferguson with her daughter, who wore a flower dress and a matching crown, the two discussed a conversation they had "abound the black experience and the black state" countless times before that morning.
"You remember how I taught you when black people have to fight for what they believe in?" she recalled what she said.
"We're going to Ferguson now because police killed an 18-year-old boy and that wasn't right.
I couldn't take them," Ferrell later said.
"What happened today is a culmination of what happened in the past.
She needs to know and see it and be raised in it.
It has to be well developed, not only activism and organization, but also know the different layers of black experience and the black constitution and what we need to do to become vacant in order to free ourselves.
Even if I'm lucky enough to give her an experience where she doesn't personally experience blatant racism, she's no different from the next black women's child.
“I feel like I’m doing her a bad service to keep her away.”
The first stop when they arrived in Ferguson was located on South Florissant Road, a location across from the police agency that became a camp area when activists waited for Officer Darren Wilson's possible non-indictment.
That's where Ferrell and her daughter connected with a local business owner who made lunch.
They prepared bags and helped pass them on to protesters.
Slowly, a new kind of activism began to take shape for Ferrell.
"My activism has totally changed after August 9," she said, noting that as a student at the University of Missouri-St. Louis, she was president of the Minority Student Nurses Association and focused on food justice and health inequalities in black communities.
"I had no experience in organising.
It's all come."
Weeks later Ferrell met Sabaah Folayan, one of the film's co-directors, during an evening demonstration.
Folayan said she and her cinematographer Lucas Alvarado-Farrar just wanted to "document" what happened.
One question came to Ferrell's mind: "Do you want to document or are you trying to find a story that you can take advantage of?"
This skeptism has been nurtured by innumerable cases of people, often by whites - in a community that benefited from its pain and resilience.
As Folayan explained in an interview recently: "We'd heared that people who were born and raised in Ferguson didn't center their voices and we wanted to do it differently."
Folayan had already collaborated with then-co-director Damon Davis, an artist from the realm known for his activism around death row inmates.
His involvement brought Ferrell closer to the project, along with six other locals, followed the "Whose Streets?" team in the years following Brown's shooting.
"This documentary isn't someone who speaks for us or talks to us. We're talking," Davis said.
"That was the main thing for me how these people are portrayed, because that's how I'll be portrayed."
The goal wasn't to focus on Ferrell's experience - to show that the movement "isn't a Messiah leader, but a community coming together," Folayan said - she proved to be the film's most open contestant.
As a result, documents from "Whose Streets?" show surprisingly personal aspects of Ferrell's life as she falls in love and her wife marries.
"I felt like naked," Ferrell said, laughing when she first saw the finished product.
"But I have understood that there must be a certain vulnerability in this work and in the humanization of black people."
In addition, Ferrell's story echoes the role of black gay women in the broader black-lives-matter movement.
Two of the movement's three founders who coined the film "BlacklivesMatter" on social media following the killing of black teenager Trayvon Martin in 2012 identify as gay.
"Black, gay women in leadership positions carried the movement as a whole," Ferrell said, "and that's why we're constantly fighting on different fronts.
Black, gay women have to bear the brunt of everything.”
Folayan agreed to note that "the movement is really maintained by black women and many black women."
However, she insists that "this is not an affirmative type of action," in which Ferrell was chosen as the film's heart because of her identity.
"She was the person who shook up that energy," she said.
"It's no coincidence, even though she's at the forefront because there's so much at stake in a life at these intersections as a black, gay woman.
“You can feel their energy through the screen.”
In the meantime, news cameras in Ferguson have long since disappeared, but the racial links between the community and the police continue to exist.
Ferrell is on probation for 21⁄2-year because of the blocking of a highway at a demonstration.
She describes the socio-political atmosphere of the area she calls her home: "A chaos."
Still, she clings to a song and she feels obligated to this memory since she took to the streets in Ferguson three years ago.
"It is our duty to fight for our freedom.
It's our duty to win.
We need to love each other
We've got nothing to lose except our chains.'
From "dream" to "magnitude delusion": TV readers discuss bit gallery
The bit gallery moves people, even though it's far from being built.
But the idea of a shopping mall at the south end of the pedestrian zone has divided the population into advocates and opponents since the first presentation in 2009.
Cold leaves the subject hardly one.
The topic will also be discussed on our Facebook page "Volksfreund Bitburg".
While some say about the Bit gallery: "That would be a dream" (Anja Becker) or "That's really great" (Nicole Schausten), comes from the other side: "Doesn't need a person" (Siggi Betzen) or quite simply: "Signacy madness!" (Lars Scholz).
While proponents rely on the fact that a gallery attracts more customers to the city, which also benefits the other shops, the opponents fear that the pedestrian zone will bleed out.
In addition, the gallery is planned exactly on the property on which the Bauernmarkthalle stands, in which 13 direct marketers offer their products.
At the beginning of the construction work, this will end.
The gallery makers want to integrate a market hall into the new building - namely to Beda-Platz with possibility to sit outside.
Simone Zimmer: "We have been shopping almost weekly for many years in the Bauernmarkthalle.
Mainly our eggs, but also the delicious cakes of the rural women again and again very happy.
We have also discovered the butcher shop for ourselves.
We'd hope that even during and especially after the construction of the gallery, there's a chance to maintain this offer.
This also exists in other cities.
Especially the Kleinmarkthalle in Frankfurt is interesting."
Gerd Densing: "Whether direct marketers settle in a market hall in the gallery depends on the amount of rent.
However, this is unlikely to be low with the new project.”
Eva Kilic: "Why shopping palaces in Bitburg?
Clothing, shoes, drugstores or supermarkets: I find this superfluous.
There's already enough of that in our city.
You can see how many shops are empty here - some for months and years.
However, since there is not even a decent weekly market here yet, it is all the more important that at least the market hall is preserved for us.
After all, the goods offered there are fresh, are mostly offered directly by the producer and is also inexpensive.
As you can see, shops in a shopping center are rented only by larger companies, because the rents there are often unsustainable for a retailer.
It’s a pity, there’s almost no special business anymore and what’s left of it will probably disappear.”
Wolf Fandel: "A shopping mall with a market hall doesn't fit together for me.
And the gallery doesn't fit Bitburg.
Above all, where should the farmers’ market hall go during the construction period?
The old Edeka would be the solution - even in the long run, if the bit gallery actually comes at some point.
Or does Bit-Galerie’s specter manage to distribute all shops from Bitburg beforehand?”
George Marquet: "That's just not going to work.
And no one needs a shopping center that's vacant.
In my opinion, it would have been better to promote a real farmers market instead of this gallery.
But for a farmer's market hall, there is no more space thanks to the insanely many - I think - pointless construction projects in Bitburg.
If the farmer's market hall is ruined, a piece of culture will disappear again."
A fitting resemblance: Artist creates portraits with Chelsea Manning's DNA
In the middle of the room of the Fridman Gallery in New York, many faces can be seen - white, black and brown, almost imperceptibly like each other - that hang on cables from the ceiling.
The 30 portraits were created by artist Heather Dewey-Hagborg of cheek swabs and haircuts sent to her by Chelsea Manning.
Manning sent the excerpts from Fort Leavenworth prison, where the former intelligence analyst served a 35-year prison sentence after she made public classified diplomatic reports via the website WikiLeaks.
The new exhibition, which opened on August 2, is called A Becoming Resemblance.
She is the product of two years of correspondence between Dewey-Hagborg, whose discipline united her expertise in technology, computer science and art, and Manning, a transwoman and groundbreaking dissident whose condemnation was transformed by Barack Obama when he had only three days left in office.
Dewey-Hagborg, through the algorithmic analysis of DNA extracted by Manning and from which 30 portraits were created, how someone with these genomic data might look, created a sharp, if somewhat intellectual, commentary not only on the malformability of DNA data - the many ways they can be interpreted and the inherent determinism of these interpretations - but also identity.
Manning, who was born Bradley Edward but has been talking openly about identification as a woman since her puberty, was convicted of 20 charges in 2013, including six violations of the espionage law, computer fraud and theft.
By prison, her image was suppressed, so much that there was only a single photo - a grainy, black-and-white selfie in which Manning sits visibly uneasily in a driver's seat and carries a platinum blonde wig - with which she was associated.
In 2015, Dewey-Hagborg just came from the great success of her 2012 'Stranger Visions' project.
In it, the artist produced portraits of strangers from forensic artifacts such as cigarette butts and chewing gum by extracting the DNA from the waste to make a picture of what these people might look like.
That was when she received an e-mail from a newspaper magazine.
"They conducted an interview with Chelsea Manning while they were in prison, and they wanted a kind of portrait to accompany this article," Dewey-Hagborg explained during a press review of the new exhibition.
And she couldn't visit at the time and she couldn't be photographed, that's where you made contact with Chelsea and asked her if she wanted to make a DNA portrait.
The artist and her imprisoned muse became unequal penitential friends who exchanged several letters over the course of two years.
They even created a "Suppressed Images" comic illustrated by Shoili Kanungo, which foresaw a future in which the president would convert Chelsea's verdict and she would be able to see the exhibition in person.
That Obama, in fact just days after the book was published, would demand Manning's release after seven brutal years at a military prison in Kansas was a welcome kind of happy meeting.
There's also something deep and strong now at the opening of the exhibition, as Donald Trump wages a war against informants from government campaign and against transgender troops to find new, tweetable ways to further inflame the rigid divisions he was supposed to fix.
When Dewey-Hagborg was asked about the strange timing of all these things, she said that "things happen for a reason."
In an artistic statement written down on the gallery's wall, Manning demanded an end of "automatic splintering whose basis have been gender, race, sexuality and culture," with her outsight signature (the "i" in her family name is provided with a heart.
On the opposite wall, her mitochondrial DNA sequence - a centipede of Cs and Gs and As, is written in pencil.
Dewey-Hagborg hoped to show by incorporating the 200 letters (or nucleotides according to the artist’s highly sophisticated scientific lexicon) how amazingly similar, at least in biological terms, we are all.
"What people will hopefully take away from this is that our genome doesn't care who we are and how open genetic data for an interpretation and how subjective they are," Dewey-Hagborg, whose 2007 video work, Spurious Memories, can also be seen.
"DNA data can tell so many different stories, so that's 30 of these stories."
The last piece in the exhibition, which is kept in its own room, is a page from the above-mentioned comic novelle.
She shows Manning appearing like King-Kong with an acquittal phone in her hand from the United States disciplines.
"If they refrigerate your speech, then they've won," it says.
“Never mouth.”
"It came directly from a letter she texted me," said the artist, who salutes from Philadelphia.
"I'm still getting goosebumps when I talk about it."
Manning hasn't seen the exhibition in person yet - just as it was prescificantly anticipated in the comic book - but if she should, then in the middle of the room she will be greeted by the masks, by her own genomic simulaks, who gather like hordes of protesters.
This was also purposeful, like everything in the exhibition, right down to size (5" 2, Manning's stature), in which their genetic haplogroups were written with pencil.
"In the past, I've had it on the wall, but I wanted them to feel like a crowd," Dewey-Hagborg said of the portraits.
"I wanted it to feel like a mass movement that formed with Chelsea, like the movement that was also behind its liberation."
Northwestern University professor and an Oxford University employee wanted for homicide
A nationwide manhunt is undercaded for a professor at Northwestern University and an Oxford University employee who are wanted as suspects after a man was stabbed with fatal injuries in a luxurious apartment in Chicago.
On Monday, arrest warrants were issued for Wyndham Lathem (42) and Andrew Warren (56) for murder for their alleged involvement in the death of Trenton H. James Cornell-Duranleau, according to Cook County District Court files.
The documents say Cornell-Duranleau, 26, died after several stitches.
A community warning system released by the Chicago Police Department says the body was discovered on July 27.
Officers were given a call, arrived at the Grand Plaza Apartments and found the victim with several injuries to his body.
Cornell-Duranleau was still pronounced dead dead at the scene, and detectives are investigating the stabbings as murder while police are actively searching for the two suspects, officials said.
Lathem has been associate professor of microbiology and immunology at Northwestern University since 2007.
Alan K. Cubbage, the vice president of university relations, said in an e-mail statement that Lathem had now be on a leave of absence and had been barred from entering Northwestern University's campus.
"This is now a criminal matter being investigated by the relevant authorities, and Northwestern University is cooperating in that investigation," Cubbage said.
Warren is Senior Treasury Assistant at Oxford University.
A spokesperson from the university and Somerville College said in an e-mail statement that "we are in contact with police in the UK and ready to help U.S. investigating authorities in any way possible."
The statement added that Warren's colleagues were shocked and want him to turn himself in.
Chief Communications Officer of the Chicago Police Department, Anthony Guglielmi, said he urged the suspects to confront the authorities.
"Police has an idea of their whereabouts, and our efforts to locate it are intensifying," Guglielmi said.
Guglielmi said local, regional and national authorities are helping find the suspects.
Guglielmi said police received a call from the building manager of the Grand Plaza building that had just received an enigmatic tip saying something had happened in apartment 1008.
Police described the scene as very cruel.
Guglielmi said it was unclear whether the incident had also taken place on July 27.
It has been confirmed by security cameras that Lathem and Warren were in the building.
"We believe Professor Latham and the victim had a relationship," Guglielmi said.
Grand Plaza's management team released a statement to residents: "Police are currently working on the timeline and background of the victim and are exploring a variety of motives, including a possible domestic incident."
"We will complete the WADA roadmap in the near future" - Russian Deputy Prime Minister Mutko
Russian Deputy Premier Vitaly Mutko said it won't be long before the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA)'s remaining criteria for the restoration of Russia's anti-doping agency (RUSADA) will be implemented.
"At a certain stage of meeting the criteria of the roadmap, RUSADA has been given the right to plan and test in cooperation with UKAD (UK anti-doping).
The WADA timetable has been updated in this regard," Mutko explained on Thursday.
The PM commented on the second part of WADA's roadmap to comply with the code, which describes the criteria for the reinstatement of RUSADA and was recently published on the organization's website.
According to Mutko, it contains nothing that could cause concern for Russia.
"Everything has been announced, everything is being implemented.
And the results will be presented to the International Olympic Committee (IOC) and WADA in the near future," he added.
One of the criteria mentioned in the list, however, finds that Russian anti-doping authorities must publicly accept the reported results of the WADA-approved investigation by Canadian lawyer Richard McLaren into alleged state-sponsored doping in Russian sport.
Relating to the matter, Mutko said: "We are carrying out the investigation as our anti-doping system has admitted a mistake.
All measures have been implemented.
But there were no government programs, and we won't admit something that doesn't exist.'
Mutko's words matched those of Vitaly Smirnov, the head of Russia's independent anti-doping commission.
Speaking to Russia's sales outlet at the start of the day, Smirnov admitted problems to the past in Russian anti-doping organizations, but then added: "We've repeatedly said that the report contains controversial positions and regulations.
No one intends to accept this report unconditionally," Smirnov added.
RUSADA was expelled from conducting doping controls in Russia in November 2015 as part of the doping scandal.
However, it has been allowed to plan and coordinate tests under the supervision of international experts and the UK anti-doping (UKAD).
Following a visit to the organization's headquarters in Moscow in July, WADA Director-General Olivier Niggli declared that RUSADA was moving "in the right direction" to be reinstated.
Aldi doesn't sell eggs for now
This was announced by the company groups.
It is purely a precautionary measure, it could not be assumed further from any health impairment, the communication said.
As of now, only eggs should be delivered to Aldi, who have been demonstrably tested negatively for fipronil-containing anti-lice.
Aldi wants to create clarity and transparency among its customers.
The start-ups that will help your business not to become the next Uber
Amy Retratt wanted to measure happiness at your e-commerce start-up, but the surveys didn’t work.
Answers were often vague, unhelpful or - even worse - fraudulent.
And even if she promised anonymity, some workers didn't trust the process.
"There was just never consistency and objectivity," Errett, who runs the 75-person San Francisco e-commerce hair care company Madison Reed, said.
She's called people from the outside for help.
A new race of human resources start-ups is growing to help companies figure out how their employees feel.
By creating and licensing software that has the specific purpose of measuring employee engagement, it enables companies to conduct surveys, target special groups and demographic groups, provide employees with anonymity and complaint hotlines, and in some cases allow whistleblowers to circumvent the C-Suite executives and turn directly to the board of directors.
"You now have tools like Strava and Fitbit for tracking their health, but where's Fitbit for your business?" said Jim Barnett, co-founder and chief executive of Redwood City start-up Glint, whose software analytics tools are used by companies to measure employee engagement.
Errett said they gained a better insight into what your employees think and feel with Glint over the three years.
In addition to the snap surveys and surveys from certain teams, it provides a heat map of the company that shows at a glance which units have the most complaints and which executives have low consent results - so you can re-drill why.
Companies come to realize that you need to stay at the forefront of workplace culture so that you don’t become the next Uber to be involved in scandals since a former employee posted a blog post describing an environment of harassment where those who have spoken have been punished.
For start-ups such as Glint, this desire for an overview is a lucrative business opportunity.
The global governance, regulation and compliance industry could have more than $118.7 billion worth in 2020, according to financial website Let's Talk Payments.
Denver, Colorado, start-up Convercent, which helps companies prevent and detect bad behaviour saw rising interest and activity into disrepute earlier this year amid the Uber case.
Convercent has nearly 600 clients, including Airbnb, Microsoft and Tesla.
Uber has recently signed up as a client.
Like Glint, Convercent lets companies send customized “pulse” surveys, collect confidential responses in real-time, and look at the heat maps of your problem areas.
It also provides an anonymous SMS hotline that allows employees to report bad behavior.
And if the chief executive is involved, complaints go directly to the supervisory board.
"The court of public opinion captured the regulators," said Patrick Quinlan, Convercent's founder and chief executive.
If a company is found that treats its employees poorly or behaves unethically, it can experience costly consequences from consumer boycotting, worker fluctuation and lawsuits, Quinlan said.
Ruby Tuesday, the restaurant chain with more than 25,000 employees and over 500 locations, has deployed Convercent for more than a year to ensure that employees are aware of politics and procedures and have an easy way to reach their corporate headquarters.
Earlier, if an employee wanted to report a problem, he or she had to find a phone number or email for the company's headquarters, file a formal complaint and hope he was taken seriously.
It's often been intimidation and uncomfortable experience, said James Vitrano, Ruby Tuesday's general counsel, who said there wasn't a good way to track staff complaints.
Now, since the company employs Convercent, issues that have previously been hidden and addressed more quickly by executives who sat in offices in cities or states - such as discrimination, harassment, or unfair practices.
"I can get closer to this holistic, 360-degree point of view into the experience with preoccupation," said Vitrano, who heads Ruby Tuesday's risk management group.
"And we're protecting our shareholders from employment litigation."
Companients began to take ethics, values and employee engagement more seriously in 2002 after accounting firm Arthur Andersen collapsed because of ethical violations by the Enron scandal, Quinlan said.
But it took time for the "social media to come to their right" that companies realized they couldn't prevent their dirty laundry going online.
"Before using technology to supervise ethics, people used hope as a strategy," he said.
Both Glint and Convercent offer your software as a service and burden the companies with recurring fees for the use of their products.
It's a business model and an opportunity that finds the approval of venture capital investors who have supported both start-ups.
Convercent received $10 million in funding in February from firms including Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital, raising all capital to $47 million.
Glitter secured $10 million in November from Bessemer Venture Partners, bringing its total funding to $60 million.
These investments come as little surprise given the connected nature of companies, culture and venture capital.
There's a growing complex of research showing that today's employees are expecting more from their workplaces than before.
In competitive markets like Silicon Valley, high salaries and interesting projects are just stakes.
The employees want to feel that you will be accepted and valued and that you give your time to a company with a positive mission.
"If people are happy to be in a company, feel that their voices belong, and that the work you're doing is worth it, then they're keen more committed to making this company successful," said Nina McQueen, vice president of global benefits and employee experience on LinkedIn, who uses both Convercent and Glint.
Investors achieve returns when their portfolio companies are doing well. Companies run well when employees are committed.
If third-party analytics tools promise to increase engagement and deployment of employees, it’s no wonder you’re finding support.
The data about employee engagement according to the experts of the workplace culture important.
But the data is useless if a company's managers don't take action.
In fact, when a company asks employees about your feedback, it can have an expectation that change is on the way.
And if the change doesn’t come fast enough, or it doesn’t come at all, this can breed disappointments and demotivate employees.
"If you're asking for 4,000 suggestions, you'll need to be willing to have 4,000 conversations," Russell Raath, president of consulting at business management firm Kotter International, has seen companies that have relied too much on collecting data.
"Because if you don't follow employees will wonder, "Have you really heard me?
You're taking care of it?
And if you don’t care, why should I?”
Madison Reed, who is now conducting monthly employee surveys, was able to take action on the same day if an issue is reported about Glint, according to Errett.
And after collecting feedback from frustrated employees, she was able to reorganize multiple teams within the company through the speed of decision-making and the quality of communication, and adding a communication training to address the issues.
At Ruby Tuesday, the company continues to gain new insights about employees, and it hopes that in the long run, this will lead to a better retention of workers in an industry known for high sales.
"If you're not obliged to create a culture of transparency, you're going to lose people," Vitrano said.
"And if you lose people, you lose customers, too.
And if you lose customers, you will lose companies.
NCAA eliminates two-day practices and makes a long day for the Bruins
It's up to 3 p.m. when a lot of UCLA football players chat on one side of the team's new practice fields on campus.
Some have taken off their jersey tops and show the back full of sweat from the first day of the training camp.
For a good part of the team, these idle minutes represent a lunch break.
Players enrolled in the summer classes will return to their final exams after the three-hour exercise before returning to Wasserman Football Center the next morning at 6:30 a.m.
"That's a real treadmill for the boys for the next three days," Bruins coach Jim Mora said just before Monday turned Tuesday.
The NCAA eliminates two-day exercises to ease players' burden, and it has actually extended their days - at least until Friday's final.
That's why UCLA's first three workouts should start at 7:15 p.m. to live up to players' already packed schedules.
Compromising away from the two days is more than a day when the Bruins postponed the start of camp almost a week after last season and carried out practical exercises during summer school for the first time during Mora's six seasons in Westwood.
They are scheduled to hold 18 exercises over the course of August. 20 prior to the start of formal game preparations for their kick-off against Texas A&M in September.
Mora said that he supported the NCAA's rule change, even if it adds another point to the players' busy routes.
"We're putting this enormous burden three or four days on them, and it's very challenging," Mora said.
"And they've just squeezed it, they're flattening each other, they're great people.
I think as soon as we get by Saturday and we're having more time, it's going to be really great.'
UCLA will end its practices once the camp is finished, which will not be an easy task given the clear lines of sight of its fields from an adjoining parking lot and adjacent Luskin Center, an upscale hotel already pelted with footballs clearing a protective net behind a goal post.
Bruins players are scheduled to occupy the hotel starting this weekend, but Mora conceded that there could be a problem once those rooms are available during the season.
"We're going to use our security," Mora said.
"That's a real problem, and it's not far-fetched to think that people are renting a room there and then setting out to record an exercise there.
If you're having the videotape from an exercise, it's a pretty good deal.
But we've got security forces there, and we've got security forces in
We're going to do the best with what we've got.
I don't worry too much about that.
We've got a plan.'
Facebook seeks to intensify fact-to-scrutiny in fight against fake news
Facebook wants to send more potential articles with false reports to third-party fact-checkers and then display their findings below the original post, the world's largest online social network said Thursday. It's one of his efforts to combat so-called fake news.
The company said in a statement on its website that it will begin using updated machine learning methods to detect possible false reports and send them to fact-checkers who will potentially view results of the fact-checking from the original article.
Facebook has been criticized as one of the main distribution points for so-called fake news, many of which believe have also influenced the 2016 U.S. presidential election.
The problem has also become a major political issue in Europe, with French voters flooded with false stories in the run-up to the presidential elections in May, and Germany supports a plan under which social media networks are supposed to be punished if they fail to quickly eliminate hate-filled postings as they did before elections there in September.
On Thursday, Facebook said in a separate statement in German that a test of the new fact review would be launched in the U.S., France, the Netherlands and Germany.
"Besides realizing whice stories are contested by third-party fact reviewers, people want more context to make informed decisions about what they're reading and sharing," Sara Su, product manager for Facebook news in a blog, said.
She added that Facebook will continue to test its feature on the "related article" and is working on other changes to its news feed to reduce false news.
Creating a Comic Novel: A Castle In the History of England
In its recent past, little Scotney Castle was featured in a squeeze music video and was a loophole for Margaret Thatcher in the 1970's and 1980's.
But Scotney has witnessed 700 years of turbulent history from his cosmopole sit-in at the Bewl River Valley in Kent and has now become the inspiration for a graphic novel by Jamie Rhodes, a mopha-like Yorkshireman with a penchant for historical documents.
"I think it's so nice to look at the handwriting of someone who's been dead for 300 years," he says wistfully.
"What a personal relationship to hold this letter in my hand."
'A Castle in England' spans the period from the Middle Ages to the age of Edward and documents the families who lived in Scotney. Each story was illustrated by a different British artist - Isaac Lenkiewicz, Briony May Smith, William Exley, Becky Palmer and Isabel Greenberg.
It's not Rhodes's first book based on an archivor: He wrote 'Dead Men's Teeth and Other Stories' in 2014, a collection inspired by documents in the British Library - a project he unexpectedly found emotional.
"I only reacted like this to the thumbprint on the page of a letter written by a double agent in the 1700's who worked for the Jacobins," he says.
"That's his thumbprint.
And letters always smell like smoke, because at that time a fire was kindled because of the light.
For someone who lives 300 years later, the smell has an effect, as if he were there himself.
“I love archives.”
For someone smashed with old papers in such a way that he is supported by the National Trust and the Arts Council England and lived in a castle for almost four months ("To be honest: I overrated my welcomemony a little"), this all seems too beautiful to be true.
Rhodos spent his days exploring the Victorian "new" castle, looking through the archives and studying the castle's many treasures and curiosities, such as the mounted hyena heads on the walls and the bottles that had spent centuries in the ditch.
He wandered through the grounds and the ruins of the old castle and spoke with landscapers, gardeners, and sometimes with the artists working on the illustrations of his book.
Then, at night he drank "Whiskey and started writing."
Scotney's intimate history is relatively unknown, since the National Trust didn't gain full access until 2006, when the last heiress, Elizabeth Hussey, died.
When Rhodes arrived a decade later, employees were only just beginning to take care of the archive that he described as "hundreds of years in which aristocrats said "Oh, throw it to the attic."
Diaries, letters from the time of the English Empire, cards depicting who what possessed: "You might think that land conflicts would be boring," says Rhodes, "but there was a birch that two families struggled over.
Boys, let everything go!"
Some of the stories in the collection use the castle as a starting point to delve into English history on a broader basis, including 'The Labourer', which follows a man who leaves the newly built castle to join the peasant uprising of 1381.
"There's a recording of a riot that occurred in the castle in 1380, in this book titled 'Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex,'" Rhodes says.
"The peasant uprising began in this area and I thought an uprising was a good start.
“Maybe they’re connected?”
Other stories are firmly rooted in the history of the castle - such as 'The Priest', which tells the story of the Jesuit Father, whom the Darrell family hid for seven years during the English Reformation.
Or 'The Smuggler', in which 18th-century smuggler Arthur Darrell is portrayed staging his own burial by filling a coffin with stones (a discovery that wasn't made until years later when his coffin was excavated).
The Darrells are Scotney's favourite family: "The gentlemen seemed to be quite crooks, repeatedly taking on debt and spending money on things they couldn't afford.
Other generations of Darrell men solved their debt crises by marrying rich old women - I introduce them to myself as a bunch of good-looking boys, with a talent to advertise heiresses.'
After the end of his assignment in the castle, Rhodes is looking for other unique writing experiences
He's put a two-week stay in the Finnish wilderness for a comic novel about Elämänmäki behind him, the site of an early 20th-century sanatorium that has since been reclaimed again by nature.
Scotney has a special place for him.
"I was sad to leave again," he says.
It was like, "Oh, now I've got to deal with the damn life again."
Campaign launched: Over 300-year-old Marienkapelle in Rhöndorf veiled
With the concealment of the 301-year-old Marienkapelle in Rhöndorf, the city of Bad Honnef launched its campaign to introduce a new umbrella brand (Rundschau from Thursday).
Until Thursday, Mayor Otto Neuhoff and his marketing team around Nelson Artz want to keep the logo and slogan secret.
At the end of August there will be a festival on the island of Grafenwerth as the highlight of the campaign.
"Too nice for us.
Or?" asks the makers of the action since yesterday on the posters at the Rhöndorfer Chapel.
"We don't really think that (of course!)," they write on their website unlocked yesterday.
"But we're using this measure to get your attention.
And to make it clear that some things are better than it seems.
Basically, we firmly believe that, as Bad Honnefer, we know very well about the beauty and attractiveness of our city."
The action is about more than "just" a logo and a slogan, it reads.
"It is necessary to formulate a stand-alone promise, one that gives the city an unmistakable unique selling point."
After all, it is in competition with other municipalities.
The city of Bad Honnef has a mid-five-figure amount cost its new umbrella brand and the campaign.
Military reforms in Turkey - Erdogan's fear of the army
Every year at the beginning of August, the Supreme Military Council (YAS) advises on retirements and promotions in the officers' corps in Ankara.
The same is this week.
At first glance, a normal process.
But nothing is routine among Turkish forces one year after the coup attempt.
The meeting no longer took place in the building of the General Staff, as before, but in the prime minister's office.
Under the chairmanship of the head of government, the five vice-premiers and the ministers of defence, home affairs, the judiciary and the external affairs also took part in the discussions.
Thus, the civilians in the YAS were outnumbered.
The government is pulling the reins in the military.
Hulusi Akar remains in office, but the commanders of the army, the air forces and the navy have been deposed.
Since the failed coup, head of state Erdogan has fired 169 of the 326 generals and admirals.
Over 10,000 soldiers have been removed from the ranks of the army - mostly because of alleged ties to the movement of exile preacher Fethullah Gülen, whom Erdogan sees as the mastermind of the coup attempt.
Against 486 people who are said to have been directly involved in the coup attempt, negotiations have been undercauted in Ankara since this week.
Already the day after the coup, Erdogan had described the overthrown attempt as a "gift of God," as he gave him the opportunity to "completely cleanse" the armed forces.
But does Erdogan really have the army under control?
At the end of February, the newspaper "Hürriyet" published an article with the headline "Unequestrancy in the military headquarters."
In the general staff, there are concerns about an increasing "Islamization" of the army, which manifests itself, among other things, in the lifting of the headscarf ban for female soldiers, wrote the newspaper.
In any case, the large-scale making in the army and the security forces continues.
In mid-July, Erdogan had another 7395 public servants fired by decree, including 2303 polices and 546 soldiers.
It seemed that Erdogan had tamed the military.
The generals used to be considered to be the actual masters of the country.
Three times they couped, citing their role as guardians of the secular state order.
After his election victory in 2002, Erdogan pushed back the power of the militaries step by step, not least citing the reform requirements of the European Union.
Former officers say that Erdogan is targeting an Islamization of the armed forces.
In Vocal Europe magazine, an unnamed officer who, after the coup attempt, sought asylum for fear of persecution abroad, warned that the Turkish army will be "full of extremists and Salafists" in a few years.
If this happens, such a development could not leave Nato cold.
In any case, there are doubts in the alliance after the purges about the readiness of the Turkish army.
The leadership structures and operational capabilities are "weakened," Nato diplomats say.
Wasband changes the rules for the green card
There will be a new U.S. immigration plan to change the rules of the green card, President Donald Trump said, quoted by the media.
He envisages lowering legal immigrants to 50,000 a year, stricter regulations for obtaining visas for a fixed-term work permit and restrictions on family-oriented visas.
The new rules are proposed by Republicans and backed by the president.
The bill is to be debated in the two chambers of Congress.
"This law will reduce poverty, raise wages and save billions of dollars for taxpayers and will do so by the U.S
The law will prevent chain migration and replace the current system.
This new bid procedure will benefit the candidates who speak English, who have the financial means to support themselves and show knowledge that will benefit our economy," Trump, quoted by the American media, said.
Ministry vows to refurbish track for Rennsteigshuttle
In the past few months, there has been a dispute about the future of the Rennsteig shuttle - now it should go very quickly.
Before the end of the year, it will be built on the ailing route on which the tourist railway runs.
In order to secure the long-term future of Rennsteigshuttle, construction work on the track is to begin later this year, according to the Ministry of Transport.
"With the decision to continue ordering and refurbishing the Ilmenau-Rennsteig route, the country is committed to this connection, which is particularly important for tourism," explained Transport Secretary Klaus Sühl in Erfurt on Friday.
He estimated the cost of repairing the line at about 2.5 million euros.
Of this sum, the country takes over 75 percent.
The contract, on the basis of which the shuttle is to be ordered from the country in the future, is currently being finalized, Sühl said.
The ministry and Südthüringen Bahn as operators have already agreed on the technical details.
"This means that the timetable, the vehicles used and the quality standards have been negotiated," Sühl said.
The planned contract between the ministry and the railway company will be concluded for a period of eleven years.
With this, the Rennsteig shuttle should be ordered regularly on December 2017.
The Rennsteig-Shuttle is a railway connection between Ilmenau and Rennsteig, which is particularly popular with tourists.
In recent months, it had been tested in the course of a pilot operation, whether the route will be used by enough people to order it regularly again in the future.
The route is also highly dilapidated in parts.
The rise of sex robots is real, and we should be worried
Just when you thought it couldn't get any crazier in 2017, there's a new report about the technological developments in the sex-robot.
Yes, in fact.
Sex robots are a big thing right now.
Most recently, the Foundation for Responsible Robotics (FRR) published a report on the development of sex robots over the next five to ten years.
According to Noel Sharkey, professor emeritus of robotics and artificial intelligence at Sheffield University and co-founder of the FR, we need to start taking their rise seriously.
Customers can choose the robot's eye color, its shape of the nipple and the type of fingernail.
"People are making fun of them, but the companies are delivering a lot of it, and we're going to see them much more often," Mr. Sharkey said when publishing his new report.
Modern sex dolls have evolved from their inflatable partners into robots with a silicone skin that feels warm - with deliberately human responses achieved by artificial intelligence.
The user can also "adapt" his robot according to his beliefs and select everything, from the robot's eye color to the shape of the nipples to the type or color of his fingernails.
But it's going to become even more disturbing, as many robots own the "personalities" who would make a Stepford woman look progressive.
Modern sex dolls have developed into robots with a silicone skin.
The RealBotix robot, for example, allows its users to adapt their robots to the characteristics they find appealingly, such as shyness.
And then there's the Roxxxy gold sex robot developed by True Companion with pre-programmed personalities like "Frigid Farrah," which gives the impression of shyness and "Wild Wendy" with an "adventurous" personality.
"Roxxxy Gold has a personality that's adapted to your personality as much as possible," enthuses True Companion's website.
So she likes what you like, and she doesn't like what you don't like, etc.
She also has her moods over the course of the day - just like real people!
She can be tired, fixated on conversation or she can be "in the mood!"
There are many disturbing aspects about this rise of sex robots - not least because they replace real human relationships - but the subservient female traits, the allusions to rape, if you take a step towards Frigid (meaning here: reluctant) Farrah, worry you the most.
On its website, True Companion Compared Roxxxy with a vibrator, but for men.
"If women (sic) can have a vibrator, why can't men have Roxxxxy?" the website states.
However, these sex robots aren’t just a fetish or just a different sex toy.
Their emergence and increasing sophistication point to something darker and deeper in our culture, to a withdrawal from the ideal of gender equality to a desire for sex with submission as an optional encore.
Most men, of course, won’t hold sex robots, and while the FRR notices their rising popularity, they still remain tentatively on the verge of consumer culture.
But what leaks out of marginal zones can be very instructive in terms of the spirit of the time in which we live.
And a lot of people aren't strange or insulting until the free market gives them permission.
There’s some overlap that these sophisticated sex robots show up at a time when women’s rights around the world are under threat, where there’s a president in the White House who’s bragged about sexually assaulting women.
The most daunting aspect of the television series 'The Handmaid's Tale' ('The Maigrant') is not the graphic images, the engulfed bodies and the cut-out eyes, but how realistic this vision works.
Gilead's republic is a leap, but at the moment it doesn't feel like a big leap.
The goal behind these sex robots is to make a physical resemblanment to actual women as realistic as technically possible (albeit with pornstar proportions).
Their builders want them to feel human, that they mimic the movement of a real body.
But in a nutshell when it comes to their "personalities," the robots don't represent real women.
They can't separate themselves from their companion or just walk away.
They have no mediation or a backstory, nothing that indicates independence or anything that could disturb the imagination of total submission.
In other words, they are pleasing slaves.
Or to put it in the speech of 2017: Maid
Trump's great accomplishment?
He makes Mitt Romney the epitome of common sense
It has become a truism of the Trump era that the political divisions that polarized U.S. electorate have also divided small-scale families.
Around Christmas and Thanksgiving there were newspapers in the U.S. with advice on how to deal with the horror of Trump-voting relatives.
We're all supposedly trying to burst our filter bubbles.
Less attention has been given to Trump’s power to bring families together.
I'm mentioning this because my cousin, a left-leaning poet who lives in Chicago and has been dealing with the Republicans in our family for a long time, visited me in New York this week.
She reminded me that unity against a common enemy can have a powerful effect.
With Trump in the White House, everyone she knows, including the Republican kinship with whom she had struggled for decades because of politics, is suddenly and quite peculiar on the same side.
This is certainly a good thing around the dining table, but it is noticeable in a further context that this also entails considerable risks.
The left has been boosted by Trump, but the disfiguring effect of his presidency has the power to push us all to the right.
When we're talking about "normalization" and Trump, we're referring to the creepy possibility that his antics won't repulse us one day.
However, there is an even more frighter long-term scenario in that Trump "normalizes" right-wing Republicans who suddenly appear as the epitome of sensibility compared to his standards.
I find myself infested these days by an active nostalgia for Mitt Romney's hilarious version of the mad - the dog on the car roof, the 14% income tax (but at least we knew what his tax return looked like) - both of which, in fact, appear to be a very lenient offence compared to Trump.
If Trump goes, the Republican's next nominee only needs to be mentally healthy to qualify as immeasurable improvement.
When my cousin was in the city, we took our children to the carousel in Central Park, the largest carousel of the New York park department, and the best three dollars you can spend in the city if you don't need caffeine.
The horses are enormous, the fiberglass designs on the central cylinder - all clowns are gnustly and the animals twistle twist to look over their own shoulders full of fear - extremely sinister and the clinking music conveys this in a strange way.
So many clichés of a cliché of a city disappoint: the views of tall buildings grow old, the skylines become too familiar to provide a lot of excitement, and the reality of the city doesn't live up to the dream.
Thirty-five years later, and I'm still not quite over the day when I found out that Swiss Cottage is in fact not a Swiss cottage - the theme-pub Ye Olde Swiss Cottage doesn't count - but a big roundabout in north London.
However, the Central Park carousel is still a strangely magical experience, as two two-year-old, an 11-year-old and two women can go on it here in the 40's and everyone has a great time.
If I could've rated the carousel, I'd've given him the full score, but luckily no one asked for it.
On the other hand, this week in a department store I was forced to rate my "experience" with transactions on a scale of one to five stars before I was able to pull through my credit card.
In the coffee house on the corner of my street it's the same, plus the option to match the rating with a 15% tipping - this to buy something over the counter.
This not only leads to a slowdown of what should actually be a neutral exchange, but also exerts pressure on the server in order to introduce personality into a situation in which none is required.
The poor woman in the department store was apologetically grinning as I tiredly had the experience of handing her my five-star credit card.
Oscar Pistorius transferred to hospital with chest pain
Pistorius has left the prison for a hospital visit for the second time.
Last year, he was taken to the hospital for treating cuts on his wrists, which he suffered, according to the prison authorities, following a case in his cell.
Pistorius was convicted of assassination following an appeal by prosecutors against an initial manslaughter verdict.
He killed Steenkamp in the early hours of Valentine's Day in 2013 by shooting her several times through a door of the toilet cubicle at his home in Pretoria.
Pistorius claimed that he thought his girlfriend was a nocturnal intruder hiding in the cabin.
Prosecutors have announced their intention this time against Pistoriu's six-year sentence, which they say is too lenient.
The National Prosecuting Authority said that it would appeal to the Supreme Court of South Africa, and the opposition could be heard this year.
Pistorius runs the risk that his sentence will be increased to 15 years if prosecutors are successful.
There is no death penalty in South Africa.
When farmer Arnold finds a strange egg
As the last of the Blumberg schools, the fourth graders from Fützen and Epfenhofen were discharged into the holidays and the secondary schools.
In the future, three of the dismissal pupils will go to high school, four will go to secondary school.
Headmistress Angelika Sitte greeted the parents and representatives of the public at the Bürgerhaus Biesental in Epfenhofen on Tuesday evening.
As a symbol of the school dismissal, the 29 pupils of the four classes led by Sophia Schumacher and Regine Meder chose a young eagle, which grows up in the chicken farm, but is then released to freedom.
In addition, the lively char led a musical entitled "Der Adler im Hühnerhof".
Farmer Arnold found a strange egg, and hatched it from his chickens.
Instead of a chicken chick, a small eagle came out.
But the hope of making a chicken out of him smashed, the eagle spread its wings on a high mountain and disappeared.
After that, grades three and four in the rehearsal of Yvonne Frank showed a chicken dance.
The hens danced with the young bells carrying cylinders around the bet.
The eagle stands for integration, said headmistress Angelika Sitte.
The juvenile is different from the other cubs (chicks), but is part of it.
He'll never be like a chicken.
His destiny is to be free.
She wished every fourth-grader to feel accepted in his new school.
This was followed by thanks to the colleagues, the former headmaster Michael Müller, who repeatedly intervenes in teaching bottlenecks, and the parent representatives.
The chick time is now over, explained class teacher Sophia Schumacher.
After two years of tuition, she was reluctant to release her students, because it was a really great time.
In the end, she was able to rely on the fourth graders.
At the end, all students sounded a song of summer time and holiday season.
There was great joy among the children.
For the new school year, eight first graders have already registered.
Two Bulgarians arrested for illegal camping in Greece
Two Bulgarians have been jailed for illegal camping in Greece, the ministry of foreign affairs, cited by television, said.
On the morning of August 1, a group of illegally camping people, including the two Bulgarians, was arrested in Sithonia by police.
On the same day, they were released, while an investigation is currently ongoing.
The State Department reported bTV that the Bulgarians were later released.
Greek authorities have tightened control over free camping and impose significant fines on anyone who is found to stay in a place that isn't intended for that purpose.
Checks will continue throughout the season.
The ministry said the court can impose a fine.
Software update for diesel vehicles: What those affected need to know now
Exactly how does the software update take place?
Do diesel drivers have to participate in the action?
The most important questions and answers at a glance.
At the diesel summit, BMW, Daimler, Opel and the VW Group offered software updates for a total of around 5.3 million cars.
They are intended to improve the gas behavior of cars of emission classes Euro 5 and partly Euro 6.
Euro 4-diesel or worse are exempt.
Anja Smetanin from ACE Auto Club Europe gives assessments:
Is the new action obligatory?
No, the negotiated software updates are voluntary.
"The mandatory updates previously only apply to the vehicles that have cheating software," says Anja Smetanin.
Their retrofitting has been ordered by the Federal Motor Transport Office (KBA).
How and from when will customers be informed about updates?
The manufacturers have announced a speedy implementation in the next few months, but have not yet given a precise date.
Daimler has announced that it is contacting everyone affected.
If an inspection is due anyway, this will be done within their scope.
How long should the conversions drag on overall?
"Difficult to say," Smetanin says.
This depends on the commitment of the manufacturers as well as on political pressure.
"Because we've seen that automakers don't act voluntarily so far when it comes to the environment."
How long will the update last?
The conversion itself could only take three to four hours in the workshop.
It could be more time-consuming to get an appointment.
What do motorists who do not drive a car from BMW, Daimler, Opel and the VW Group do?
The ACE advises waiting.
Presumably foreign manufacturers would follow suit, according to Smetanin.
Is there a risk of a massive drop in the price of Euro 4 cars?
ACE fears that.
But especially in the regions where driving bans threaten.
In rural areas, there have so far been no drops in value for older diesels.
The ACE advises waiting with a sale.
LL Cool J Receives Kennedy Center Award
The Kennedy Awards Gala is one of the most glamorous events in Washington.
The first award ceremony under President Trump promises to become political.
One of the laureates doesn't want to come - in protest.
LL Cool J (49) is the first rapper to receive one of the coveted awards from the renowned Kennedy Center.
As the cultural institution announced on Thursday, singer Gloria Estefan (59), Lionel Richie (68) as well as the choreographer Carmen de Lavallade (86) and the TV producer Norman Lear (95) are among the honorees this year.
The awards will be handed over at the beginning of December.
Traditionally, U.S. President Donald Trump will host a reception and, along with his wife Melania, take part in the ceremony.
Lear, who has become known with progressive TV series, has therefore already announced that he will refrain from participating in the gala.
“This presidency has decided to completely neglect the arts and human rights—even depriving them of the funds—and that puts a strain on me.”
The 59-year-old Estefan stressed that she would use the conversation with Trump to clarify her point of view.
Named to former President John F. Kenneny, the Kennedy Center is the largest cultural institution in the U.S. capital.
Former winners include Meryl Streep, Aretha Franklin, Robert De Niro, George Lucas, Tom Hanks and Sting.
We're making the city livable
Roland Wacker, Haslach's city builder, will retire next year.
The position has already been advertised - but suitable applicants are rare.
Wacker has been city master builder for 31 years.
And he still enjoys working, as he conveys in the press conference.
He reports on varied, but also demanding tasks awaiting his successor.
At the same time, he is surprised that so far no application fulfills the requirements profile.
The city is looking for an architect or civil engineer at the earliest opportunity.
Thus - in compliance with the regular notice periods - there should still be enough time for an incorporation.
Since Haslach acts as the Lower Building Law Authority for the administrative community, the applicant must show appropriate degrees.
Because the city definitely wants to retain this function.
If they could no longer fill them, the citizens would have to drive up to Offenburg for advice.
But citizen proximity is important to the city and himself, says Wacker, who reports of a good working atmosphere as well as a good mood between authority and citizens.
In more than 30 years in office, several significant projects have accumulated, which Wacker looks back on with pride.
"We're making the city livable," he's sure about the construction office and its staff.
But varied projects are also on the agenda in the future: The redesign of the former Hukla site and the Niederhofen parking lot, for example, are initiated.
The Kinzig Bridge between Bollenbach and Steinach is being renewed, even the stadium renovation is still an issue.
The latter alone will cost around 1.5 million euros "if we do it right," estimates Wacker.
"All in all, great tasks are waiting for my successor.
Work here is fun.”
There is a lot of room for design for a city builder.
With Haslach's own building yard and nursery, many things can be reacted quite flexibly to many things.
"You can move a lot," says Wacker, and the design of the green areas is often praised by citizens and visitors.
Since the old town as an ensemble is under monument protection, it is important to have "an eye for what fits in" if new buildings are approved, emphasizes the outgoing city builder.
In any case, the city master builder bears high responsibility.
He is builder, "in the end, everything runs over my table".
In terms of supervision and coordination, he can count on long-standing employees: Markus Neumaier (building construction) has been there for ten years, Manfred Schöner (civil engineering) for 15 years.
Wacker also hopes that his successor will remain for a long time: "We are looking for someone who really identifies and wants to stay with the position".
Continuity is not only important for a good working atmosphere, but also for the further design of the city.
If you want to inherit Roland Wacker, according to the city of Haslach, you should be able to show a degree as a graduate engineer or a bachelor's degree in architecture and have good expertise in the areas of building regulations and construction planning law.
The city construction office is undergone building and civil engineering, construction planning, land acquisition matters, administration of the municipal properties, municipal depot with construction yard and city gardening as well as lower building law authority including monument protection.
Bicycle thrown on lines: Southern railway line closed
According to police, the hitherto unknown perpetrators had thrown the blue bike from the train trail overpass in the area of Leobener Straße (B116) onto the lines.
These were so badly damaged that the approaching freight train had to be stopped.
Investigators assume two male perpetrators and have been searching for them since the night hours.
Near the crime scene, a pink scooter and a bicycle rider had been witnessed by witnesses.
"This goes far beyond a lousy boy prank," ÖBB spokesman Christoph Posch told the APA.
The perpetrator or perpetrators had put themselves in great danger and also endangered train traffic.
In addition to the damage to the locomotive and the facility as well as the delay minutes and the costs for rail replacement traffic, penalties of the railway customers could also come to the perpetrators.
Overall, a "significant" sum should be assumed, the spokesman said.
The damage was repaired on Friday afternoon, the rail traffic on the southern railway line could be routed without obstructions.
Nearly ten passenger trains were cancelled on Friday morning, two of which involved long-distance traffic.
Freight trains did not fail, but they were delayed.
According to ÖBB spokesman Christoph Posch, about 200 meters of overhead lines had to be replaced.
Insulators and masts were also damaged.
Red meat, dairy products and chocolate could help alleviate IBD
Red meat and chocolate can sound like a nightmare for your digestive system.
But a new study claims just the opposite: a diet rich in meat-based proteins and dairy products could alleviate the symptoms of patients with inflammatory bowel disease.
The incurable condition, which affects around 1.3 million in the U.S. and 300,000 in Britain, causes stomach cramps and bloating, recurrent or bloody diarrhea, weight loss and extreme fatigue.
While sufferers are usually advised to avoid meat and dairy products to alleviate their symptoms, researchers at Washington University found the essential amino acid tryptophan of proteins helps develop immune cells that promote a tolerant gut.
The lead researcher, Dr Marco Colonna, explained that immune cells patroll in the gut to ensure that harmful microbes hiding in food do not enter the body.
Cells capable of triggering inflammation are compensated by cells that promote tolerance and protect the body without harming sensitive tissues.
If the balance tends too far towards inflammation, this can result in inflammatory bowel disease.
In experiments, it was found that a type of tolerant immune cell occurs in mices carrying a certain bacterium in their intestines.
And the bacterium requires tryptophan - one of the building blocks of proteins - to trigger the appearance of the cells.
Professor Colonna said: "We've established a connection between a type of bacteria - Lactobacillus reuteri - which is a normal part of the intestinal microbiome, and the development of a population of cells that promote tolerance.
The more tryptophan the mice had in their food, the more they had of these immune cells.”
He suggested that the combination of L. reuteri and a diet rich in tryptophan promotes more tolerant, less inflammatory intestinal milieus when the same system also works in humans.
Postdoctoralist Dr. Luisa Cervantes-Barragan investigated a type of immune cell that promotes tolerance when she found out that a group of study mice had such cells, but had another group of the same strain, but which was housed separately, did not.
She suspected that the difference had to do with the gut microbiomes of the mice - the community of bacteria, viruses and fungi that normally live in the gastrointestinal tract.
The intestines of the mice were DNA-sequenced and six types of bacteria were found present in the mice with the immune cells, but were missing without them in the mice.
Mices were raised in a sterile environment, so they lacked an intestinal microbiome and they did not develop this type of immune cells, and they were given birth to the L. reuteri and the immune cells.
To understand how the bacteria affected the immune system, the researchers allowed L. reuteri to grow in a fluid and then convicted small amounts of the liquid - without the bacteria - on immature immune cells isolated from the mice.
The immune cells developed into the tolerance-promoting cells.
When the active component was purified from the liquid, it turned out to be a by-product of tryptophan metabolism known as indole-3-lactic acid.
If the researchers doubled the amount of tryptophan in the mice's feed, the number of such cells increased by about 50 percent, but when tryptophan levels were halved, the number of cells dropped by half.
Dr Cervantes-Barragan explained that humans have the same tolerance-enhancing cells as mice, and that most of us protect L. reuteri in our gastrointestinal tracts.
It is not known whether tryptophan by-products from L. reuteri develop the cells in humans as in mice, but defects in genes related to tryptophan have been found in people with inflammatory bowel disease.
She said: "The development of these cells is probably something we would like to promote as these cells control inflammation on the inner surface of the intestinal.
Potentially high tryptophan levels in the presence of L. reuteri can lead to an expansion of this population."
Jose Mourinho believes Manchester United have signed a "genius" with Nemanja Matic
At Manchester United, a "genius" has signed with Nemanja Matic, Jose Mourinho believes after the midfielder made his debut against Sampdoria on Wednesday night.
Chelsea's 40 million pound signature began with a 2:1 victory in Dublin, the last pre-season game, before the UEFA Super Cup against Real Madrid begins on Tuesday, and impressed in the holder's role as people like Paul Pogba and Andreas Pereira advanced at will.
