"We're going to help him be ready sooner than later."
In addition to Matic, Victor Lindelof and Romelu Lukaku also competed at the Aviva Stadium when Mourinho formed his starting XI for the start of the season.
The Independent revealed this week Mourinho wished for three more new commitments, but he insisted that the club had already done a "fantastic job" of fulfilling three of its four overridden re-commitments.
"Getting three out of four, that's what I thank the club for," he added.
"I'm going to be happier if they get four out of four for me, but they've gone to great lengths."
Boeing "paints" Dreamliner into the sky
U.S. aircraft maker Boeing has painted the silhouette of a Dreamliner into the sky.
The pilots flew off from Seattle in the U.S. state of Washington, but the drawing began in Marquette in the US state of Michigan and dragged in the form of a Dreamliner over the U.S., which is several thousand kilometers.
The action lasted 18 hours and one minute.
On the radar screens was to be the shape of a Dreamliner.
The whole thing was an endurance test for new engines.
Boeinge had already done such advertising earlier.
That's how the number "787" and the Boeing logo were painted in the sky.
New low blow for Martin Schulz - Hannover, we have a problem
How the wind turns: When Sigmar Gabriel was still SPD leader, Social Democrat prime ministers always looked to Berlin before elections with a certain concern.
Only no disturbing fires from the party headquarters, just no headwinds from the federal government - this was the most important wish of the election campaigners.
Since Martin Schulz leads the party, that has fundamentally changed.
Since then, the employees from the Willy-Brandt-Haus have been observing the events in the SPD-ruled state capitals with growing horror.
What Schulz has received from there since the beginning of the year would not even be described with headwinds.
It was deep blows.
One after the other.
After the surprising end of the red-green majority in Lower Saxony, the federal CDU sees a general signal against coalitions of the SPD and Greens.
"This shows once again: Red-green simply cannot govern reliably," CDU Secretary-General Peter Tauber told the editorial network Germany (RND).
The surprising factional exit of a Green MP has plunged the government of Prime Minister Stephan Weil (SPD) into a serious crisis.
Five months before the state election, his red-green coalition thus lost its one-vote majority to the opposition from the CDU and FDP.
Green's federal managing director Michael Kellner has urged breakaway Lower Saxony's Greens MP Elke Twesten to return her mandate.
The transition to the CDU means a clear distortion of the will of voters.
Elke Twesten must immediately return her mandate," the Federal Managing Director and campaign manager of the Green Party told RedaktionsNetzwerk Deutschland (RND).
"All this must not be true," sighs an SPD campaigner, when the new job message reaches the party headquarters on Friday morning.
Stephan Weil, SPD Prime Minister of Lower Saxony, stands without his own government majority after the conversion of the green deputies Elke Twesten to the CDU.
It is the tentative culmination of a whole rank of large and small crises in SPD state associations.
And nobody knows what's next.
The misery began with the unexpectedly marked defeat in the Saarland election in March.
A black eye, nothing more, the comrades reassured at the time.
After the state elections in Schleswig-Holstein and North Rhine-Westphalia, everything will be different.
It turned out - and everything got much worse.
The defeat in Kiel caused the SPD tumbling, the Düsseldorf bankruptcy knocked over the party smoothly.
The comrades rappeared painstakingly again in the weeks after.
Then, when they had just scoured new courage, the G-20 summit came from Hamburg.
And with him came the next low blow.
Mayor Olaf Scholz, the last strong SPD state leader, lost his nimbus that weekend because he was sitting in the Elbphilharmonie while his city burned.
And because he did pretty much everything wrong in crisis communication afterwards, which you can do wrong.
Scholz has been fighting for his political survival ever since.
Now Stephan Weil too.
Due to the VW emissions affair and its role on the supervisory board, the Lower Saxony prime minister has been under criticism for a long time.
Lousy poll numbers were added.
Nevertheless, or perhaps precisely for this reason, Weil has increasingly tried in recent weeks to set accents in the waistband.
Comrades around Martin Schulz did not hide the former mayor’s desire to expand his power in Berlin.
They let him grant him, as they promised Weil a stabilization of the party.
Now the exact opposite could happen.
Weil is basically not to blame for the party-internal dispute of the Lower Saxony Greens.
And Schulz certainly doesn't.
In the meantime, the comrades therefore strive to keep their own rows closed.
Her anger is directed at the Greens, and here above all the breakaway parliamentarian Twesten.
"It is unbearable and democracy-damaging how Ms. Twesten puts her own career interests above everyone else's interests," Matthias Miersch, head of the party leaders in the SPD parliamentary group, tells the RedaktionsNetzwerk Deutschland (RND).
Ms Twesten has not been elected to parliament because of her person as a direct candidate, but via the state list of the Lower Saxon Greens, the Lower Saxon MP continued.
The logical consequence would be for it to return its mandate.
Thomas Oppermann, head of the SPD parliamentary group, laments an "undemocratic manoeuvre."
"The will of the voters four years ago was clear: the people have made Stephan Weil the prime minister," Oppermann, who leads the Lower Saxony SPD as the top candidate in the federal election, tells the RND.
"Now the personal career thinking of a future CDU deputy distorts the election result," Oppermann continued.
He called for clarifying the background.
I want to know whether there has been more or there have been further commitments from the CDU Lower Saxony.
Funds for town centre III
If you want to renovate your old house, you can soon get support from the country.
Mayor Albert Schindler and three representatives of STEG Stadtentwicklung informed about this on Wednesday in Empfingen.
At the beginning of the information event, Schindler once again gave a review of what has happened so far.
There have been redevelopment areas in Empfingen since 1991.
At that time, in the redevelopment area I from 1991 to 2004, the state of Baden-Württemberg took over two-thirds of the funding amount.
In redevelopment area II from 2005 to 2016, the subsidy was still 60 percent.
For Schindler, the previous renovations are a "success project" - after all, it is important to promote internal development, and not only to open up new construction areas.
On April 4, the Karlsruhe government office received the information that there will be a redevelopment area III.
The subsidy amounted to 833 333 euros, of which 500 000 euros would come from the country, the rest from the municipality of Empfingen.
How is a rehabilitation area created?
Schindler continues to investigate where the "really old" houses are.
But it also plays a role as to whether there is any interest in the planned area to renovate at all.
If necessary, the territorial boundaries could then also be postponed again.
STEG represented Martin Keller, Gaby Pietzschmann and Sarah Gotzel on Wednesday night.
Keller presented the contents of the federal-state program "Sociale Stadt", in the framework of which the renovations would then be carried out.
At the heart of the programme are the revitalization of inner cities, the improvement of housing and working conditions and the maintenance and expansion of infrastructure.
The measures would be carried out object-related, i.e
The redevelopment area would then finally be defined as the statutes.
Since 1 January 2017, the approval period for the funding will run until 30 April 2026.
Until September of this year, however, the inventory would first be carried out, within the framework of which the parties involved in the rehabilitation area will receive a questionnaire to fill out.
In November or December, if possible, the bylaws should take place, so that the renovations can begin next year.
Pietzschmann then explained that each municipality had to carry out the said pre-examinations.
It is a matter of assessing urban maladministrations and deciding when to refurbish and to what extent.
A refurbishment notice will then also be entered in the land register for the respective property.
Pietzschmann himself will come again to Empfingen and get an idea of the situation, she promised.
Gotzel emphasized that an essential success factor for the project is the participation of those involved.
Therefore, there will be questionnaires, which will be sent to the citizens by post in the coming weeks and can be filled out until after the end of the summer holidays.
The yellow questionnaire is the "building and land sheet", which deals, among other things, with the structural condition and the renovation intentions.
In the gray questionnaire, the "household and apartment form", information on the size, distribution and residents of a living space can be provided.
The blue "operating sheet" must only be completed if business premises are operated in the building.
However, it should be noted that Gotzel pointed out that a completed questionnaire is not an application for funding at the same time.
In addition, new construction measures are not eligible.
Schindler emphasized that the distribution of the subsidies works according to the "greyhound principle" - whoever comes first grinds first.
You can't "reserve" funding for later.
Finally, photos of the "Ortskern II" project were shown in order to make a redevelopment "tasty".
British inventor puts a microchip on himself to get into the house and car with a hand gesture
A British inventor is the first in the country equipped with a microchip so he can open his front door, enter his office, and even start his car with a hand gesture.
Steven Northam, 33, had implanted the tiny chip between the thumb and forefinger and is now offering service to companies and individuals who want to simplify their lives with technology through his company BioTeq.
He has partnered with Dr. Geoff Watson, an advisory anaesthesist at Royal Hampshire County Hospital in Winchester, Hants, to make sure the implant procedure is carried out according to medical standards.
The technology resembles a cat and dog implanted microchip and only lasts 30 seconds.
Mr Northam's company would also like to change the locks on houses and cars in such a way that they are compatible with the chip, all for an extra charge of £300 per lock.
The married father of one has now built up technology both at home and with his BMW Z4, which begins when his hand is behind the wheel.
White House's advertising costs cost taxpayers thousands
The White House's decision to fly a naval helicopter to the South Lawn for an event that highlights American manufacturing last month costs taxpayers up to US$24,000, according to military data left to TIME magazine.
The green-and-white Sikorsky VH-3D, known as Marine One when the president is on board, was the centerpiece of the July 17 event at the White House with American building programs.
President Donald Trump, Vice-President Mike Pence and senior White House officials made a tour of products from all 50 states.
The Connecticut-made helicopter was exhibited in addition to a yacht from Maine, a fire truck from Wisconsin and a forklift from Mississippi.
The White House military office demanded the presence of the helicopter in a fragmentary order or FRAGO, barely 36 hours before the helicopter landed on the South Lawn, according to the records of the Marine Helicopter Squadron One (HMX-1), the unit responsible for operating the helicopter.
"I just wanted you to be aware of it because it's such an unusual event with high visibility," the commander of HMX-1 wrote on the eve of the event his supervisor, the deputy naval commander for aviation.
The news indicated that the helicopter arrived at the South Lawn around 7 a.m. on July 17 at around 3 p.m., and remained until the lawn was cleared between 18:00 and 21:00 that evening.
The note adds that if the departure from the White House were to be delayed, due to the limitation of hours of service, a second crew would be needed for the return flight.
The original flight crew was on the helicopter during the event, while the unit's security personnel remained on the ground with the aircraft during the period.
It was not clear from the records made available to TIME whether a second flight crew was ultimately required.
The Hatch Act prohibits federal employees from using state property for political purposes.
Data sets indicate that HMX-1 has asked if the event could violate determination.
"One question we asked was whether it was a violation of the Hatch Act and we were informed that this was not the case," the commander wrote.
"Sounds like you're locked," the deputy commander replied.
The "Made in America" event has been dubbed by the White House as an official event and would not have been covered by the Hatch Act.
But official events also have political overtones.
At the event, the president gave an impetus to the reform of health care reforce underway in the Senate, and he propagated efforts to curb government rules.
Using the aircraft, known as Marine One or Air Force One, for politically beneficial purposes is hardly a new phenomenon.
Presidents are obligated to use them for travel.
But requesting their use only for photo surgery is unusual.
Trump conducted an investigation in February when the presidential plane provided taxi services against the backdrop of his first campaign rally in Melbourne, Florida, and his Twitter account is full of reports complaining about his predecessor's use of the presidential plane.
"The White House is thrilled by the "Made In America" show - which showcases products from all 50 states made by American workers using American materials," White House deputy press secretary Lindsay Walters said.
HMX-1 occupations frequently conduct training flights in the area around Washington and the South Lawn to depose the U.S. president at the White House with precise centimeter sets.
When Trump was at the G20 summit in Germany in early July, HMX-1 carried out 58 practice landings at the White House.
The flight for the "Made in America" event counted on the flight crew's training needs for the month, an official said.
Helicopter units flew an additional 11 hours that day for "non-presidential support missions."
The White House said taxpayers didn't take the burden on any of the other props presented at the event.
A Sikorsky spokesperson said the company had not been involved in exhibiting the helicopter on the South Lawn.
HMX-1 doesn't maintain budget records for presidential flights, and it's difficult to determine the exact cost of the event.
However, according to U.S. Department of Defense Fares for the financial year 2017, the DoD reimbursement rate for the VH-3D is 24,380 USD per hour.
Records of the unit record the flight time of the aircraft recorded as 30 minutes from the home base of the Marine Corps Air Facility in Quantico in Virginia.
The White House argues that the actual cost of operating the helicopter is well below that rate.
"The cost for taxpayers based on the estimates for reimbursement rates is highly misleading," Walters said.
"These estimates include personnel, maintenance and many other hidden costs included in the annual funds."
According to a RAND study from 2015, the reimbursement rates for DoD do not include personnel costs, but take into account the costs of fuel, maintenance and contract conclusion.
At that time, former White House press secretary Sean Spicer defended the helicopter's use for the event.
"The idea is to present things made in America this week," Spicer said.
"And I know Sikorsky and the state of Connecticut are very proud that they contribute to our national security, and I assume hundreds, if not thousands, of people depend on it.
And I think, like most Americans, we're all proud of the helicopter and other military equipment that so many Americans have worked tirelessly for.
Of course, it’s appropriate to highlight that.”
It wasn't clear whether Trump interacted with the helicopter as he entered his tour of the South Lawn for the event, but he sat himself in the driver's seat of a firefighting car.
At the White House, Trump spoke to Sikorsky representatives and joked with the media about his own fleet of company products.
"I know Sikorsky very well," the president said, "I have three of them."
