Narco's Seasolage 3 trailer: Without Pablo Escobar, the Cali Cartel will take centre stage in Netflix's hit series
Despite Narco's second seasoon, which reveals Pablo Escobar's killer, Netflix's series is far from over.
Seasons three and four have already been confirmed by the streaming service, the first received their first trailer and featured the show's latest villains.
With season three, the DEA has turned its attention to the world's richest organisation of drug trafficking: the Cali cartel.
Led by four powerful godparents, they operate "like a Fortune 500 company" only with more state bribes and violent actions.
Gilberto Rodriguez Orejuela (Damian Alcazar) is the leader of the cartel, Miguel Rodriguez Orejuela (Francisco Denis) is her head, Pacho Herrera (Alberto Ammann) commands the Mexican connection, and Chepe Santacruz Londono (Pepe Rapazote) is in New York.
In the newly released trailer, Pedro Pascal returns as Javier Peña, the guy who invites American and Colombian law enforcement agencies to support the war on drugs.
Other new actors include Matias Varela, Michael Stahl-David, Matt Whelan, Miguel Angel Silvestre, Kerry Bishe and Arturo Castro.
Narco's third seasoon will begin on September 1.
Role backwards: The city hires 46 kitchen aids
The city organizes the day-care table itself again.
With permanent contact persons for the children and an assignment to the team.
In urban day-care centers, the clock is turned back.
On 1 September, the city of Krefeld takes over the food supply in the municipal day-care facilities (day-care centers) again on its own.
For Sonja Pommeranz, deputy head of the department of youth assistance, this is good news: "We were all very pleased".
It will continue to be boiled out of the house: a caterer takes over this task and delivers freshly cooked refrigerated.
The city operates 46 day-care centers in the Krefeld area, in 44 the children receive a hot meal at lunchtime.
In the past daycare year, 2800 children each sat at the tables, and the demand grows steadily.
"Meanwhile, not only children, who are cared for 45 hours per week, eat lunch in the day-care center," explains Pommeranz.
"The offer is also valid in 35-hour care if children stay until 2 p.m."
46 part-time workers have kitchen service from 1 September: they heat the delivered dishes, supplement them with salad, raw food and fruit, portion the meal individually, spend the food, clear, rinse and clean the kitchen.
"Employees are firmly assigned to a day-care center," says Pommeranz.
The size - there are two-group as well as seven-group facilities - decides on the length of hours.
"The kitchen assistants are now firmly part of the day-care team," says Pommeranz with satisfaction.
"So they are much more involved and it is easier for daycare management to implement pedagogical concepts."
Because that's also what it's all about: healthy diet and cooking classes.
"The children should help prepare the food."
The direct connection of the kitchen power to the team makes sense.
For the children, too.
"They have a well-known force in daycare, a person familiar to them, rather than ever-changing people."
This continuity is important for children.
The cleaning of the day-care rooms - so far also the task of the kitchen staff - will be carried out by special cleaning staff in the future.
In addition to improving the quality of the kitchen performance, this variant - the separation of cleaning and kitchen service - also has the charm, which it turned out to be the most comparable in the cost comparison between self-promotion and third-party allocation for the city, according to the press office.
And this, although 46 part-time workers are hired again.
"But these hours are not so important for around 750 people in the educational sector," says Sonja Pommeranz.
Afghan democracy depends on women's political role, the UN says
Elections can only be representative if all members of society, including women, play a role, the head of the U.N. mission in Afghanistan said Wednesday.
Nicholas Haysom, incumbent head of the United Nations mission, said hundreds of women will run in the council elections in the provincies. Three women are running for vice-president's role and more than 30 percent of newly registered voters are women.
"The role of women cannot be overstimated," he said in a statement on Wednesday.
Elections can only be truly representative and credible if women participate fully and are included in all parts of the electoral process.
Afghan elections for the provincial council and presidential elections will take place on April 5.
The Taliban has released threats against an electoral trial that coincides with the transition of international combat operations to an advisory role.
This year, for the first time, the elections will peacefully hand over power from one democratically elected government to the next.
Haysom said a strong role for women will reinforce Afghanistan's democratic success.
The Afghan Independent Electoral Commission reports that 1.2 million women from a total registered 3.6 million voters are registered.
