If you need a ride from Kaiseringen to Frohnstetten: Meeting point is in Kaiseringen at the school.
Men try to rob 13-year-old
Two young men aged 19 and 23 are alleged to have tried to rob a 13-year-old girl on Thursday.
According to information from the police, the men approached the 13-year-old, who was with friends on Kolpingstraße, around 2:55 p.m. because they wanted to make phone calls with the girl's mobile phone.
However, the 13-year-old declined the request and went on with her friends.
As a result, one of the perpetrators tried to grab the girl's mobile phone and necklace, but this failed.
Without prey, the duo fled.
The two young men were later arrested by police.
Investigations are ongoing.
GMB Concern About Investing in the McVitie Factory in Glasgow
A union would like to make sure of the long-term commitment to production at its Glasgow-based plant with managers of the biscuit manufacturer McVitie.
GMB Scotland said the company had remained silent about the future of investments at the Tollcross site.
More than 500 employees work at the plant, which produces Hobnobs and Rich Tea biscuits among other products.
McVities owner Pladis said he had invested more than 5 million pounds in recent years.
He added that employees and union representatives "would be the first to know" if their activities changed.
In a letter to the company's branch manager, GMB called on Scotland McVitie's "concern that funding for modernisation of production lines would not be pushed forward."
GMB senior organizer Drew Duffy said: "The fact that we can't gain clarity on the business needs and investment plans for Tollcross's future from the general site manager deeply affects our members.
Generations of families have worked here and the workers are strongly focused on the local community and the greater Glasgow area, an important pillar of decent employment in an area with high unemployment and social disadvantage.
Therefore, from the perspective of jobs, wages and prosperity, and against the backdrop of Brexit, the rising cost of living and the chronic decline in production, the eastern end of Glasgow urgently needs a thriving production presence at McVitie."
A spokeswoman for Pladis said: "The store manager at Tollcross only received a letter from GMB Scotland yesterday, and the company will react directly to it as we appreciate the continued commitment among staff and union representatives.
We had a regular meeting two months ago with staff and GMB representatives at Tollcross, during which some questions were discussed openly.
We discussed with the employees that, like all companies operating in an extremely competitive and insecure economic climate, we are continuously reviewing our businesses - and this applies to all our businesses and locations - in order to remain competitive.
She added: "Over the past few years, we have invested more than 5 million pounds in our Tollcross site, particularly in production lines and operational improvements.
We fully recognize Tollcross’ heritage as a manufacturing facility. Should our operations change at the site, the employees and their union representatives will be the first to know."
Did PSG warn Barcelona about Neymar three years ago?
La Liga's side may regret their interest in Marco Verratti
Paris Saint-Germain won't slacken in her pursuit of Neymar after handing over an offer of £189 million to trigger his release clause, but did they warn Barcelona three years again about the world record robbery?
The sensational approach has fuelled the transfer window in the summer, as the Ligue 1 club is ready to break the world record by more than double the previous mark, with Paul Pogba returning to Manchester United last season for 89 million pounds.
Uponching, Neymar becomes the world's most eco-paying player and the world's highest-paid professional athlete. PSG offers wages of more than 500,000 GBP per week.
Barcelona are furious about the way Neymar has forced his way out, although a source near the Catalan board insists that the club has always done its best to take care of him since his arrival from Santos in 2013.
But was the football world warned three years ago by the PSG president about this prospect?
When Manchester City expressed interest in signing Blaise Matuidi in 2014, Nasser Al-Khelaifi had a strict warning for any club looking to have one of PSG's best players signed.
"I'm not worried because they know that if they're touching one of our players, then we're going to be in opposition to them, we're going to contact their players," Al-Khelaifi said.
"We respect all clubs.
But if they try to have one of our players signed with them, it will have consequences.”
Barcelona appear to have crossed that line, with the Spanish club expressing interest in PSG midfielder Marco Verratti earlier this summer.
PSG made it clear that they would not allow the International from Italy to leave under any circumstances, and Barcelona could feel the consequences of this interest by losing one of their price offers for Neymar.
Scooter overthrew and damaged
In the night from Friday to Saturday, an unknown perpetrator deliberately overturned a scooter parked in Königsfelder Straße in front of estate number 30 and thus caused property damage of over 200 euros on the covering of the vehicle.
Schramberg police, phone 07422/ 27 01-0, are asking for clues.
To an accident on the B 215 between Stolzenau and Leese, the police are looking for witnesses.
Because a car approached him in his lane, the driver of a VW Touran had to dodge and touched the guardrail.
On Thursday evening, the 34-year-old from Rehburg-Loccum with his VW around 19.55 o'clock on the B 215 from Stolzenau in the direction of Leese.
In the area of the bypass of Stolzenau, a car approached him in his lane, so that he had to swerve to the right.
In doing so, the Touran driver touched the guardrail.
Property damage was caused to the car.
The driver of the oncoming passenger car, a Mercedes Benz, had overtaken a truck and continued its journey without worrying about the road accident.
According to the accident victim, it may well be that the Mercedes driver had not even noticed the accident.
Police are now asking witnesses to the incident as well as the driver of the polluting vehicle to get in touch with the commissioner's office in Stolzenau on tel. 05761 / 92060.
Interview: "Domspatzen-Report exonerates Pope-brother Georg Ratzinger"
The Regensburg Domspatzen report on abuse cases exonerates Pope brother Georg Ratzinger.
That's what author and historian Michael Hesemann means.
The final report on the abuse scandal at the Regensburger Domspatzen continues to provide for discussions.
Christian Weisnder, spokesperson of the "Wir sind Kirche" reform movement, declared after publication to the German Press Agency that he expected an apology from the former Regensburg bishop and today's cardinal Gerhard Ludwig Müller as well as the cathedral chapel master Georg Ratzinger, the 93-year-old brother of pope emeritus Benedict XVI.
It would serve the reputation of the Catholic Church very much if Müller and Ratzinger expressed their deep regret about their own omissions or their then-false assessment of events.
The Vatican newspaper "Osservatore Romano", on the other hand, criticized that the reporting of violence at the Domspatzen was measured with "twice standards".
With regard to the attacks in Regensburg, no one doubted that they were "disgraceful and shameful deeds" that had to be punished and prevented in the future, but the "degree of medial manipulation," according to the author Lucetta Scaraffia, a historian and a member of the national Italian bioethics committee.
"The habit of describing the Catholic Church as the source of all evils is already part of the everyday experience and prepares public opinion to consider this normal," Scaraffia writes.
Princess Gloria von Thurn and Taxis also said in an interview with Bayerischer Rundfunk: "You like to go to the church and that's a found food".
In addition, she pointed out that in her youth there had been "beats a normal educational means" "to cope with cheeky children, as I was one."
She would find it unfair to apply today's standards to previous decades, because the world has changed.
In connection with the reports about the cases of abuse, pope-brother Georg Ratzinger often also appeared.
Quite wrong, says author and historian Michael Hesemann, which wrote Ratzinger's "My Brother of the Pope" (translated into 12 languages).
"I have to protect Georg Ratzinger," says Hesemann, "because all this had nothing to do with him," he evaluates the results of the final report.
What exactly is stated in the 440-page report submitted by Ulrich Weber, who was commissioned by the diocese of Regensburg with enlightenment?
A total of 547 Domspatzen in Regensburg were sexually abused between 1945 and the early 1990s "with high plausibility" of assault.
The final report reads about Georg Ratzinger, who worked in Regensburg from 1964 to 1994: "In summary, it can therefore be stated that in many cases forms of physical and psychological violence have been used by Domkapellmeister R.
Especially with high emotionality, the punishment for the students could also be more violent.
Nevertheless, in contrast to numerous other defendants, it is noticeable that many victims appreciated the general humanity of R. and therefore in numerous cases, despite violence, even associate positive memories with him."
In an interview, Hesemann explains why the final report on the abuse scandal at the Regensburg Domspatzen relieves Georg Ratzinger in his opinion.
Mr. Hesemann, the 440-page report on the "incidentions of the exercise of violence on protective orders at the Regensburg Domspatzen" gives a contradictory picture of Domkapellmeister Georg Ratzinger.
On the one hand, many students stand out his "humanity."
On the other hand, he also appears as a cholerist and perfectionist, who often became palpable when a Domspatz did not perform musically.
As his biographer, how do you evaluate this final report?
Michael Hesemann: If you make the effort and read the entire 440-page report from front to back, you have to ask yourself why the name Georg Ratzinger occurs in it at all.
Just eight pages are dedicated to him and he is clearly acquitted of any suspicion of accomplicity or co-scrutiny in the horrific cases of sexual abuse.
The excesses of violence at the pre-school also occurred outside its sphere of action.
At best, you can accuse him of learning about it too late or not taking initial hints seriously enough.
The Domspatzengymnasium, on which he worked, was perceived by virtually all respondents as - so literally - "redemption", "heaven" and "better world".
Thus, the vast majority of his pupils Georg Ratzinger also give an excellent testimony, describing him as "warm-hearted", "popular", "sincerely", "competent" and "comprehensible", as an extremely good-natured teacher who regularly supplied the boys with cakes, biscuits and candies and was "always surrounded by grapes of children".
Only a minority complains about his perfectionism and his high emotionality, which sometimes led to outbursts of anger.
The fact that he not only slammed with chairs and threw the keychain to the floor, but also handed out slaps and pulled too cheeky boys by the hair or ears, he himself admitted years ago, deeply regretted and apologized for it.
However, it must be said that such educational methods were widespread in the time in question, the 1960s and 1970s, with parents as well as with teachers.
Do you still know methods of chastisement like this?
Of course, I'm thinking each of us who was born before 1968.
I, too, still experienced this in a secular school, a state high school.
Compared to what this report is actually about, namely the horrific sexual abuse and the excesses of violence at pre-school, these are, of course.
One wonders whether it is not more to do with his notoriety and his role as the brother of Benedict XVI that many mediaries just threw themselves at him.
On the Bavarian pope, many who have never understood him are still eagerly working.
But the truth is that the report exonerates Georg Ratzinger in every way!
In connection with the report, a lot has been reported in the media about the topic of abuse at the Regensburger Domspatzen.
Is Georg Ratzinger also accused of any involvement or co-acquaintance with regard to sexual abuse in the final report?
Michael Hesemann: Clearly no.
None of the 67 victims of sexual assault claims to have confided in the cathedral chapel master.
Not even the parents were inaugurated.
"I never dared to talk to my father or my mother about it," said one sufferer on behalf of the others, "It was all very embarrassing to me."
The real problem in enlightening sexual abuse was and still is that most of the victims are silent out of shame.
If they didn't even confide parents and siblings, they certainly didn't trust a choirmaster who would have been a colleague of the perpetrators professionally.
How do Domspatzen students rate Ratzinger’s role with regard to the abuse cases?
Michael Hesemann: An interviewed student put it this way: "With regard to sexual abuse ... I do not see ... a case in which he (Georg Ratzinger) of all people would have had timely findings and could have stopped the respective perpetrator.
I think it ... is also really absurd to assume that of all things ... boys have opened more urgently or clearly or rather than towards their parents, boarding school managers, trust teachers, etc.
That you're talking to him about sex ... - unthinkable."
Thus, the report comes to the very clear conclusion: "It could not be confirmed in the context of the investigation that pupils and/or their environment were entrusted to him in advance."
As far as sexual abuse is concerned, Georg Ratzinger must therefore be acquitted of any opinion.
So he cannot be accused of failing to prevent him or have brought him to report.
Therefore, there is no reason to link his good name to these obnoxious crimes, as unfortunately happened in some cases.
In the media, a lot of "violence excesses" could be read at the Regensburg Domspatzen.
Former cathedral chapel master Georg Ratzinger is accused in the report of "looking away."
Does he have to live with this accusation?
Michael Hesemann: That’s the big problem, that everything was mixed and mixed up in the reporting to a huge melange of sexual abuse and excesses of violence and somewhere, in the middle of it, Georg Ratzinger.
But that, in turn, is an abuse of the report.
Because it is neatly separated: between the preschool and the gymnasium of the Domspatzen, between sexual abuse and excesses of violence and, let's say, other accusations.
In addition, the report deals with incidents from the period since 1945, i.e. also from four decades in which Georg Ratzinger was not choir director of the Domspatzen.
The whole terrible beating pedagogy, the cases in which boys were downright harassed and maltreated by sadistic teachers and suffered horribly, all occurred at the preschool in Etterzhausen and Pielenhofen, where really scandalous conditions must have prevailed.
But Domkapellmeister Georg Ratzinger had nothing to do with them, because he didn't see the boys until they were already at the Domspatzengymnasium.
On which, as the vast majority of witnesses emphasize, the topic of "pre-school" was taboo.
One was only happy to have escaped her and enjoyed the so much better treatment and for many even "paradisial" conditions.
What was different at Domspatzengymnasium?
Michael Hesemann: There was already discipline, but there was no sadistic violence.
And this also applies to Georg Ratzinger, in which the report concludes that "many victims appreciated the general humanity of R. and therefore in numerous cases, in spite of violence even connect positive memories with him".
At most, there remains the accusation of having looked away.
However, despite all its efforts, the report was only able to define two periods during which he was told about the conditions at pre-school.
For the first time in 1970/71, when he began to establish himself and from the events at a school where he just did not work and were responsible (so the witness) did not want to hear, and then again around 1993.
Did Georg Ratzinger consistently close his ears before the conditions?
Michael Hesemann: No, because as early as 1989 he wrote a letter to the director of the Domspatzengymnasium, which the report even reproduces.
In it, he points out "that the beating penalty continues to be practiced in pre-school".
Even in view of the danger of negative press releases, he recommended urgent intervention.
This was, of course, at a time when there was legal clarity, when beating sentences were also already prohibited in Bavaria.
Before that, they happened in a legal grey area, as each of us probably remembers.
It was also difficult for him to estimate where those affected perhaps exaggerated or dramatized and where the permissible limit had really been crossed.
But you can't say he'd always looked away.
That's just not true.
It wasn't until 1980 that the beating penalty at schools was officially abolished in Bavaria, too.
Princess Gloria von Thurn and Taxis claimed that it was unfair to apply today’s educational standards to the time.
How do you see it?
Michael Hesemann: We historians are talking about the "case-knit of anachronism", i.e. the danger of assessing the past according to today's moral standards.
That's where the princess is right.
But, of course, this does not apply to sexual abuse, which at all times was abhorrent and also not for what was committed in preschool due to adversities and violence against innocent children's souls, this goes far beyond what was usual at that time.
It's all terrible and I'm glad it's finally being refurbished.
But at the same time I have to protect Georg Ratzinger, because all this had nothing to do with him.
His means of disciplining and punishing, i.e
When corporal punishment was then banned, he also strictly adhered to it.
What image of his time in Regensburg did Georg Ratzinger reproduce in the conversations with you for the book "My brother the pope"?
Did the topic of beating punishment also come up?
Michael Hesemann: Well, the book is about the life of his brother Joseph, i.e
Nevertheless, I do not hide the fact that he led a strict regiment there.
But even this has to be said once: Without this perfectionism, without the demand of unconditional discipline, it would hardly have been possible for him to form a world-ranked institution out ofrom the hitherto rather nationally known Regensburg Domspatzen, true cultural ambassadors of Europe and his musical tradition, who went on tour twice in the USA (1983 and 1987) and Japan (1988 and 1991).
With biscuits and candies alone, you don’t turn unruly boys into great singers and face every success, regardless of field, discipline, passion and self-overcome.
As what kind of person have you experienced Georg Ratzinger during the conversations about her book?
Michael Hesemann: By working on the book and beyond, I really got to know Georg Ratzinger quite well and experienced him again and again as a wonderful, humorous, fundamentally kind, deeply believing and good-hearted person, of whom one cannot imagine that he could do even a fly something.
I regularly get involved, as many former cathedral sparrows still today, 23 years after his emeritusion, keep regular contact with him, visit him, read aloud - he is almost blind - and treat him like a member of their family.
Those who do not forgive him until today that he was once strict to them are clearly in the minority.
Most dome sparrows are grateful to him today for enriching their lives and bringing them close to the world of music.
And that he has always been a good, paternal teacher - a strict father-minded, certainly, that may be - but he was also a lovable respondent, to whom she still looks up today and whom they almost worship.
And please, once again quite clearly: all that is accused of him are methods of education, which were on the agenda at that time.
We can be glad that we have long since overcome this time.
But it's highly unjust, a man just because he's prominent and has an even more prominent brother to make a media scapegoat.
There's the evil intention behind it, all too obvious.
Because the report that's just been released exonerates him.
Now the decency would command a 93-year-old man, who has acquired great merits and without whom outside of Bavaria and perhaps still Germany probably no one would know the Regensburg cathedral sparrows to finally leave in peace.
